{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015CocK6oPXcytLu2DXggJah","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Densha de Halo!\n2\t\n3\t*A playful riff on* **Densha de Go!** (\"Go by train!\"). Here it's \"Hello by train!\" (*harō*, ハロー, Japanese for \"hello\"), and the halo is the\n4\tring-shaped **Halo Line**, the loop railway around Akari City. The title was picked with a real RNG\n5\t(`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50)+1)\"` → **27**) from 50 riffs brainstormed silently, as TASK.md asks.\n6\t\n7\t- **Display title:** `Densha de Halo!` (title screen logo: `DENSHA DE HALO!`)\n8\t- **File-name tokens:** GameTitle = `DenshaDeHalo`, SOURCE = `DenshaDeGo`.\n9\t  - Dev folder: `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model-tag>-<timestamp>/`\n10\t  - Deliverable: `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model-tag>-<timestamp>.html` in the root folder (beside TASK.md), with `exit-interview.json` next to it.\n11\t  - Take `<model-tag>-<timestamp>` from **your** TASK.md. In the TASK.md this plan was written from, that's `claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174801`.\n12\t- **Plan file:** this file, `PLAN.md`. In the game's Source Information, \"Plan\" = `PLAN.md`.\n13\t- **Research behind this plan:**\n14\t  - Read in the engine handoff package (v0.85.2): `README.md`, `API.md`, `modules/road.js`, `kits/road.js`, the template game and its tests, the\n15\t    Shell, input glyphs, the chip-audio internals, the Dialog voice code, the Humanoid pose blend, `modules/mode7.js`'s rig billboards and\n16\t    `docs/ASSETS.md`.\n17\t  - Read the AI Hub `lib/hub` README, its three examples and the harness checks.\n18\t  - The source's mechanics come from general knowledge of *Densha de Go!*. No web research was done.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 0. How to use this plan\n23\t\n24\t1. Read TASK.md, then this plan once end to end. Section numbers (§) are used throughout.\n25\t2. Read these in the engine handoff package, in this order: `README.md`, then `API.md` (all of it), then `modules/road.js` (150 lines; the\n26\t   renderer you will fork), then `kits/road.js` (a whole pseudo-3D game: scenes, HUD, bot and proof), then `game/test.mjs` and `kits/test.mjs`\n27\t   (headless testing), then `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes and `E.shapeSprite`). Look names up in `API-INDEX.md` with grep; don't read it end to end.\n28\t3. From the AI Hub, read `lib/hub/README.md` (all of it) and `lib/hub/examples/ghost.html`, which is your template.\n29\t4. **Fetching the sources** (put each in its own new, empty folder inside your dev folder, e.g. `_src/engine`, `_src/hub`):\n30\t   - Engine: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` (~150 MB). Copy `dist/handoff/` into your dev folder as\n31\t     `engine/` (see §3.2).\n32\t   - Hub: the full repo is over 1 GB. Clone only `lib/hub`:\n33\t     `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git && cd ai-hub && git sparse-checkout set lib/hub`.\n34\t5. **Machine notes from planning.** `/tmp` is a small shared tmpfs and was **100% full**, which made `git clone` there fail. Run npm, Playwright and\n35\t   Chromium with `TMPDIR=<dev folder>/.tmp`. Other agents share the ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and headless Node tests. When you need a\n36\t   server (the hub harness), run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, check the page is yours, and later stop it by\n37\t   **its own PID** (never pkill, killall or fuser -k).\n38\t6. TASK.md rules that apply throughout: no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. All audio is made in code. Voices are gibberish. Tests go behind one\n39\t   command that prints only failures plus a one-line count. Don't use subagents or workflows to review or judge.\n40\t\n41\tEngine version at plan time: **my-3D2dge v0.85.2** (handoff package). Hub: **Session Protocol 1, client `session.js` 1.4.0**.\n42\t\n43\t---\n44\t\n45\t## 1. The game on one page\n46\t\n47\t**Pitch.** You're the rookie driver on the Halo Line, a 20.6 km loop railway around Akari City. In one day of service, from the depot at dawn\n48\tto the last train at midnight, you drive five shifts round the ring. Chief Instructor **Tetsuo Hoshino** rides with you. He has driven this ring for\n49\t38 years and was never once late, and today is his last day. Every stop has to land on the mark, every arrival on the second, and every signal gets\n50\tpointed at and called out. Close the ring and earn your halo.\n51\t\n52\t**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** *Read the line ahead, notch power and brake to keep the timetable, and stop\n53\teach train exactly on the mark: on time, smoothly and by the book. Then do it again at the next station.*\n54\t\n55\t**Kept from the source** (its world, mechanics and tone):\n56\t- the first-person cab view;\n57\t- a notched master controller;\n58\t- braking to a precise stop marker;\n59\t- a timetable kept to the second, with a time-based life (**Halo Time**, our version of the source's remaining-time system);\n60\t- block signals and ATS;\n61\t- horn boards;\n62\t- station departure melodies and polite announcements;\n63\t- times of day and weather;\n64\t- trains with different handling;\n65\t- terse arcade feedback (\"GREAT!\", \"OVERRUN!\").\n66\t\n67\t**Our own additions:**\n68\t- the loop line and the halo theme (route map, life gauge, PERFECT HALO judgment, mascot);\n69\t- point-and-call (*shisa kanko*) as a timing layer;\n70\t- the craft of a smooth stop (ease the brake off just before you stop);\n71\t- passengers and a comfort rating;\n72\t- a side-view **Stop Cam** replay of every stop;\n73\t- the four-track **Parallel Run** race for 2–4 players;\n74\t- a short, warm story with recurring characters.\n75\t\n76\t**Genre:** train driving simulation with arcade scoring. Its AI Hub genre is **Simulation**.\n77\t**Perspective:** the main view is the **first-person cab**, drawn in pseudo-3D. Supporting views:\n78\t- exterior chase, platform and trackside cameras (attract mode, the View button, the ending);\n79\t- a side-view Stop Cam inset after each stop;\n80\t- a top-down ring map for stage select.\n81\t\n82\t**Modes:**\n83\t- **Career:** S0 tutorial, then five service stages S1–S5 with story; stages unlock in order.\n84\t- **Free Run:** any unlocked stage, with time of day, weather, train and difficulty to choose.\n85\t- **Duel:** local 2-player split screen on the Parallel Run.\n86\t- **AI Hub session:** 2–4 players online in ghost mode, on the Parallel Run.\n87\t- **Attract mode:** an AI demo after 10 s idle.\n88\t- **Driver's Log:** records and postcards.\n89\t- **How to Drive** (help), **Settings**, **About & Credits**.\n90\t- **Dev Mode:** opened from the pause menu.\n91\t\n92\t**Length:** S0 takes about 3 min, S1–S4 about 4–6 min each, S5 about 7 min. A full career is about 30–35 min; a Parallel Run about 4 min.\n93\t\n94\t**What the player can do:**\n95\t- move one lever through **EB, B8…B1, N, P1…P5** (the train type decides how many notches);\n96\t- snap the lever to N, or pull the emergency brake;\n97\t- sound the horn;\n98\t- press ATS Confirm;\n99\t- point and call;\n100\t- change camera and look around a little;\n101\t- pause, and open Dev Mode (outside sessions).\n102\t\n103\t**What the player cannot do** (say so in S0 and in How to Drive):\n104\t- steer (the track does that);\n105\t- open or close doors (the conductor does);\n106\t- reverse (a big overrun is backed up for you, at a cost);\n107\t- move with the doors open;\n108\t- choose a route.\n109\t\n110\t---\n111\t\n112\t## 2. Controls (final mapping)\n113\t\n114\t| Action (id) | DualSense (Xbox) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n115\t|---|---|---|---|\n116\t| Lever toward power (`power`) | **R2** (RT), D-pad ↑, left stick ↑ | **W**, ↑ | wheel up; drag the lever handle forward |\n117\t| Lever toward brake (`brake`) | **L2** (LT), D-pad ↓, left stick ↓ | **S**, ↓ | wheel down; drag the handle back |\n118\t| Neutral, snap to N (`neutral`) | **R1** (RB) | **N** | click \"N\" on the notch strip |\n119\t| Emergency brake (`eb`) | **L1** (LB) | **Backspace**, X | click the red EB button |\n120\t| Point and call (`call`) | **✕** (A) | **E** | **left click** the target in the view (or anywhere in the view while a call is open) |\n121\t| Horn, hold (`horn`) | **□** (X) | **Space** | hold the horn button on the dash |\n122\t| ATS confirm (`ats`) | **△** (Y) | **Q** | click the ATS button |\n123\t| Change view (`view`) | **○** (B) | **C** | click the camera icon |\n124\t| Look around (`look`) | right stick | — | right-drag (springs back when released) |\n125\t| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc, P | the corner menu button |\n126\t| Menus | D-pad/stick, ✕ confirm, ○ back | arrows, Enter, Esc | hover moves the focus, click picks |\n127\t\n128\t- Holding a lever button repeats: one notch, then a repeat every 0.18 s after 0.25 s (`input.repeat(a, .25, .18)`). Each mouse-wheel notch moves one\n129\t  notch, with an 80 ms debounce.\n130\t- The keyboard follows PC train-sim habits: Space is the horn, Q acknowledges (the vigilance-key habit), Backspace is the emergency brake, W/S\n131\t  move the throttle.\n132\t- The **Lever direction** setting (\"Up = Power\" by default, or \"Up = Brake\" as on Japanese one-handle cabs) flips only the D-pad, stick and W/S/↑/↓.\n133\t  R2/L2 and the mouse wheel stay power/brake.\n134\t- Every gameplay action can be rebound through the engine's Controls screen. Name the actions with\n135\t  `menu.controls: { power: 'Lever: power', brake: 'Lever: brake', ... }` and hide the engine's unused defaults (`attack: false`, and so on).\n136\t- The `call` binding `Mouse0` only counts inside the forward view. Over dashboard widgets, call `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')`, so a click on a cab\n137\t  button never also counts as a call.\n138\t- **Duel keyboard halves** (from the engine's `halfKeys`, with actions ordered `call, horn, ats, eb, neutral, view`):\n139\t  - P1: W/S lever, F call, G horn, H ATS, R EB, T neutral, Y view.\n140\t  - P2: ↑/↓ lever, `.` call, `/` horn, `,` ATS, L EB, `;` neutral, `'` view.\n141\t  - Show these on the Duel lobby screen.\n142\t\n143\t---\n144\t\n145\t## 3. Architecture\n146\t\n147\t### 3.1 Stack and why\n148\t\n149\t- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2, full engine** (`my-3d2dge.js` from the handoff package). It includes every module, the HTML Shell (pause, settings, controls),\n150\t  Menus, Dialog with portraits, Humanoid and Blob rigs, the chip synth, input with glyph labels and rumble, split screen, boot steps, store and\n151\t  saves, and the headless `game.advance` / `game.debug` testing.\n152\t  - Use the 2D engine only. The three.js 3D engine is not in the handoff package, it runs poorly headless, and its look isn't the engine's\n153\t    native pixel style.\n154\t- **New module `rail.js`**, our pseudo-3D railway renderer. It is a fork of `modules/road.js` (OutRun-style segments) extended for tracks, platforms,\n155\t  trains and rear-facing cameras (§4.1). This is the heart of the game's look.\n156\t- **AI Hub client `session.js` 1.4.0**, pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`.\n157\t- **No other libraries and no CDN.** The single HTML inlines everything, so it runs offline from `file://`.\n158\t- **Tooling:** Node 18+; Playwright 1.63.0 (as pinned in the handoff `package.json`; reuse an installed Chromium if one is there); python3\n159\t  `http.server` for the hub harness only.\n160\t- **Resolution:** `res: 'ps1', expand: true` gives 320×240 as a minimum and about 427×240 on 16:9. Anchor all HUD to `r.W`/`r.H`.\n161\t\n162\t### 3.2 Dev folder layout\n163\t\n164\t```\n165\tDenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model>-<ts>/\n166\t  engine/                  copy of dist/handoff (my-3d2dge.js patched per §3.8, tools/, docs/, package.json, kits/ for reference)\n167\t  engine/modules/rail.js   NEW: the rail renderer (§4.1), loaded after my-3d2dge.js\n168\t  vendor/aihub/            session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (harness files are for tests only, never bundled)\n169\t  src/                     the game, in load order (each file one IIFE writing only into the DH namespace):\n170\t    00-core.js             DH namespace, E, the E.Game, constants, units, DH.rng, settings definitions, input map (§2)\n171\t    01-tuning.js           DH.TUNING defaults + difficulty tables (every dev slider reads/writes here, §4.21)\n172\t    05-glyphs.js           prompt badges (§4.15)\n173\t    10-art-*.js            drawables: art-train.js, art-cab.js, art-signals.js, art-station.js, art-town.js, art-landmarks.js, art-people.js, art-ui.js\n174\t    20-routes.js           route scripts: halo loop + depot (§5.1), zone scenery sets\n175\t    21-route-compiler.js   script → segments/objects/strips/things (§4.2)\n176\t    22-stages.js           stage definitions S0–S5 + PR (§5.3), story lines, postcards\n177\t    30-train.js            dynamics + lever (§4.3)\n178\t    31-signals.js          blocks, signals, ATS, other trains (§4.4)\n179\t    32-stations.js         approach, stop judgment, dwell/departure (§4.6)\n180\t    33-rules.js            speed limits, calls, horn, Halo Time, score, comfort (§4.5, 4.7, 4.9, 4.10)\n181\t    34-events.js           crossings, workers, obstruction, door rush, rival, oncoming, fog, glare, tips (§4.11)\n182\t    35-autopilot.js        AI driver (§4.19)\n183\t    40-sound.js            continuous soundscape + sfx + melodies + music (§4.16)\n184\t    41-babble.js           gibberish voices (§4.17)\n185\t    50-cab.js, 51-hud.js   cab overlay, HUD, stop gauge, stop cam (§4.14)\n186\t    52-story.js            briefings, radio chatter, subtitles, tips (§4.18)\n187\t    55-ui-*.js             title, menus, halo map, free run, briefing, results, game over, ending, log, help, about (§4.26)\n188\t    60-drive.js            the drive scene (§3.4)\n189\t    61-attract.js          attract mode + camera director (§4.20, §4.13)\n190\t    62-dev.js              dev mode + debug overlay (§4.21)\n191\t    63-hub.js              AI Hub session flow + card + ghosts (§4.23)\n192\t    64-duel.js             local split-screen duel (§4.24)\n193\t    99-start.js            boot steps, game.start, deep links (#gallery, #stage=S3, #attract, #dev ...)\n194\t  index.html               dev page: session.js first, then engine, rail.js, src/*.js (works from file://)\n195\t  test/run.mjs             THE one test command (§8)\n196\t  test/lib.mjs, test/*.test.mjs, test/browser.mjs, test/hub-harness.mjs\n197\t  tools/bundle.mjs         builds ../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model>-<ts>.html (§9)\n198\t  tools/calibrate.mjs      runs the autopilot headless over every stage and prints par times (§4.8)\n199\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md        every engine edit, one line of why each (feeds Engine Feedback)\n200\t  shots/                   screenshots for look review (§8.5)\n201\t```\n202\t\n203\t**Namespace rule.** Every src file starts with `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, DH = (window.DH = window.DH || {}); ... })();`.\n204\tThe only globals are `My3D2dge`, `AIHub` and `DH`. `tools/bundle.mjs` concatenates the files in the same order as `index.html`.\n205\t\n206\t### 3.3 Scenes and flow\n207\t\n208\t```\n209\tboot (engine boot steps) ──► AIHub.active? ──yes──► hubCard ─► hubRun ─► hubEnd (stays; the hub removes the game)\n210\t                               │no\n211\t                               ▼\n212\t        ┌──────────────► title ◄──── any input ──── attract (10 s idle on title or any menu screen)\n213\t        │                 │\n214\t        │   ┌─────────────┼───────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────┬───────────┐\n215\t        │   ▼             ▼               ▼              ▼             ▼           ▼\n216\t        │ haloMap      freeRun         duelLobby      driversLog     help        about\n217\t        │ (career)     (setup)            │\n218\t        │   ▼             │               ▼\n219\t        │ briefing ───────┴──► drive ──► results ──► next briefing | haloMap      duel ─► duelResults\n220\t        │                       │  ▲         └─(S5)─► ending (credits) ─► title\n221\t        │                       │  └── pause (Shell): Resume · Retry stage · Retry from last station · Dev Mode · Settings · Controls · How to Drive · Quit\n222\t        │                       └──► gameOver ──► retry | continue from last station | haloMap\n223\t        └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n224\t```\n225\t\n226\t- `drive`, `duel` and `hubRun` are the `pausable` gameplay scenes. Duel's pause menu has no Dev Mode; the hub's has only Resume, Settings and\n227\t  Controls (§4.23).\n228\t- Use `game.go(name, data, { fade: 'wipe' })` for scene changes. Use `'iris'` into the Stop Cam and the ending.\n229\t- Deep links for testing: `#gallery`, `#stage=S2&s=5400&clock=10:17:30`, `#attract`, `#halomap`, `#results=S1`, `#duel`, `#about`.\n230\t\n231\t### 3.4 Per-step order in `drive` (fixed 1/120 s steps)\n232\t\n233\t1. Input becomes lever and button commands (or the autopilot writes the same commands, §4.19).\n234\t2. Train dynamics (§4.3).\n235\t3. Other trains move; block occupancy updates; signal aspects update (§4.4).\n236\t4. ATS and speed checks (§4.4, §4.5).\n237\t5. Events, crossings and fog (§4.11).\n238\t6. Station logic: approach, stop, judgment, dwell, departure (§4.6).\n239\t7. Call and horn windows (§4.7).\n240\t8. Halo Time, score, comfort (§4.9, §4.10).\n241\t9. Story triggers and tips (§4.18), then sound parameters (§4.16).\n242\t10. Camera (§4.13), then HUD state (§4.14).\n243\t11. Network or duel sync (§4.23, §4.24).\n244\t\n245\t**Draw order** (all inside `r.overlay`, screen pixels):\n246\t1. `rail.draw` (sky, then the world far to near);\n247\t2. weather overlays;\n248\t3. the cab;\n249\t4. the HUD;\n250\t5. call brackets and the glove;\n251\t6. subtitles and dialog;\n252\t7. toasts and judgments;\n253\t8. the Stop Cam inset;\n254\t9. the debug overlay.\n255\t\n256\t`draw` only reads state; it never changes it.\n257\t\n258\t### 3.5 Units and coordinates\n259\t\n260\t- `s`: metres along a route from its origin. On the halo route, s = 0 is Akari Central's 6-car stop marker, and the loop wraps at 20,600 m.\n261\t- `x`: metres across; **+ is to the right** in the direction of travel. Our track is at x = 0. Trains keep left as in Japan, so the counter-clockwise\n262\t  track is at x = +3.8.\n263\t- `y`: metres up. A segment's track height is absolute.\n264\t- Speeds: m/s inside the code, km/h in the UI. Accelerations are in **km/h/s** in all tables (1 km/h/s = 0.2778 m/s²).\n265\t- A train's reference point is its **front**. Cars are 20 m long, so 6 cars = 120 m and 10 cars = 200 m. The cab camera sits 1.5 m behind the front,\n266\t  2.9 m above the rail.\n267\t- Clock: seconds since midnight (06:40:00 = 24000). Game time drives it (1 s = 1 s). It pauses with the game.\n268\t- Characters: **1 m ≈ 15 engine units** (a 26-unit hero ≈ 1.7 m). A rig drawn at k pixels per metre uses a View scaled by k/15 (§4.1).\n269\t\n270\t### 3.6 Determinism and randomness\n271\t\n272\t- All world building and cosmetic randomness uses `DH.rng(label)`:\n273\t  - in a hub session, `AIHub.rng(label)`;\n274\t  - otherwise `E.rng(hash(label + ':' + stageSeed))`.\n275\t- Never use `Math.random`, `Date.now` or `performance.now` in game logic.\n276\t- The engine's `rand` and particles use `Math.random`. That's fine for frame-to-frame cosmetics, but no game state may depend on it.\n277\t- The hub harness only checks that `AIHub.seeded` is true on every copy. We build every world from our own streams, so all copies match anyway.\n278\t- `DH.proof(stageId, seconds)` runs a scripted autopilot drive and returns an FNV hash of the state samples. The test runs it twice and expects\n279\t  the same hash.\n280\t\n281\t### 3.7 `game.debug` handle (tests, tools/check.mjs, tools/replay.mjs)\n282\t\n283\t`snapshot()` returns:\n284\t- `scene`, `stage`, `s`, `km`, `v` (km/h), `notch` ('B3'), `brakeEff`, `grace`, `score`, `clock` ('06:41:12');\n285\t- `nextStop` ({ id, dist }), `lastJudgment`, `signals` (the next 3: { id, dist, aspect }), `limit`;\n286\t- `events` (ids fired), `comfort`, `doors`, `pilot`, `ats` ({ ringing, confirmed }), `camera`;\n287\t- `attract` ({ active, vignette }), `hub` ({ active, phase, sent, progressCalls }), `players` (duel), `errors`.\n288\t\n289\tFunctions:\n290\t- `jump(stageId, { s, clock, v })`\n291\t- `auto(on, { skill, cheat })`\n292\t- `setTod(h)`, `setWeather(w)`\n293\t- `forceAspect(signalId, aspect)`, `trigger(eventId)`\n294\t- `finishStage(perfect)`, `setGrace(n)`\n295\t\n296\tAlso set `game.stateHash = () => [s, v, grace, score]`, which makes recorded sessions catch divergence (`?record=1`, `tools/replay.mjs`).\n297\t\n298\tIn the browser, also expose `window.__dh = { game, DH, snapshot: () => game.debug.snapshot() }` for the Playwright checks, as the kits do with\n299\t`window.__game`.\n300\t\n301\t### 3.8 Engine changes (keep minimal; log each in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`)\n302\t\n303\t1. **`engine/modules/rail.js` (new)**: the rail renderer (§4.1). It is a fork of road.js, because road.js can't do multiple tracks, raised platforms,\n304\t   long side objects (trains, buildings), rear cameras or square-pixel projection on wide screens.\n305\t2. **Dialog voice hook**: let `talk.voice` be a function `(ch, index, text, talk)`, called for each revealed character, so Babble can speak\n306\t   syllables (§4.17). Today `voice` is only an sfx name, blipped every second letter (`Dialog.update`, around line 8956).\n307\t3. **Settings screen filter**:\n308\t   - `ui.show('settings', { groups: ['Dev'], title: 'Dev Mode · Balance' })` shows only those groups.\n309\t   - The standard Settings screen hides the `Dev` group (`Shell._settingsRows`).\n310\t   - This gives live, controller-navigable sliders for free (§4.21).\n311\t4. **Glyphs**:\n312\t   - Make the standard `glyphs` setting default to `'playstation'` instead of `'auto'`; TASK.md wants PlayStation glyphs unless the player picks Xbox.\n313\t   - Add the 5×7 font glyphs `✕ ○ □ △`, so any `input.label()` text on canvas renders. The font currently has `×` but not these.\n314\t5. **ChipAudio accessors**: `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'master')` and `audio.noiseBuffer()`. The continuous train sound and Babble then route\n315\t   through the player's volume, mute and duck settings.\n316\t6. **Humanoid poses**:\n317\t   - `'bow'`: torso pitched forward about 40°, hands at the thighs, head down.\n318\t   - `'point'`: right arm straight forward at shoulder height, index finger out.\n319\t   - `'flag'`: right arm raised; the game draws a small flag at `rig.hand()`.\n320\t   - Add them next to `cheer`/`wave` in `poseW` and the solver. Check with `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n321\t7. **Hub button rule**: while `AIHub.active` and Create (`Pad8`) is held, ignore Options (`Pad9`) as pause (hub README, Rules).\n322\t\n323\tAfter patching, rerun the handoff's own `node game/test.mjs` and `node kits/test.mjs road` to check nothing else broke.\n324\t\n325\t---\n326\t\n327\t## 4. Systems\n328\t\n329\tEach subsection ends with **Tests**. All tests run under `node test/run.mjs` (§8).\n330\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"871d2b20-f742-4b08-b090-bf4615dfed27","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:00.851Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Densha de Halo!\n\n*A playful riff on* **Densha de Go!** (\"Go by train!\"). Here it's \"Hello by train!\" (*harō*, ハロー, Japanese for \"hello\"), and the halo is the\nring-shaped **Halo Line**, the loop railway around Akari City. The title was picked with a real RNG\n(`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50)+1)\"` → **27**) from 50 riffs brainstormed silently, as TASK.md asks.\n\n- **Display title:** `Densha de Halo!` (title screen logo: `DENSHA DE HALO!`)\n- **File-name tokens:** GameTitle = `DenshaDeHalo`, SOURCE = `DenshaDeGo`.\n  - Dev folder: `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model-tag>-<timestamp>/`\n  - Deliverable: `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model-tag>-<timestamp>.html` in the root folder (beside TASK.md), with `exit-interview.json` next to it.\n  - Take `<model-tag>-<timestamp>` from **your** TASK.md. In the TASK.md this plan was written from, that's `claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174801`.\n- **Plan file:** this file, `PLAN.md`. In the game's Source Information, \"Plan\" = `PLAN.md`.\n- **Research behind this plan:**\n  - Read in the engine handoff package (v0.85.2): `README.md`, `API.md`, `modules/road.js`, `kits/road.js`, the template game and its tests, the\n    Shell, input glyphs, the chip-audio internals, the Dialog voice code, the Humanoid pose blend, `modules/mode7.js`'s rig billboards and\n    `docs/ASSETS.md`.\n  - Read the AI Hub `lib/hub` README, its three examples and the harness checks.\n  - The source's mechanics come from general knowledge of *Densha de Go!*. No web research was done.\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n1. Read TASK.md, then this plan once end to end. Section numbers (§) are used throughout.\n2. Read these in the engine handoff package, in this order: `README.md`, then `API.md` (all of it), then `modules/road.js` (150 lines; the\n   renderer you will fork), then `kits/road.js` (a whole pseudo-3D game: scenes, HUD, bot and proof), then `game/test.mjs` and `kits/test.mjs`\n   (headless testing), then `docs/ASSETS.md` (shapes and `E.shapeSprite`). Look names up in `API-INDEX.md` with grep; don't read it end to end.\n3. From the AI Hub, read `lib/hub/README.md` (all of it) and `lib/hub/examples/ghost.html`, which is your template.\n4. **Fetching the sources** (put each in its own new, empty folder inside your dev folder, e.g. `_src/engine`, `_src/hub`):\n   - Engine: `git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git` (~150 MB). Copy `dist/handoff/` into your dev folder as\n     `engine/` (see §3.2).\n   - Hub: the full repo is over 1 GB. Clone only `lib/hub`:\n     `git clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub.git && cd ai-hub && git sparse-checkout set lib/hub`.\n5. **Machine notes from planning.** `/tmp` is a small shared tmpfs and was **100% full**, which made `git clone` there fail. Run npm, Playwright and\n   Chromium with `TMPDIR=<dev folder>/.tmp`. Other agents share the ports and processes. Prefer `file://` and headless Node tests. When you need a\n   server (the hub harness), run `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`, read the port it prints, check the page is yours, and later stop it by\n   **its own PID** (never pkill, killall or fuser -k).\n6. TASK.md rules that apply throughout: no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features. All audio is made in code. Voices are gibberish. Tests go behind one\n   command that prints only failures plus a one-line count. Don't use subagents or workflows to review or judge.\n\nEngine version at plan time: **my-3D2dge v0.85.2** (handoff package). Hub: **Session Protocol 1, client `session.js` 1.4.0**.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game on one page\n\n**Pitch.** You're the rookie driver on the Halo Line, a 20.6 km loop railway around Akari City. In one day of service, from the depot at dawn\nto the last train at midnight, you drive five shifts round the ring. Chief Instructor **Tetsuo Hoshino** rides with you. He has driven this ring for\n38 years and was never once late, and today is his last day. Every stop has to land on the mark, every arrival on the second, and every signal gets\npointed at and called out. Close the ring and earn your halo.\n\n**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** *Read the line ahead, notch power and brake to keep the timetable, and stop\neach train exactly on the mark: on time, smoothly and by the book. Then do it again at the next station.*\n\n**Kept from the source** (its world, mechanics and tone):\n- the first-person cab view;\n- a notched master controller;\n- braking to a precise stop marker;\n- a timetable kept to the second, with a time-based life (**Halo Time**, our version of the source's remaining-time system);\n- block signals and ATS;\n- horn boards;\n- station departure melodies and polite announcements;\n- times of day and weather;\n- trains with different handling;\n- terse arcade feedback (\"GREAT!\", \"OVERRUN!\").\n\n**Our own additions:**\n- the loop line and the halo theme (route map, life gauge, PERFECT HALO judgment, mascot);\n- point-and-call (*shisa kanko*) as a timing layer;\n- the craft of a smooth stop (ease the brake off just before you stop);\n- passengers and a comfort rating;\n- a side-view **Stop Cam** replay of every stop;\n- the four-track **Parallel Run** race for 2–4 players;\n- a short, warm story with recurring characters.\n\n**Genre:** train driving simulation with arcade scoring. Its AI Hub genre is **Simulation**.\n**Perspective:** the main view is the **first-person cab**, drawn in pseudo-3D. Supporting views:\n- exterior chase, platform and trackside cameras (attract mode, the View button, the ending);\n- a side-view Stop Cam inset after each stop;\n- a top-down ring map for stage select.\n\n**Modes:**\n- **Career:** S0 tutorial, then five service stages S1–S5 with story; stages unlock in order.\n- **Free Run:** any unlocked stage, with time of day, weather, train and difficulty to choose.\n- **Duel:** local 2-player split screen on the Parallel Run.\n- **AI Hub session:** 2–4 players online in ghost mode, on the Parallel Run.\n- **Attract mode:** an AI demo after 10 s idle.\n- **Driver's Log:** records and postcards.\n- **How to Drive** (help), **Settings**, **About & Credits**.\n- **Dev Mode:** opened from the pause menu.\n\n**Length:** S0 takes about 3 min, S1–S4 about 4–6 min each, S5 about 7 min. A full career is about 30–35 min; a Parallel Run about 4 min.\n\n**What the player can do:**\n- move one lever through **EB, B8…B1, N, P1…P5** (the train type decides how many notches);\n- snap the lever to N, or pull the emergency brake;\n- sound the horn;\n- press ATS Confirm;\n- point and call;\n- change camera and look around a little;\n- pause, and open Dev Mode (outside sessions).\n\n**What the player cannot do** (say so in S0 and in How to Drive):\n- steer (the track does that);\n- open or close doors (the conductor does);\n- reverse (a big overrun is backed up for you, at a cost);\n- move with the doors open;\n- choose a route.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controls (final mapping)\n\n| Action (id) | DualSense (Xbox) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lever toward power (`power`) | **R2** (RT), D-pad ↑, left stick ↑ | **W**, ↑ | wheel up; drag the lever handle forward |\n| Lever toward brake (`brake`) | **L2** (LT), D-pad ↓, left stick ↓ | **S**, ↓ | wheel down; drag the handle back |\n| Neutral, snap to N (`neutral`) | **R1** (RB) | **N** | click \"N\" on the notch strip |\n| Emergency brake (`eb`) | **L1** (LB) | **Backspace**, X | click the red EB button |\n| Point and call (`call`) | **✕** (A) | **E** | **left click** the target in the view (or anywhere in the view while a call is open) |\n| Horn, hold (`horn`) | **□** (X) | **Space** | hold the horn button on the dash |\n| ATS confirm (`ats`) | **△** (Y) | **Q** | click the ATS button |\n| Change view (`view`) | **○** (B) | **C** | click the camera icon |\n| Look around (`look`) | right stick | — | right-drag (springs back when released) |\n| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc, P | the corner menu button |\n| Menus | D-pad/stick, ✕ confirm, ○ back | arrows, Enter, Esc | hover moves the focus, click picks |\n\n- Holding a lever button repeats: one notch, then a repeat every 0.18 s after 0.25 s (`input.repeat(a, .25, .18)`). Each mouse-wheel notch moves one\n  notch, with an 80 ms debounce.\n- The keyboard follows PC train-sim habits: Space is the horn, Q acknowledges (the vigilance-key habit), Backspace is the emergency brake, W/S\n  move the throttle.\n- The **Lever direction** setting (\"Up = Power\" by default, or \"Up = Brake\" as on Japanese one-handle cabs) flips only the D-pad, stick and W/S/↑/↓.\n  R2/L2 and the mouse wheel stay power/brake.\n- Every gameplay action can be rebound through the engine's Controls screen. Name the actions with\n  `menu.controls: { power: 'Lever: power', brake: 'Lever: brake', ... }` and hide the engine's unused defaults (`attack: false`, and so on).\n- The `call` binding `Mouse0` only counts inside the forward view. Over dashboard widgets, call `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')`, so a click on a cab\n  button never also counts as a call.\n- **Duel keyboard halves** (from the engine's `halfKeys`, with actions ordered `call, horn, ats, eb, neutral, view`):\n  - P1: W/S lever, F call, G horn, H ATS, R EB, T neutral, Y view.\n  - P2: ↑/↓ lever, `.` call, `/` horn, `,` ATS, L EB, `;` neutral, `'` view.\n  - Show these on the Duel lobby screen.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack and why\n\n- **my-3D2dge v0.85.2, full engine** (`my-3d2dge.js` from the handoff package). It includes every module, the HTML Shell (pause, settings, controls),\n  Menus, Dialog with portraits, Humanoid and Blob rigs, the chip synth, input with glyph labels and rumble, split screen, boot steps, store and\n  saves, and the headless `game.advance` / `game.debug` testing.\n  - Use the 2D engine only. The three.js 3D engine is not in the handoff package, it runs poorly headless, and its look isn't the engine's\n    native pixel style.\n- **New module `rail.js`**, our pseudo-3D railway renderer. It is a fork of `modules/road.js` (OutRun-style segments) extended for tracks, platforms,\n  trains and rear-facing cameras (§4.1). This is the heart of the game's look.\n- **AI Hub client `session.js` 1.4.0**, pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`.\n- **No other libraries and no CDN.** The single HTML inlines everything, so it runs offline from `file://`.\n- **Tooling:** Node 18+; Playwright 1.63.0 (as pinned in the handoff `package.json`; reuse an installed Chromium if one is there); python3\n  `http.server` for the hub harness only.\n- **Resolution:** `res: 'ps1', expand: true` gives 320×240 as a minimum and about 427×240 on 16:9. Anchor all HUD to `r.W`/`r.H`.\n\n### 3.2 Dev folder layout\n\n```\nDenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model>-<ts>/\n  engine/                  copy of dist/handoff (my-3d2dge.js patched per §3.8, tools/, docs/, package.json, kits/ for reference)\n  engine/modules/rail.js   NEW: the rail renderer (§4.1), loaded after my-3d2dge.js\n  vendor/aihub/            session.js (unmodified), harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (harness files are for tests only, never bundled)\n  src/                     the game, in load order (each file one IIFE writing only into the DH namespace):\n    00-core.js             DH namespace, E, the E.Game, constants, units, DH.rng, settings definitions, input map (§2)\n    01-tuning.js           DH.TUNING defaults + difficulty tables (every dev slider reads/writes here, §4.21)\n    05-glyphs.js           prompt badges (§4.15)\n    10-art-*.js            drawables: art-train.js, art-cab.js, art-signals.js, art-station.js, art-town.js, art-landmarks.js, art-people.js, art-ui.js\n    20-routes.js           route scripts: halo loop + depot (§5.1), zone scenery sets\n    21-route-compiler.js   script → segments/objects/strips/things (§4.2)\n    22-stages.js           stage definitions S0–S5 + PR (§5.3), story lines, postcards\n    30-train.js            dynamics + lever (§4.3)\n    31-signals.js          blocks, signals, ATS, other trains (§4.4)\n    32-stations.js         approach, stop judgment, dwell/departure (§4.6)\n    33-rules.js            speed limits, calls, horn, Halo Time, score, comfort (§4.5, 4.7, 4.9, 4.10)\n    34-events.js           crossings, workers, obstruction, door rush, rival, oncoming, fog, glare, tips (§4.11)\n    35-autopilot.js        AI driver (§4.19)\n    40-sound.js            continuous soundscape + sfx + melodies + music (§4.16)\n    41-babble.js           gibberish voices (§4.17)\n    50-cab.js, 51-hud.js   cab overlay, HUD, stop gauge, stop cam (§4.14)\n    52-story.js            briefings, radio chatter, subtitles, tips (§4.18)\n    55-ui-*.js             title, menus, halo map, free run, briefing, results, game over, ending, log, help, about (§4.26)\n    60-drive.js            the drive scene (§3.4)\n    61-attract.js          attract mode + camera director (§4.20, §4.13)\n    62-dev.js              dev mode + debug overlay (§4.21)\n    63-hub.js              AI Hub session flow + card + ghosts (§4.23)\n    64-duel.js             local split-screen duel (§4.24)\n    99-start.js            boot steps, game.start, deep links (#gallery, #stage=S3, #attract, #dev ...)\n  index.html               dev page: session.js first, then engine, rail.js, src/*.js (works from file://)\n  test/run.mjs             THE one test command (§8)\n  test/lib.mjs, test/*.test.mjs, test/browser.mjs, test/hub-harness.mjs\n  tools/bundle.mjs         builds ../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model>-<ts>.html (§9)\n  tools/calibrate.mjs      runs the autopilot headless over every stage and prints par times (§4.8)\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md        every engine edit, one line of why each (feeds Engine Feedback)\n  shots/                   screenshots for look review (§8.5)\n```\n\n**Namespace rule.** Every src file starts with `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge, DH = (window.DH = window.DH || {}); ... })();`.\nThe only globals are `My3D2dge`, `AIHub` and `DH`. `tools/bundle.mjs` concatenates the files in the same order as `index.html`.\n\n### 3.3 Scenes and flow\n\n```\nboot (engine boot steps) ──► AIHub.active? ──yes──► hubCard ─► hubRun ─► hubEnd (stays; the hub removes the game)\n                               │no\n                               ▼\n        ┌──────────────► title ◄──── any input ──── attract (10 s idle on title or any menu screen)\n        │                 │\n        │   ┌─────────────┼───────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────┬───────────┐\n        │   ▼             ▼               ▼              ▼             ▼           ▼\n        │ haloMap      freeRun         duelLobby      driversLog     help        about\n        │ (career)     (setup)            │\n        │   ▼             │               ▼\n        │ briefing ───────┴──► drive ──► results ──► next briefing | haloMap      duel ─► duelResults\n        │                       │  ▲         └─(S5)─► ending (credits) ─► title\n        │                       │  └── pause (Shell): Resume · Retry stage · Retry from last station · Dev Mode · Settings · Controls · How to Drive · Quit\n        │                       └──► gameOver ──► retry | continue from last station | haloMap\n        └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n```\n\n- `drive`, `duel` and `hubRun` are the `pausable` gameplay scenes. Duel's pause menu has no Dev Mode; the hub's has only Resume, Settings and\n  Controls (§4.23).\n- Use `game.go(name, data, { fade: 'wipe' })` for scene changes. Use `'iris'` into the Stop Cam and the ending.\n- Deep links for testing: `#gallery`, `#stage=S2&s=5400&clock=10:17:30`, `#attract`, `#halomap`, `#results=S1`, `#duel`, `#about`.\n\n### 3.4 Per-step order in `drive` (fixed 1/120 s steps)\n\n1. Input becomes lever and button commands (or the autopilot writes the same commands, §4.19).\n2. Train dynamics (§4.3).\n3. Other trains move; block occupancy updates; signal aspects update (§4.4).\n4. ATS and speed checks (§4.4, §4.5).\n5. Events, crossings and fog (§4.11).\n6. Station logic: approach, stop, judgment, dwell, departure (§4.6).\n7. Call and horn windows (§4.7).\n8. Halo Time, score, comfort (§4.9, §4.10).\n9. Story triggers and tips (§4.18), then sound parameters (§4.16).\n10. Camera (§4.13), then HUD state (§4.14).\n11. Network or duel sync (§4.23, §4.24).\n\n**Draw order** (all inside `r.overlay`, screen pixels):\n1. `rail.draw` (sky, then the world far to near);\n2. weather overlays;\n3. the cab;\n4. the HUD;\n5. call brackets and the glove;\n6. subtitles and dialog;\n7. toasts and judgments;\n8. the Stop Cam inset;\n9. the debug overlay.\n\n`draw` only reads state; it never changes it.\n\n### 3.5 Units and coordinates\n\n- `s`: metres along a route from its origin. On the halo route, s = 0 is Akari Central's 6-car stop marker, and the loop wraps at 20,600 m.\n- `x`: metres across; **+ is to the right** in the direction of travel. Our track is at x = 0. Trains keep left as in Japan, so the counter-clockwise\n  track is at x = +3.8.\n- `y`: metres up. A segment's track height is absolute.\n- Speeds: m/s inside the code, km/h in the UI. Accelerations are in **km/h/s** in all tables (1 km/h/s = 0.2778 m/s²).\n- A train's reference point is its **front**. Cars are 20 m long, so 6 cars = 120 m and 10 cars = 200 m. The cab camera sits 1.5 m behind the front,\n  2.9 m above the rail.\n- Clock: seconds since midnight (06:40:00 = 24000). Game time drives it (1 s = 1 s). It pauses with the game.\n- Characters: **1 m ≈ 15 engine units** (a 26-unit hero ≈ 1.7 m). A rig drawn at k pixels per metre uses a View scaled by k/15 (§4.1).\n\n### 3.6 Determinism and randomness\n\n- All world building and cosmetic randomness uses `DH.rng(label)`:\n  - in a hub session, `AIHub.rng(label)`;\n  - otherwise `E.rng(hash(label + ':' + stageSeed))`.\n- Never use `Math.random`, `Date.now` or `performance.now` in game logic.\n- The engine's `rand` and particles use `Math.random`. That's fine for frame-to-frame cosmetics, but no game state may depend on it.\n- The hub harness only checks that `AIHub.seeded` is true on every copy. We build every world from our own streams, so all copies match anyway.\n- `DH.proof(stageId, seconds)` runs a scripted autopilot drive and returns an FNV hash of the state samples. The test runs it twice and expects\n  the same hash.\n\n### 3.7 `game.debug` handle (tests, tools/check.mjs, tools/replay.mjs)\n\n`snapshot()` returns:\n- `scene`, `stage`, `s`, `km`, `v` (km/h), `notch` ('B3'), `brakeEff`, `grace`, `score`, `clock` ('06:41:12');\n- `nextStop` ({ id, dist }), `lastJudgment`, `signals` (the next 3: { id, dist, aspect }), `limit`;\n- `events` (ids fired), `comfort`, `doors`, `pilot`, `ats` ({ ringing, confirmed }), `camera`;\n- `attract` ({ active, vignette }), `hub` ({ active, phase, sent, progressCalls }), `players` (duel), `errors`.\n\nFunctions:\n- `jump(stageId, { s, clock, v })`\n- `auto(on, { skill, cheat })`\n- `setTod(h)`, `setWeather(w)`\n- `forceAspect(signalId, aspect)`, `trigger(eventId)`\n- `finishStage(perfect)`, `setGrace(n)`\n\nAlso set `game.stateHash = () => [s, v, grace, score]`, which makes recorded sessions catch divergence (`?record=1`, `tools/replay.mjs`).\n\nIn the browser, also expose `window.__dh = { game, DH, snapshot: () => game.debug.snapshot() }` for the Playwright checks, as the kits do with\n`window.__game`.\n\n### 3.8 Engine changes (keep minimal; log each in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md`)\n\n1. **`engine/modules/rail.js` (new)**: the rail renderer (§4.1). It is a fork of road.js, because road.js can't do multiple tracks, raised platforms,\n   long side objects (trains, buildings), rear cameras or square-pixel projection on wide screens.\n2. **Dialog voice hook**: let `talk.voice` be a function `(ch, index, text, talk)`, called for each revealed character, so Babble can speak\n   syllables (§4.17). Today `voice` is only an sfx name, blipped every second letter (`Dialog.update`, around line 8956).\n3. **Settings screen filter**:\n   - `ui.show('settings', { groups: ['Dev'], title: 'Dev Mode · Balance' })` shows only those groups.\n   - The standard Settings screen hides the `Dev` group (`Shell._settingsRows`).\n   - This gives live, controller-navigable sliders for free (§4.21).\n4. **Glyphs**:\n   - Make the standard `glyphs` setting default to `'playstation'` instead of `'auto'`; TASK.md wants PlayStation glyphs unless the player picks Xbox.\n   - Add the 5×7 font glyphs `✕ ○ □ △`, so any `input.label()` text on canvas renders. The font currently has `×` but not these.\n5. **ChipAudio accessors**: `audio.bus('sfx' | 'music' | 'master')` and `audio.noiseBuffer()`. The continuous train sound and Babble then route\n   through the player's volume, mute and duck settings.\n6. **Humanoid poses**:\n   - `'bow'`: torso pitched forward about 40°, hands at the thighs, head down.\n   - `'point'`: right arm straight forward at shoulder height, index finger out.\n   - `'flag'`: right arm raised; the game draws a small flag at `rig.hand()`.\n   - Add them next to `cheer`/`wave` in `poseW` and the solver. Check with `tools/filmstrip.mjs`.\n7. **Hub button rule**: while `AIHub.active` and Create (`Pad8`) is held, ignore Options (`Pad9`) as pause (hub README, Rules).\n\nAfter patching, rerun the handoff's own `node game/test.mjs` and `node kits/test.mjs road` to check nothing else broke.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Systems\n\nEach subsection ends with **Tests**. All tests run under `node test/run.mjs` (§8).\n","numLines":330,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1952}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015CocK6oPXcytLu2DXggJah","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
