{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrYqbJT3VsZEaon73oY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PnRny64JzUQXqaWvLRQvAU","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","offset":331,"limit":300},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11159,"cache_read_input_tokens":69665,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11159},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"fe6bdc1b-a2b3-4d8f-9208-3d5f9fd4c4ee","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:29.970Z","request_id":"req_011CghrYqHSfvrcSFPQJwqnA","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PnRny64JzUQXqaWvLRQvAU":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","offset":331,"limit":300}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PnRny64JzUQXqaWvLRQvAU","type":"tool_result","content":"331\t### 4.1 Rail renderer (`DH.Rail`, module `rail.js`): the riskiest piece, so build it first\n332\t\n333\t**Model.** A route is a run of **5 m segments**. Each segment has:\n334\t- `i`, `s`;\n335\t- `curve`: the lateral bend added per segment, `= ±L²/R` m (+ bends right), eased over 20 m at each end;\n336\t- `y0`, `y1`;\n337\t- `zone`;\n338\t- `flags`: tunnel, bridge, crossing, viaduct, quad;\n339\t- `tracks`: `[{ id, x0, x1, electrified, kind }]`, with offsets at both segment ends so tracks can diverge;\n340\t- the lists of strips and things that touch it.\n341\t\n342\t**Camera.** `{ s, x, h, dir: 1 | -1, fov: 75, horizon: .40, lookX, lookY, pitchPx, rollPx, shake }`.\n343\t\n344\t**Projection.** Keep road.js's two passes (near→far to accumulate the bend and clip at crests, then far→near to paint), but use **square pixels**:\n345\t\n346\t```\n347\tdepth = 1 / tan(fov/2)\n348\tscale = depth / d                    // d = distance ahead of the camera (m), must be > 0.5\n349\tsx = cx + scale * (wx - camX) * hw   // hw = W/2\n350\tsy = cy - scale * (wy - camY) * hw   // NOTE: hw again. road.js uses hh here, which squashes the world on wide screens\n351\tcx = vp.x + W/2 + lookX ;  cy = vp.y + H*horizon + lookY + pitchPx\n352\t```\n353\t- `wx = segment bend + lateral offset`; `wy = segment height + object height`.\n354\t- Pixels per metre at a point: `k = scale * hw`.\n355\t- Use horizon 0.40 for the cab (the dash covers the bottom 30%) and 0.50 for full-screen exterior cameras.\n356\t- Draw distance is 360 segments (1.8 km). The fog default fades from 900 m.\n357\t\n358\t**Rear view (`dir = -1`).** Walk the segments backward, negate the curve and every lateral offset, and swap the strip ends. This is the forward view\n359\tof the mirrored line, so the same painter works. The platform and trackside cameras use it (§4.13).\n360\t\n361\t**Painting, per segment, far→near:**\n362\t1. **Ground band.**\n363\t   - The full-width ground row is the zone's ground colour, alternating two tones every 4 segments for a sense of speed.\n364\t   - On **bridges**, draw water instead: a band 8 m below the deck, with animated glints.\n365\t   - At **crossings**, draw an asphalt road band across all x, with white edge lines.\n366\t2. **Track beds.** For each track, a ballast quad 3.0 m wide. Side tracks and sidings are darker and rustier.\n367\t3. **Sleepers.** Only within 160 m of the camera. Split each segment into 8 slices and draw one dark quad per sleeper (0.24 m along s, 2.4 m\n368\t   across). Beyond 160 m the ballast tone stands in.\n369\t4. **Rails.**\n370\t   - Two quads at ±0.533 m from the track centre, 0.065 m wide, in steel grey.\n371\t   - Where the rail is 2 px or wider, add a 1-px bright top line.\n372\t   - When wet, the rails get brighter and pick up the sky colour.\n373\t5. **Strips** (below): sorted by lateral distance from the camera, farthest first.\n374\t6. **Things** (below): also sorted farthest lateral first.\n375\t7. **Train slices**: actors are drawn as strips (§4.4), plus their front-face thing.\n376\t8. **Catenary wires.**\n377\t   - For each electrified track, two 1-px lines between this segment's projected points: the contact wire at y +5.1 and the messenger at +5.9.\n378\t   - The wire zig-zags ±0.2 m, alternating per mast. The converging wires are a strong depth cue; keep them.\n379\t\n380\t**Strips** are extrusions along the line: `{ s0, s1, x0, x1, y0, y1, faces: { side, top, under, front, back }, kind }`.\n381\t- Each face has a drawer `(g, quad, u0, u1, k, tint)`, where u is metres along s, so patterns repeat in world space (windows every 2.5 m, doors\n382\t  every 5 m, a tactile line).\n383\t- Which faces show:\n384\t  - the **side** nearer to camX (x1 if the strip lies left of the camera, x0 if right; none if it spans the camera);\n385\t  - **top** if the camera is above y1;\n386\t  - **under** if the camera is below y0 (canopies, overpasses, station roofs);\n387\t  - **front** or back where s0/s1 falls inside the slice being painted.\n388\t- Strips are used for:\n389\t  - platforms: y 0→1.1 m; top face with a white edge line and a yellow tactile strip 0.8 m in;\n390\t  - canopies (y 3.6→3.9), station buildings, stair housings;\n391\t  - sound walls, fences, retaining walls;\n392\t  - truss sides (X pattern);\n393\t  - tunnel walls and roof (road.js's tunnel, generalised; roof at 6.2 m; portal face; a wall lamp every 25 m);\n394\t  - overpasses: cross strips spanning all x, with an under face;\n395\t  - rows of houses and warehouses, viaduct parapets;\n396\t  - **trains** (§4.4).\n397\t\n398\t**Things** are point objects: `{ s, x, y, w, h, draw(g, sx, sy, k, ctx) }`.\n399\t- `(sx, sy)` is the screen position of the base centre; `k` is pixels per metre.\n400\t- Every drawer picks a level of detail from k: below 0.4, a dot or nothing; below 3, a 2–3 tone silhouette; 3 and up, full detail with\n401\t  `E.tones` shading.\n402\t- `E.sprite` sprites and `E.shapeSprite` bakes are wrapped as things with metres-per-pixel.\n403\t- **Humanoids and Blobs** draw through a cached `E.View('rail', 'Rail', yaw, 8, q, 1)`, where `q = round16(k / 15)`. Copy `Mode7.view(k)` in\n404\t  `modules/mode7.js`, and turn the yaw so rigs face the camera's side.\n405\t\n406\t**Tint pipeline.** Every colour goes through `tint(hex, depth01, emissive)`:\n407\t- mix toward the sky's horizon colour by the fog factor `1 - exp(-(d/fogDist)^2 * density)`;\n408\t- then apply the time-of-day grade (§4.12) unless the colour is emissive (signal lamps, lamps, lit windows, LED boards, headlight-lit ground);\n409\t- cache the results per frame.\n410\t\n411\t**Night lights.**\n412\t- The headlight is an additive trapezoid (`px.blend(g, a, 'add', ...)`) over our track from 3 m to 120 m, plus a boost to rail and sleeper tones\n413\t  inside it.\n414\t- Lamps and signals get glow discs (`r.glowDisc` or `px.blend` discs).\n415\t\n416\t**API.**\n417\t- `new DH.Rail(compiledRoute, { fov, draw, fog })`\n418\t- `rail.draw(r, cam, { actors, tod, weather, t })`\n419\t- `rail.project(s, x, y)` returns `[sx, sy, k]` (or null) for the last camera. Used for the HUD brackets, the call glove and mouse picks.\n420\t- `rail.pick(sx, sy)` returns the call target or thing under a screen point.\n421\t- `rail.visible(s, x, y)` checks that a point isn't hidden behind a crest or tunnel.\n422\t\n423\t**`#gallery` deep link.** Thirteen fixed shots, each with a caption:\n424\t1. straight, day\n425\t2. curve plus hill\n426\t3. station approach\n427\t4. alongside a platform with a crowd\n428\t5. close to a signal\n429\t6. tunnel mouth\n430\t7. truss bridge\n431\t8. crossing with gates down\n432\t9. quad track with a train alongside\n433\t10. night with headlights\n434\t11. rain\n435\t12. platform rear camera with a train arriving\n436\t13. chase camera\n437\t\n438\t**Tests:**\n439\t- Projection math:\n440\t  - A point on the centreline straight ahead lands at cx.\n441\t  - k halves when the distance doubles.\n442\t  - Square pixels: a 1 m × 1 m quad at 20 m measures the same width and height in pixels.\n443\t  - Rear view mirrors a point.\n444\t- Crest clipping: on a hill, a point beyond the crest returns `visible = false`.\n445\t- Strip faces: a platform left of the camera paints its right side face, not the left one.\n446\t- `project`, then `pick`, round-trips for a signal.\n447\t- The gallery renders all 13 shots with no errors. This is a browser test: a page with `new E.Game({ loop: false })`, drawing with\n448\t  `game.advance(0, { draw: true })`.\n449\t- **Screenshots** for the look review (§8.5).\n450\t\n451\t### 4.2 Route data and compiler (`20-routes.js`, `21-route-compiler.js`)\n452\t\n453\tRoutes are **designer-editable scripts**: sorted arrays of `[s, command, params]`. This is how \"the remainder of the game could later be built out\n454\twith normal designer tools\".\n455\t- On loop routes s wraps modulo the length, so `[-230, 'station', ...]` (Akari Central's platform) lands at 20,370.\n456\t- Spans may cross the seam.\n457\t\n458\t| Command | Params | Produces |\n459\t|---|---|---|\n460\t| `zone` | id | scenery set, ground colours, backdrop layers from s on |\n461\t| `tracks` | `[{ id, x }]` (optional `to` for a diverge over `len`) | track layout from s (e.g. the quad section) |\n462\t| `curve` | `{ len, r, dir: 'L'|'R' }` | segment curves |\n463\t| `grade` | `{ len, permille }` | heights, eased over 50 m at both ends |\n464\t| `limit` | `{ v, until }` (until optional = line speed change) | limit board at s, END board at until, call point |\n465\t| `signal` | `{ id, kind: 'start'|'block'|'home'|'shunt', track }` | signal thing, block boundary, ATS beacon at s−600, call point |\n466\t| `station` | `{ id, name, en, side, len, stop: { 6: s, 10: s }, roof: [a, b], melody, crowd, landmark, tracks }`; the command's s is the platform's start (its rear end) and `len` its length | platform strips, canopy, boards, props, crowd spawn, approach board at stop−400, a stop marker board for each key of `stop` |\n467\t| `tunnel` | `{ len }` | tunnel strips, portal, lamps, sound zone |\n468\t| `bridge` | `{ len, kind: 'truss'|'girder', water }` | deck, truss strips, water band, sound zone |\n469\t| `crossing` | `{ id, road, cars, peds, special }` | road band, gates, lamps, crossbuck, waiting traffic; `special` adds the obstruction emitter 350 m before |\n470\t| `horn` | `{}` | horn board, horn zone of 150 m |\n471\t| `workers` | `{ until, limit, lookout }` | slow-order boards (yellow 25 / end), crew things, lookout rig |\n472\t| `overpass` | `{ w, h, kind }` | cross strip |\n473\t| `viaduct` | `{ len, h }` | the track raised; ground drawn as rooftops far below |\n474\t| `landmark` | `{ id, x, side }` | one hand-drawn landmark thing |\n475\t| `scenery` | `{ thing, x, ... }` | a manual placement |\n476\t\n477\t**Auto-generated:**\n478\t- catenary masts every 50 m on electrified pairs (lattice in the city, concrete poles in the suburbs);\n479\t- km posts every 100 m on the left (\"3.4\");\n480\t- ATS beacons (yellow boxes between the rails);\n481\t- call points for every signal, limit board, station approach board, pass board and horn board;\n482\t- utility poles with sagging wires along suburban roads; sound walls in the city;\n483\t- zone scenery from a **seeded RNG per 50 m cell per side**. Cells occupied by a station, bridge, tunnel, crossing or landmark are skipped.\n484\t\n485\t**Validation** (run at boot in dev, and as a test):\n486\t- stations are in order;\n487\t- each stop target for n cars lies within `[platform start + 20·n, platform end]`, so the whole train fits;\n488\t- home signals stand before their platform's start;\n489\t- every limit ends;\n490\t- no signal stands in a portal segment;\n491\t- block boundaries are strictly increasing;\n492\t- each stage's stops exist on its route.\n493\t\n494\t**Tests:** compile both routes; the segment count = length / 5; `curve` at mid-curve has the right sign; the height after a 25‰ × 800 m grade is\n495\t+20 m (±0.5); a 600 m bridge sets the flag on 120 segments; validation passes; a corrupted script (a stop outside its platform) fails validation.\n496\t\n497\t### 4.3 Train dynamics and the lever (`30-train.js`)\n498\t\n499\t**Lever.**\n500\t- One axis with positions `EB, B8…B1, N, P1…P5`; the trains in §5.2 decide how many notches.\n501\t- `power` moves the lever one position toward P and `brake` one toward B. `neutral` snaps it to N. `eb` jumps to EB.\n502\t- Leaving EB needs speed = 0 (`EB RELEASE` prompt), then one `power` press per position.\n503\t- Each change: a mechanical clack sfx, the handle animation, and a glove follow (§4.14).\n504\t\n505\t**Model, per step (dt = 1/120):**\n506\t```\n507\ttgtP = notch>0 && doorsClosed && !EB ? aP[notch] * min(1, vk[notch] / max(v,1)) * clamp((vmax - v)/5, 0, 1) : 0\n508\ttgtB = EB ? aEB : notch<0 ? aB[-notch] : 0\n509\tP += (tgtP - P) * (1 - exp(-dt / tauP))\n510\tB += (tgtB - B) * (1 - exp(-dt / (tgtB > B ? tauBapply : tauBrelease)))\n511\tres = v > 0 ? r0 + r1*v + r2*v*v : 0                    // km/h/s, v in km/h\n512\tgrd = -0.0353 * grade_permille(s)                        // km/h/s\n513\tadhesion = dry ? 6.0 : TUNING.adhesionRain (2.6)          // km/h/s\n514\tif (P > adhesion) slip: P *= .4 for .8 s (SLIP lamp, whine, comfort −2)\n515\tif (B > adhesion) slide: B = adhesion * .85 (WSP lamp, judder sound)\n516\ta = P - B * sign(v) - res + grd                          // brakes and resistance never push backward\n517\tv = v + a*dt (km/h); if v would cross 0 with the brakes on → v = 0 (held by the brakes)\n518\trollback: if v == 0 and B < .2 and grd < 0 → may roll backward (ROLLBACK warning; >1 m costs −2 s)\n519\ts += v / 3.6 * dt\n520\tjerk = (a - aPrev) / dt (smoothed), used by comfort and the smooth-stop judgment\n521\t```\n522\t\n523\t**Default numbers (Halo 8000; every number below is a dev slider or scaled by one):**\n524\t\n525\t| | P1 | P2 | P3 | P4 | P5 |\n526\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n527\t| a0 (km/h/s) | 0.8 | 1.4 | 2.0 | 2.6 | 3.2 |\n528\t| vk (km/h) | 15 | 25 | 35 | 40 | 45 |\n529\t\n530\t- Brake: Bn = 0.5·n km/h/s (B8 = 4.0). EB 5.0 with τ = 0.3 s.\n531\t- τ apply 0.7 s, τ release 0.5 s, τP 0.3 s. vmax 110 km/h.\n532\t- Resistance: r0 = 0.03, r1 = 0.0005, r2 = 0.000012.\n533\t\n534\t**Check values** (these are tests; allow the stated ranges):\n535\t- 0→80 km/h on P5, level track: 29–34 s.\n536\t- B4 from 80 km/h to a stop: 37–41 s and 425–465 m.\n537\t- EB from 80 km/h: 175–195 m.\n538\t- Coasting at 80 km/h on level track loses 7–10 km/h per minute.\n539\t- (A quick simulation of this model gave 30.4 s, 39.2 s / 439 m, 181 m and 8.2 km/h, and doubling `brakeScale` cut the B4 distance to 232 m.)\n540\t- On 25‰ up with P5 at 30 km/h, the train still accelerates. On 25‰ down at N, it gains speed.\n541\t\n542\t**Tests:** the check values above; the lever passes through every position, clamps at the ends, can't leave EB while moving, and gives no traction with\n543\tthe doors open; rain plus P5 from a stand slips (SLIP flag set); dry track never slips; determinism (the same inputs give the same s after 60 s).\n544\t\n545\t### 4.4 Signals, blocks, other trains, ATS (`31-signals.js`)\n546\t\n547\t**Blocks.** Each `signal` starts a block that runs to the next signal on that track. A block is occupied while any part of any train actor is in it.\n548\t\n549\t**Aspects** (Japanese 4-lamp head, top to bottom Y-G-R-Y). The aspect follows from the clear blocks ahead:\n550\t- 0 clear → **R**;\n551\t- 1 → **Y**;\n552\t- 2 → **YG**;\n553\t- 3 or more → **G**.\n554\t\n555\tRoute data can force **YY** (restricted), used at the terminal entry in S5.\n556\t\n557\t**Aspect speeds:** G = line speed, YG 65, Y 45, YY 25, R = stop before the signal.\n558\t\n559\t**Starting signals** stay R until the dwell sequence says \"ready\" (§4.6), and then show the block aspect.\n560\t\n561\t**Sighting.** A signal's aspect shows on the HUD and becomes callable only once it is *sighted*:\n562\t- within 600 m, visible (`rail.visible`) and not in fog;\n563\t- in fog, within 180 m.\n564\t\n565\tLamps draw at least 1 px with a glow, so far signals read as points of light.\n566\t\n567\t**Train actors** (`DH.TrainActor`):\n568\t- Fields: `{ id, track, cars, livery, s, v, dir, lights, script }`.\n569\t- The script is a list of legs: `{ vmax, accel, decel, until: s|clock, stopAt, dwell, waitAspect }`.\n570\t- Kinds: the preceding freight (S2), the rival express (S4), oncoming locals, parked trains, players' ghosts (§4.23) and the duel opponent.\n571\t- Drawing is all strips:\n572\t  - body side y 1.0→3.9 m, with windows, doors and a livery stripe pattern, repeated per car (20 m) with gaps;\n573\t  - roof, with a pantograph thing on 2 of the cars;\n574\t  - skirt and bogies, as dark boxes;\n575\t  - the front-face thing: headlights, tail lights, destination LED \"HALO LINE · AKARI CENTRAL\", windows, driver silhouette, coupler.\n576\t- Oncoming trains face us (headlights). A preceding train shows its tail lights.\n577\t\n578\t**ATS (simplified ATS-S plus a speed check):**\n579\t- Passing a signal's beacon (s−600) while that signal shows R starts the ATS bell (a repeating ding), a red **ATS** lamp and the\n580\t  `[△] ATS CONFIRM` prompt.\n581\t- If `ats` isn't pressed within **5.0 s**, the ATS fires EB (−10 s, −500 pts).\n582\t- Confirming starts a soft continuous chime, which stops when the speed is under 15 km/h or the signal clears.\n583\t- **Passing a red** (front past the signal while R) is a SPAD: forced EB, −20 s, −1000 pts, banner \"SIGNAL PASSED AT DANGER\", and a strict\n584\t  Hoshino line.\n585\t- Speed check: passing a limit board more than 10 km/h over the limit fires ATS EB (on top of the §4.5 penalty).\n586\t\n587\t**Tests:**\n588\t- The aspect table, with an actor placed in blocks 0, 1, 2 and 3 ahead.\n589\t- A starting signal stays R until ready.\n590\t- ATS: passing the beacon at R rings; no confirm within 5 s fires EB; confirming stops the EB.\n591\t- SPAD is detected exactly when the front crosses s_signal.\n592\t- The S2 freight script reproduces R at signal M6000 when the player follows par (test through the autopilot).\n593\t\n594\t### 4.5 Speed limits (`33-rules.js`)\n595\t\n596\t- The limit in force = min(line speed, every limit board in force over **the whole train length** (the front sets it; it clears when the rear\n597\t  passes the END board), the aspect speed of the last passed signal, the slow-order limit).\n598\t- **Violation:** more than limit + 3 km/h: \"LIMIT!\" banner, −2 s, then −1 s per further 5 km/h of the peak excess, and −300 pts. It counts once\n599\t  per limit section, re-arming when the speed falls under the limit. The tolerance is a slider.\n600\t- The HUD shows the limit in force plus the next limit and its distance (§4.14).\n601\t\n602\t**Tests:** a 70 board; the front passes at 72 (no penalty), then at 76 (one penalty); staying at 80 for 10 s gives no second penalty; the limit clears\n603\tonly after the rear passes the END board.\n604\t\n605\t### 4.6 Stations: approach, stop, judgment, dwell, departure (`32-stations.js`)\n606\t\n607\t**Approach:**\n608\t- At 600 m the announcer starts the PA (§4.17) and the **Stop Gauge** appears (§4.14).\n609\t- At 400 m the station approach board (\"STOP\" or \"PASS\") is a call point.\n610\t- Then the home signal.\n611\t\n612\t**Stop targets.** Each platform has a stop marker per train length (\"6\", \"10\"). The stage's train length picks which one counts; the HUD says\n613\t\"STOP AT 10\".\n614\t\n615\t**Stopping.** When v reaches 0 at a stopping station within 50 m of the target, `err = s_front - target` (+ is past the mark):\n616\t\n617\t| err | label | Halo Time | points | then |\n618\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n619\t| ≤ 0.10 m | **PERFECT HALO!** | +5 s | 1000 | doors open |\n620\t| ≤ 0.30 m | **GREAT!** | +3 s | 700 | doors open |\n621\t| ≤ 0.70 m | **GOOD** | +1 s | 400 | doors open |\n622\t| ≤ 1.00 m | **OK** | 0 | 200 | doors open |\n623\t| short 1–5 m | (6 s to creep up and re-stop) → **SHORT** | −1 s per m | 0 | judged where it finally stands; each re-stop −1 s |\n624\t| short > 5 m | \"MOVE UP\" (doors stay shut) | — | — | must creep up |\n625\t| over 1–5 m | **OVERRUN** | −2 s per m | 0 | doors open |\n626\t| over > 5 m | **OVER THE MARK** | −10 s | −300 | auto back-up at 5 km/h to the mark, then OK |\n627\t\n628\t- The windows scale with difficulty (Easy ×1.5, Expert ×0.7) and the dev slider.\n629\t- **Arrival time** = the clock at the stop, against the timetable:\n630\t  - within ±2 s: **ON TIME!** (+3 s, 500 pts);"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"6170cb8a-a018-458e-a700-2772a5748c63","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:29.975Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","content":"### 4.1 Rail renderer (`DH.Rail`, module `rail.js`): the riskiest piece, so build it first\n\n**Model.** A route is a run of **5 m segments**. Each segment has:\n- `i`, `s`;\n- `curve`: the lateral bend added per segment, `= ±L²/R` m (+ bends right), eased over 20 m at each end;\n- `y0`, `y1`;\n- `zone`;\n- `flags`: tunnel, bridge, crossing, viaduct, quad;\n- `tracks`: `[{ id, x0, x1, electrified, kind }]`, with offsets at both segment ends so tracks can diverge;\n- the lists of strips and things that touch it.\n\n**Camera.** `{ s, x, h, dir: 1 | -1, fov: 75, horizon: .40, lookX, lookY, pitchPx, rollPx, shake }`.\n\n**Projection.** Keep road.js's two passes (near→far to accumulate the bend and clip at crests, then far→near to paint), but use **square pixels**:\n\n```\ndepth = 1 / tan(fov/2)\nscale = depth / d                    // d = distance ahead of the camera (m), must be > 0.5\nsx = cx + scale * (wx - camX) * hw   // hw = W/2\nsy = cy - scale * (wy - camY) * hw   // NOTE: hw again. road.js uses hh here, which squashes the world on wide screens\ncx = vp.x + W/2 + lookX ;  cy = vp.y + H*horizon + lookY + pitchPx\n```\n- `wx = segment bend + lateral offset`; `wy = segment height + object height`.\n- Pixels per metre at a point: `k = scale * hw`.\n- Use horizon 0.40 for the cab (the dash covers the bottom 30%) and 0.50 for full-screen exterior cameras.\n- Draw distance is 360 segments (1.8 km). The fog default fades from 900 m.\n\n**Rear view (`dir = -1`).** Walk the segments backward, negate the curve and every lateral offset, and swap the strip ends. This is the forward view\nof the mirrored line, so the same painter works. The platform and trackside cameras use it (§4.13).\n\n**Painting, per segment, far→near:**\n1. **Ground band.**\n   - The full-width ground row is the zone's ground colour, alternating two tones every 4 segments for a sense of speed.\n   - On **bridges**, draw water instead: a band 8 m below the deck, with animated glints.\n   - At **crossings**, draw an asphalt road band across all x, with white edge lines.\n2. **Track beds.** For each track, a ballast quad 3.0 m wide. Side tracks and sidings are darker and rustier.\n3. **Sleepers.** Only within 160 m of the camera. Split each segment into 8 slices and draw one dark quad per sleeper (0.24 m along s, 2.4 m\n   across). Beyond 160 m the ballast tone stands in.\n4. **Rails.**\n   - Two quads at ±0.533 m from the track centre, 0.065 m wide, in steel grey.\n   - Where the rail is 2 px or wider, add a 1-px bright top line.\n   - When wet, the rails get brighter and pick up the sky colour.\n5. **Strips** (below): sorted by lateral distance from the camera, farthest first.\n6. **Things** (below): also sorted farthest lateral first.\n7. **Train slices**: actors are drawn as strips (§4.4), plus their front-face thing.\n8. **Catenary wires.**\n   - For each electrified track, two 1-px lines between this segment's projected points: the contact wire at y +5.1 and the messenger at +5.9.\n   - The wire zig-zags ±0.2 m, alternating per mast. The converging wires are a strong depth cue; keep them.\n\n**Strips** are extrusions along the line: `{ s0, s1, x0, x1, y0, y1, faces: { side, top, under, front, back }, kind }`.\n- Each face has a drawer `(g, quad, u0, u1, k, tint)`, where u is metres along s, so patterns repeat in world space (windows every 2.5 m, doors\n  every 5 m, a tactile line).\n- Which faces show:\n  - the **side** nearer to camX (x1 if the strip lies left of the camera, x0 if right; none if it spans the camera);\n  - **top** if the camera is above y1;\n  - **under** if the camera is below y0 (canopies, overpasses, station roofs);\n  - **front** or back where s0/s1 falls inside the slice being painted.\n- Strips are used for:\n  - platforms: y 0→1.1 m; top face with a white edge line and a yellow tactile strip 0.8 m in;\n  - canopies (y 3.6→3.9), station buildings, stair housings;\n  - sound walls, fences, retaining walls;\n  - truss sides (X pattern);\n  - tunnel walls and roof (road.js's tunnel, generalised; roof at 6.2 m; portal face; a wall lamp every 25 m);\n  - overpasses: cross strips spanning all x, with an under face;\n  - rows of houses and warehouses, viaduct parapets;\n  - **trains** (§4.4).\n\n**Things** are point objects: `{ s, x, y, w, h, draw(g, sx, sy, k, ctx) }`.\n- `(sx, sy)` is the screen position of the base centre; `k` is pixels per metre.\n- Every drawer picks a level of detail from k: below 0.4, a dot or nothing; below 3, a 2–3 tone silhouette; 3 and up, full detail with\n  `E.tones` shading.\n- `E.sprite` sprites and `E.shapeSprite` bakes are wrapped as things with metres-per-pixel.\n- **Humanoids and Blobs** draw through a cached `E.View('rail', 'Rail', yaw, 8, q, 1)`, where `q = round16(k / 15)`. Copy `Mode7.view(k)` in\n  `modules/mode7.js`, and turn the yaw so rigs face the camera's side.\n\n**Tint pipeline.** Every colour goes through `tint(hex, depth01, emissive)`:\n- mix toward the sky's horizon colour by the fog factor `1 - exp(-(d/fogDist)^2 * density)`;\n- then apply the time-of-day grade (§4.12) unless the colour is emissive (signal lamps, lamps, lit windows, LED boards, headlight-lit ground);\n- cache the results per frame.\n\n**Night lights.**\n- The headlight is an additive trapezoid (`px.blend(g, a, 'add', ...)`) over our track from 3 m to 120 m, plus a boost to rail and sleeper tones\n  inside it.\n- Lamps and signals get glow discs (`r.glowDisc` or `px.blend` discs).\n\n**API.**\n- `new DH.Rail(compiledRoute, { fov, draw, fog })`\n- `rail.draw(r, cam, { actors, tod, weather, t })`\n- `rail.project(s, x, y)` returns `[sx, sy, k]` (or null) for the last camera. Used for the HUD brackets, the call glove and mouse picks.\n- `rail.pick(sx, sy)` returns the call target or thing under a screen point.\n- `rail.visible(s, x, y)` checks that a point isn't hidden behind a crest or tunnel.\n\n**`#gallery` deep link.** Thirteen fixed shots, each with a caption:\n1. straight, day\n2. curve plus hill\n3. station approach\n4. alongside a platform with a crowd\n5. close to a signal\n6. tunnel mouth\n7. truss bridge\n8. crossing with gates down\n9. quad track with a train alongside\n10. night with headlights\n11. rain\n12. platform rear camera with a train arriving\n13. chase camera\n\n**Tests:**\n- Projection math:\n  - A point on the centreline straight ahead lands at cx.\n  - k halves when the distance doubles.\n  - Square pixels: a 1 m × 1 m quad at 20 m measures the same width and height in pixels.\n  - Rear view mirrors a point.\n- Crest clipping: on a hill, a point beyond the crest returns `visible = false`.\n- Strip faces: a platform left of the camera paints its right side face, not the left one.\n- `project`, then `pick`, round-trips for a signal.\n- The gallery renders all 13 shots with no errors. This is a browser test: a page with `new E.Game({ loop: false })`, drawing with\n  `game.advance(0, { draw: true })`.\n- **Screenshots** for the look review (§8.5).\n\n### 4.2 Route data and compiler (`20-routes.js`, `21-route-compiler.js`)\n\nRoutes are **designer-editable scripts**: sorted arrays of `[s, command, params]`. This is how \"the remainder of the game could later be built out\nwith normal designer tools\".\n- On loop routes s wraps modulo the length, so `[-230, 'station', ...]` (Akari Central's platform) lands at 20,370.\n- Spans may cross the seam.\n\n| Command | Params | Produces |\n|---|---|---|\n| `zone` | id | scenery set, ground colours, backdrop layers from s on |\n| `tracks` | `[{ id, x }]` (optional `to` for a diverge over `len`) | track layout from s (e.g. the quad section) |\n| `curve` | `{ len, r, dir: 'L'|'R' }` | segment curves |\n| `grade` | `{ len, permille }` | heights, eased over 50 m at both ends |\n| `limit` | `{ v, until }` (until optional = line speed change) | limit board at s, END board at until, call point |\n| `signal` | `{ id, kind: 'start'|'block'|'home'|'shunt', track }` | signal thing, block boundary, ATS beacon at s−600, call point |\n| `station` | `{ id, name, en, side, len, stop: { 6: s, 10: s }, roof: [a, b], melody, crowd, landmark, tracks }`; the command's s is the platform's start (its rear end) and `len` its length | platform strips, canopy, boards, props, crowd spawn, approach board at stop−400, a stop marker board for each key of `stop` |\n| `tunnel` | `{ len }` | tunnel strips, portal, lamps, sound zone |\n| `bridge` | `{ len, kind: 'truss'|'girder', water }` | deck, truss strips, water band, sound zone |\n| `crossing` | `{ id, road, cars, peds, special }` | road band, gates, lamps, crossbuck, waiting traffic; `special` adds the obstruction emitter 350 m before |\n| `horn` | `{}` | horn board, horn zone of 150 m |\n| `workers` | `{ until, limit, lookout }` | slow-order boards (yellow 25 / end), crew things, lookout rig |\n| `overpass` | `{ w, h, kind }` | cross strip |\n| `viaduct` | `{ len, h }` | the track raised; ground drawn as rooftops far below |\n| `landmark` | `{ id, x, side }` | one hand-drawn landmark thing |\n| `scenery` | `{ thing, x, ... }` | a manual placement |\n\n**Auto-generated:**\n- catenary masts every 50 m on electrified pairs (lattice in the city, concrete poles in the suburbs);\n- km posts every 100 m on the left (\"3.4\");\n- ATS beacons (yellow boxes between the rails);\n- call points for every signal, limit board, station approach board, pass board and horn board;\n- utility poles with sagging wires along suburban roads; sound walls in the city;\n- zone scenery from a **seeded RNG per 50 m cell per side**. Cells occupied by a station, bridge, tunnel, crossing or landmark are skipped.\n\n**Validation** (run at boot in dev, and as a test):\n- stations are in order;\n- each stop target for n cars lies within `[platform start + 20·n, platform end]`, so the whole train fits;\n- home signals stand before their platform's start;\n- every limit ends;\n- no signal stands in a portal segment;\n- block boundaries are strictly increasing;\n- each stage's stops exist on its route.\n\n**Tests:** compile both routes; the segment count = length / 5; `curve` at mid-curve has the right sign; the height after a 25‰ × 800 m grade is\n+20 m (±0.5); a 600 m bridge sets the flag on 120 segments; validation passes; a corrupted script (a stop outside its platform) fails validation.\n\n### 4.3 Train dynamics and the lever (`30-train.js`)\n\n**Lever.**\n- One axis with positions `EB, B8…B1, N, P1…P5`; the trains in §5.2 decide how many notches.\n- `power` moves the lever one position toward P and `brake` one toward B. `neutral` snaps it to N. `eb` jumps to EB.\n- Leaving EB needs speed = 0 (`EB RELEASE` prompt), then one `power` press per position.\n- Each change: a mechanical clack sfx, the handle animation, and a glove follow (§4.14).\n\n**Model, per step (dt = 1/120):**\n```\ntgtP = notch>0 && doorsClosed && !EB ? aP[notch] * min(1, vk[notch] / max(v,1)) * clamp((vmax - v)/5, 0, 1) : 0\ntgtB = EB ? aEB : notch<0 ? aB[-notch] : 0\nP += (tgtP - P) * (1 - exp(-dt / tauP))\nB += (tgtB - B) * (1 - exp(-dt / (tgtB > B ? tauBapply : tauBrelease)))\nres = v > 0 ? r0 + r1*v + r2*v*v : 0                    // km/h/s, v in km/h\ngrd = -0.0353 * grade_permille(s)                        // km/h/s\nadhesion = dry ? 6.0 : TUNING.adhesionRain (2.6)          // km/h/s\nif (P > adhesion) slip: P *= .4 for .8 s (SLIP lamp, whine, comfort −2)\nif (B > adhesion) slide: B = adhesion * .85 (WSP lamp, judder sound)\na = P - B * sign(v) - res + grd                          // brakes and resistance never push backward\nv = v + a*dt (km/h); if v would cross 0 with the brakes on → v = 0 (held by the brakes)\nrollback: if v == 0 and B < .2 and grd < 0 → may roll backward (ROLLBACK warning; >1 m costs −2 s)\ns += v / 3.6 * dt\njerk = (a - aPrev) / dt (smoothed), used by comfort and the smooth-stop judgment\n```\n\n**Default numbers (Halo 8000; every number below is a dev slider or scaled by one):**\n\n| | P1 | P2 | P3 | P4 | P5 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| a0 (km/h/s) | 0.8 | 1.4 | 2.0 | 2.6 | 3.2 |\n| vk (km/h) | 15 | 25 | 35 | 40 | 45 |\n\n- Brake: Bn = 0.5·n km/h/s (B8 = 4.0). EB 5.0 with τ = 0.3 s.\n- τ apply 0.7 s, τ release 0.5 s, τP 0.3 s. vmax 110 km/h.\n- Resistance: r0 = 0.03, r1 = 0.0005, r2 = 0.000012.\n\n**Check values** (these are tests; allow the stated ranges):\n- 0→80 km/h on P5, level track: 29–34 s.\n- B4 from 80 km/h to a stop: 37–41 s and 425–465 m.\n- EB from 80 km/h: 175–195 m.\n- Coasting at 80 km/h on level track loses 7–10 km/h per minute.\n- (A quick simulation of this model gave 30.4 s, 39.2 s / 439 m, 181 m and 8.2 km/h, and doubling `brakeScale` cut the B4 distance to 232 m.)\n- On 25‰ up with P5 at 30 km/h, the train still accelerates. On 25‰ down at N, it gains speed.\n\n**Tests:** the check values above; the lever passes through every position, clamps at the ends, can't leave EB while moving, and gives no traction with\nthe doors open; rain plus P5 from a stand slips (SLIP flag set); dry track never slips; determinism (the same inputs give the same s after 60 s).\n\n### 4.4 Signals, blocks, other trains, ATS (`31-signals.js`)\n\n**Blocks.** Each `signal` starts a block that runs to the next signal on that track. A block is occupied while any part of any train actor is in it.\n\n**Aspects** (Japanese 4-lamp head, top to bottom Y-G-R-Y). The aspect follows from the clear blocks ahead:\n- 0 clear → **R**;\n- 1 → **Y**;\n- 2 → **YG**;\n- 3 or more → **G**.\n\nRoute data can force **YY** (restricted), used at the terminal entry in S5.\n\n**Aspect speeds:** G = line speed, YG 65, Y 45, YY 25, R = stop before the signal.\n\n**Starting signals** stay R until the dwell sequence says \"ready\" (§4.6), and then show the block aspect.\n\n**Sighting.** A signal's aspect shows on the HUD and becomes callable only once it is *sighted*:\n- within 600 m, visible (`rail.visible`) and not in fog;\n- in fog, within 180 m.\n\nLamps draw at least 1 px with a glow, so far signals read as points of light.\n\n**Train actors** (`DH.TrainActor`):\n- Fields: `{ id, track, cars, livery, s, v, dir, lights, script }`.\n- The script is a list of legs: `{ vmax, accel, decel, until: s|clock, stopAt, dwell, waitAspect }`.\n- Kinds: the preceding freight (S2), the rival express (S4), oncoming locals, parked trains, players' ghosts (§4.23) and the duel opponent.\n- Drawing is all strips:\n  - body side y 1.0→3.9 m, with windows, doors and a livery stripe pattern, repeated per car (20 m) with gaps;\n  - roof, with a pantograph thing on 2 of the cars;\n  - skirt and bogies, as dark boxes;\n  - the front-face thing: headlights, tail lights, destination LED \"HALO LINE · AKARI CENTRAL\", windows, driver silhouette, coupler.\n- Oncoming trains face us (headlights). A preceding train shows its tail lights.\n\n**ATS (simplified ATS-S plus a speed check):**\n- Passing a signal's beacon (s−600) while that signal shows R starts the ATS bell (a repeating ding), a red **ATS** lamp and the\n  `[△] ATS CONFIRM` prompt.\n- If `ats` isn't pressed within **5.0 s**, the ATS fires EB (−10 s, −500 pts).\n- Confirming starts a soft continuous chime, which stops when the speed is under 15 km/h or the signal clears.\n- **Passing a red** (front past the signal while R) is a SPAD: forced EB, −20 s, −1000 pts, banner \"SIGNAL PASSED AT DANGER\", and a strict\n  Hoshino line.\n- Speed check: passing a limit board more than 10 km/h over the limit fires ATS EB (on top of the §4.5 penalty).\n\n**Tests:**\n- The aspect table, with an actor placed in blocks 0, 1, 2 and 3 ahead.\n- A starting signal stays R until ready.\n- ATS: passing the beacon at R rings; no confirm within 5 s fires EB; confirming stops the EB.\n- SPAD is detected exactly when the front crosses s_signal.\n- The S2 freight script reproduces R at signal M6000 when the player follows par (test through the autopilot).\n\n### 4.5 Speed limits (`33-rules.js`)\n\n- The limit in force = min(line speed, every limit board in force over **the whole train length** (the front sets it; it clears when the rear\n  passes the END board), the aspect speed of the last passed signal, the slow-order limit).\n- **Violation:** more than limit + 3 km/h: \"LIMIT!\" banner, −2 s, then −1 s per further 5 km/h of the peak excess, and −300 pts. It counts once\n  per limit section, re-arming when the speed falls under the limit. The tolerance is a slider.\n- The HUD shows the limit in force plus the next limit and its distance (§4.14).\n\n**Tests:** a 70 board; the front passes at 72 (no penalty), then at 76 (one penalty); staying at 80 for 10 s gives no second penalty; the limit clears\nonly after the rear passes the END board.\n\n### 4.6 Stations: approach, stop, judgment, dwell, departure (`32-stations.js`)\n\n**Approach:**\n- At 600 m the announcer starts the PA (§4.17) and the **Stop Gauge** appears (§4.14).\n- At 400 m the station approach board (\"STOP\" or \"PASS\") is a call point.\n- Then the home signal.\n\n**Stop targets.** Each platform has a stop marker per train length (\"6\", \"10\"). The stage's train length picks which one counts; the HUD says\n\"STOP AT 10\".\n\n**Stopping.** When v reaches 0 at a stopping station within 50 m of the target, `err = s_front - target` (+ is past the mark):\n\n| err | label | Halo Time | points | then |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ≤ 0.10 m | **PERFECT HALO!** | +5 s | 1000 | doors open |\n| ≤ 0.30 m | **GREAT!** | +3 s | 700 | doors open |\n| ≤ 0.70 m | **GOOD** | +1 s | 400 | doors open |\n| ≤ 1.00 m | **OK** | 0 | 200 | doors open |\n| short 1–5 m | (6 s to creep up and re-stop) → **SHORT** | −1 s per m | 0 | judged where it finally stands; each re-stop −1 s |\n| short > 5 m | \"MOVE UP\" (doors stay shut) | — | — | must creep up |\n| over 1–5 m | **OVERRUN** | −2 s per m | 0 | doors open |\n| over > 5 m | **OVER THE MARK** | −10 s | −300 | auto back-up at 5 km/h to the mark, then OK |\n\n- The windows scale with difficulty (Easy ×1.5, Expert ×0.7) and the dev slider.\n- **Arrival time** = the clock at the stop, against the timetable:\n  - within ±2 s: **ON TIME!** (+3 s, 500 pts);","numLines":300,"startLine":331,"totalLines":1952}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PnRny64JzUQXqaWvLRQvAU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
