{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrZD3PcnoCD3gpud4Gb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Q2RCJcqFoRyhw4rtG27dMZ","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","offset":631,"limit":320},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9214,"cache_read_input_tokens":80824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9214},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"752f4fb9-7fce-4eba-b5ee-a8710f4b7eb9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:32.590Z","request_id":"req_011CghrZCWtS14dTmpwd6jCC","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Q2RCJcqFoRyhw4rtG27dMZ":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","offset":631,"limit":320}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q2RCJcqFoRyhw4rtG27dMZ","type":"tool_result","content":"631\t  - late beyond the window: **LATE +n** (−1 s for each second past the window; the multiplier is a slider);\n632\t  - early beyond the window: **EARLY** (no bonus, 100 pts).\n633\t- **Smooth stop:** if the deceleration at the moment of stopping is ≤ 1.0 km/h/s and EB wasn't used in the last 100 m: **SMOOTH!** (+2 s, 300 pts).\n634\t  Teach it in S0 (\"ease to B1 just before you stop\").\n635\t- Passing a stopping station without stopping (the front more than 50 m past the target while moving): −10 s, \"MISSED STOP\", and the stage\n636\t  continues to the next stop. If it was the last stop, the stage ends there and goes to results.\n637\t- Stopping at a **pass** station within 50 m: \"WRONG STOP\" (−5 s); the next `power` continues.\n638\t\n639\t**Dwell and departure** (every beat has a sound; timings for Normal):\n640\t1. t+1.0: doors open (chime \"pin-pon\" ×2; side lamp L/R on the dash). Passengers alight and board (§4.10).\n641\t2. The departure melody (the station's own, §5.8) starts at `max(dep − 9 s, t + 6 s)` and lasts 6–8 s.\n642\t3. Then the \"doors closing\" announcement (Mika, gibberish) and the door chime; the doors close (thunk).\n643\t4. +1.0 s: the conductor's buzzer (two short buzzes). +1.5 s: the **pilot lamp** lights (all doors closed).\n644\t5. The starting signal clears at `max(dep − 3 s, pilot lamp)` if the block allows. A **departure call** opens: \"Shuppatsu, shinko!\" (§4.7).\n645\t6. Power is accepted once the pilot lamp is lit. Pressing power earlier gives a short \"DOORS\" buzz and no penalty.\n646\t- If the train arrived late, the dwell shrinks to at least 12 s, so the train can make time back.\n647\t- Events can add time: the **door rush** in S4 re-opens the doors for 8 s.\n648\t\n649\t**Stop Cam** (setting, on by default):\n650\t- After each judgment, an inset slides in (or full screen on Easy) for 2.5 s; any button skips it.\n651\t- It is a **2D side elevation**: the platform edge, the stop marker post with its number, the train's front car in profile easing to its final\n652\t  position, a dimension line (\"+0.12 m\"), and the door queue marks lining up with the doors.\n653\t- Haro-kun pops up on PERFECT.\n654\t\n655\t**Tests:**\n656\t- The judgment table at err = 0.05, 0.25, 0.6, 0.95, −2, +3 and +7 (back-up then OK).\n657\t- Re-stop counting.\n658\t- ON TIME, LATE and EARLY at clock offsets −3, 0, +5.\n659\t- SMOOTH when B1 is applied at < 3 km/h; no SMOOTH when B8 is held to the stop.\n660\t- Dwell order: doors, melody, close, buzzer, pilot, signal, call.\n661\t- Power before the pilot lamp gives no movement.\n662\t- A missed stop and a wrong stop each apply their penalty.\n663\t\n664\t### 4.7 Point and call (*shisa kanko*) and the horn (`33-rules.js`)\n665\t\n666\t**Call points:** every signal (once sighted), limit board, station approach board, pass board, crossing (optional, no penalty) and the departure.\n667\t\n668\t**Window.**\n669\t- For things on the line, the window opens 180 m ahead and closes 30 m ahead.\n670\t- The **GREAT** sweet spot is 120–60 m ahead.\n671\t- The departure call's window runs from \"signal clear\" until 5 s after the doors close; it counts as GREAT if made before the first power notch.\n672\t- On screen: a thin white **bracket** around the target (via `rail.project`), with a prompt `[✕] CALL` (glyph per device, §4.15).\n673\t\n674\t**Pressing `call`:**\n675\t- The **white-gloved hand** rises from the bottom of the screen and points at the target's screen position (raise 0.15 s, hold 0.4 s, back 0.25 s).\n676\t- The driver's voice says the phrase in gibberish (§4.17), with a subtitle in romaji and English.\n677\t- Score: GREAT +0.5 s and 100 pts; OK 50 pts. Missing a call costs nothing in Halo Time; Hoshino remarks once per stage.\n678\t- **Auto calls** (assist setting) makes the calls for you at the sweet spot, with no score.\n679\t- With the mouse, clicking the bracketed thing (`rail.pick`) or anywhere in the view while one call is open does the same.\n680\t\n681\t**Phrases** (aspect-aware; subtitles shown):\n682\t\n683\t| Target | Romaji | English subtitle |\n684\t|---|---|---|\n685\t| starting signal | Shuppatsu, shinko! | Departure. Proceed! |\n686\t| block G | Heisoku, shinko! | Block. Proceed! |\n687\t| home G | Jonai, shinko! | Home signal. Proceed! |\n688\t| YG | Gensoku! | Reduce speed! |\n689\t| Y | Chui! | Caution! |\n690\t| YY | Keikai! | Restricted! |\n691\t| R | Teishi! | Stop! |\n692\t| limit board | Seigen, (number)! | Limit (number)! |\n693\t| stop approach | Teisha! | Stopping! |\n694\t| pass board | Tsuka! | Passing! |\n695\t| crossing | Fumikiri, yoshi! | Crossing, clear! |\n696\t\n697\t**Horn.**\n698\t- The horn board starts a 150 m **horn zone**. Holding `horn` for at least 0.5 s inside it gives \"HORN +1\" (+1 s, 100 pts).\n699\t- Leaving the zone without sounding it costs −1 s (Normal/Expert) or nothing (Easy).\n700\t- The horn always plays when held (§4.16). Track workers (§4.11) wave when they hear it.\n701\t\n702\t**Tests:** a window opens and closes at the right distances; GREAT vs OK by distance; a call works only while its target is sighted; the departure call\n703\tcounts as GREAT before power; the auto-calls setting gives no points; a horn hold of 0.6 s in the zone scores; 0.3 s doesn't; a missed zone costs −1 s\n704\ton Normal and 0 on Easy.\n705\t\n706\t### 4.8 Timetable and clock (`32-stations.js`)\n707\t\n708\t- Each stage has `startClock` and `stops: [{ id, arr, dep }]`, plus `passes: [{ id, at }]` for display. Show times as `HH:MM:SS`.\n709\t- The **timetable card** on the dash lists each stop's ARR and DEP, highlights the next stop, and marks passes as \"PASS\".\n710\t- The **pocket watch** shows the clock, with a ticking second hand.\n711\t- **Calibration** (`node tools/calibrate.mjs`): run the autopilot in **min-time mode** (§4.19, \"par\") over every stage with default tuning and\n712\t  print the arrival clocks. Set each timetable so par arrives **6–12 s early** on Normal: a good human can then be on time, and the\n713\t  timetable-aware autopilot has room to arrive on the second. The S4 door rush and the S2 freight wait are part of the par run.\n714\t- The §5.3 numbers are starting values; replace them with calibrated ones and commit them as data.\n715\t\n716\t**Tests:** the clock advances 1:1 with game time and stops while paused; calibrated par arrives early by 6–12 s at every stop (a guard against tuning\n717\tdrift); the timetable card highlights the right row.\n718\t\n719\t### 4.9 Halo Time, score, ranks (`33-rules.js`)\n720\t\n721\t**Halo Time** is the life: a pool of seconds.\n722\t\n723\t| Difficulty | Start | Stop windows | Penalties |\n724\t|---|---|---|---|\n725\t| Easy | 40 s | ×1.5 | half |\n726\t| Normal | 25 s | ×1.0 | as listed |\n727\t| Expert | 15 s | ×0.7 | ×1.5 |\n728\t\n729\t- The cap is 99 s.\n730\t- Changes apply at events (arrival, stop, penalty, bonus), each with a floating \"+3 s\" or \"−2 s\" by the ring.\n731\t- At ≤ 10 s the ring pulses red and a clock tick plays.\n732\t- At **0**, game over at once: the screen desaturates, the halo ring cracks (particles), and Hoshino says \"That's enough. Hand me the key.\"\n733\t- S0 shows the ring but freezes it.\n734\t\n735\t**Bonuses and penalties not already covered:**\n736\t- obstruction avoided (stopped before the crossing): **HAZARD AVOIDED** +5 s, 800 pts;\n737\t- EB with no hazard: −3 s, −200 pts;\n738\t- rollback over 1 m: −2 s.\n739\t\n740\t**Score:** the points in the tables above, plus remaining Halo Time × 50 and comfort × 5 at the end of the stage.\n741\t\n742\t**Checkpoints.**\n743\t- At every departure (the moment the pilot lamp lights), take a snapshot: s, clock, Halo Time, score, comfort, the events fired, the other train\n744\t  actors' state, and the dwell phase.\n745\t- The pause menu's **Retry from last station** restores it as it was.\n746\t- After a game over, **Continue from last station** restores it with Halo Time set back to the stage's start value and the score halved.\n747\t- **Retry stage** starts the stage over.\n748\t\n749\t**Ranks:** let **Max** = the sum of every scoring chance in the stage (each stop PERFECT, each arrival on time, each SMOOTH, every call GREAT, every\n750\thorn, the hazard bonus, Halo Time end value = start + bonuses, comfort 100).\n751\t- **S** ≥ 90% of Max;\n752\t- **A** ≥ 75%;\n753\t- **B** ≥ 55%;\n754\t- **C** ≥ 35%;\n755\t- otherwise **D**.\n756\t\n757\tThe ranks show as a red *hanko* stamp on the results screen.\n758\t\n759\t**Tests:** game over fires at 0; Halo Time is capped at 99; Max computes for S1 with no NaN; a perfect autopilot run ranks S; a run with three\n760\toverruns ranks C or lower; difficulty multipliers apply.\n761\t\n762\t### 4.10 Passengers, crowds and comfort (`33-rules.js`, `art-people.js`)\n763\t\n764\t- **Door queues.** For the stage's train length, compute the door positions back from the stop target (4 doors per 20 m car, at 2.6 / 7.4 /\n765\t  12.6 / 17.4 m from the car front).\n766\t- Each door mark gets 0–3 queued Humanoids (the station's `crowd` level times the stage's time-of-day factor), from a seeded stream.\n767\t- Mix of looks: commuters (suit and coat, briefcase drawn at `rig.hand()`), students (uniforms), elders, kids, festival visitors (yukata colours,\n768\t  S3), and umbrellas in rain.\n769\t- **Animation:**\n770\t  - idle breathing (built in);\n771\t  - heads turn toward the arriving train (facing);\n772\t  - kids `pose: 'wave'` at the cab;\n773\t  - when the doors open, alighting passengers spawn at the doors and walk to the stairs, and the queued ones walk in and vanish;\n774\t  - at departure, the platform staff do `pose: 'point'`, then `'flag'`;\n775\t  - at the S5 finale, everyone `'bow'`s.\n776\t- **Levels of detail:** full rigs within 60 m; beyond that, cached 2-tone silhouette sprites.\n777\t- **Comfort** starts at 100.\n778\t  - Penalties: a change of ≥ 3 notches within 0.4 s while moving (−2); EB (−15); slip or slide (−3); more than 5 km/h over a curve limit (−5/s);\n779\t    a stop jolt with final decel > 2.0 km/h/s (−8); rollback (−5).\n780\t  - It recovers +1 every 8 s of smooth running.\n781\t  - A passenger face icon on the dash has 4 moods. Mika gives a line at < 60.\n782\t\n783\t**Tests:** queue counts per crowd level; door positions match the train length and stop target; boarding empties the queues before the doors close;\n784\tcomfort penalties and recovery; the LOD switches at 60 m.\n785\t\n786\t### 4.11 Events (`34-events.js`)\n787\t\n788\tData-driven per stage: `{ id, type, at: s | clock | trigger, params }`. Each fires once and is logged in `snapshot().events`.\n789\t\n790\t| type | behaviour |\n791\t|---|---|\n792\t| `oncoming` | a train actor spawns on the opposite track 1,400 m ahead, heading toward us at 80 km/h; passing gives a whoosh, a pressure jolt (shake) and a toot |\n793\t| `preceding` | a train actor ahead on our track with a legs script (S2 freight); it holds blocks |\n794\t| `rival` | a same-direction express on the parallel track (S4), scripted relative to our progress: alongside, then pulling ahead, stopping at Koen beside us |\n795\t| `crossing` | gates and bells: warning when our ETA < 25 s or within 500 m; gates down in 4 s; up after our rear clears. Waiting kei cars, bikes and pedestrians |\n796\t| `obstruction` | a crossing blocked (S3: the festival's takoyaki cart). The special emitter 350 m before flashes red; stop before the crossing. It clears 12 s after we stop |\n797\t| `workers` | slow order 25 km/h; a lookout rig with a flag and whistle; the crew steps aside and waves on the horn (bows if we're under the limit) |\n798\t| `doorRush` | at a station: Kenji runs, Mika re-opens the doors (+8 s dwell) |\n799\t| `festival` | crowd ×2, lantern strings, taiko drum loop near the station |\n800\t| `fog` | fog density from s to s, plus sighting distance 180 m |\n801\t| `glare` | sun-glare bloom when the track heading points at the low sun; the visor drops |\n802\t| `radio` | a story line (§4.18) |\n803\t| `tip` | a first-time tutorial tip (once per save, unless \"Show tips\" is on) |\n804\t| `flash` | the oncoming last train flashes its headlights; answer with the horn within 4 s for +2 s (S5) |\n805\t\n806\t**Tests:** each event fires once in its stage under the autopilot playthrough; crossing gates are down while our train is on the crossing; the obstruction\n807\tclears only after we stop; the horn makes workers wave; door rush adds 8 s to that dwell.\n808\t\n809\t### 4.12 Time of day, weather and light (`40-sound.js` for audio, `rail.js` for the image)\n810\t\n811\t**Time of day** is a float hour from the stage (or Free Run). It picks one of these grades, blended linearly between neighbours:\n812\t\n813\t| grade | hours | sky (top → horizon) | ambient | notes |\n814\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n815\t| dawn | 5–7 | #2a2850 → #f0a080 | .70 warm | low sun east, long shadows (darker ground bands) |\n816\t| day | 8–15 | #3a78d8 → #bfe0ff | 1.0 | |\n817\t| afternoon haze | 14–16 | #5a8ad0 → #e8e0c8 | .95 | |\n818\t| sunset | 17–18.5 | #3a2a6a → #ff9a4a | .75 orange | big low sun on the backdrop; glare zones |\n819\t| night | 19.5–4.5 | #060818 → #1a2440 | .30 blue | stars, moon, city glow; lit windows; headlights; platform lights stay bright |\n820\t\n821\t- The backdrop is an `E.Backdrop` with layers per zone (mountains, hills, city, sea, clouds). Its `turn` scrolls with the curve × distance travelled.\n822\t- **Rain** (S2, Free Run):\n823\t  - darker grade (−20%); streaks and windshield droplets that build up;\n824\t  - wipers sweep every 1.2 s in the cab;\n825\t  - wet rails; puddles on platforms (bright blots);\n826\t  - umbrellas on the crowds;\n827\t  - rain hiss in the audio, and lower adhesion (§4.3).\n828\t- **Fog:** distance fog, larger signal glows, short sighting. Hoshino: \"When you can't see, the signals see for you.\"\n829\t- **Glare:** an additive white-orange bloom over the upper half, scaled by `cos(heading − sunHeading)`. The sun visor animates down.\n830\t\n831\t**Tests:** the grade blend is continuous at every boundary hour; rain sets the adhesion; fog sets sighting to 180 m.\n832\t\n833\t### 4.13 Cameras and the director (`60-drive.js`, `61-attract.js`)\n834\t\n835\t| camera | placement | use |\n836\t|---|---|---|\n837\t| `cab` | s_front − 1.5, x = track, h 2.9, horizon .40 | gameplay (default) |\n838\t| `cabMin` | the same, without the dashboard | \"Minimal\" cab display setting; duel viewports |\n839\t| `chase` | behind the rear by 25 m, h 9, x +2, fov 70 | View button; attract |\n840\t| `chaseHigh` | rear − 40 m, h 22, x +6 | quad-track shots, the hub card |\n841\t| `platform` | on the platform 15 m past the stop target, h 1.7, `dir: -1` | View button at stations; attract; the S5 finale |\n842\t| `trackside` | 4 m beside the line at a scripted s ahead, h 2, `dir: -1`; holds while the train passes, then cuts | attract, ending |\n843\t| `stopCam` | 2D side view (§4.6) | after every stop |\n844\t\n845\t- `view` cycles through cab → chase → platform (platform only near a station) → cab.\n846\t- **Cab feel:**\n847\t  - lateral sway ∝ curve × v²;\n848\t  - 1-px vertical bumps at rail joints (every 25 m, timed with the joint sound);\n849\t  - the view pitches 2–3 px forward under braking and back under power (`pitchPx` follows the smoothed acceleration);\n850\t  - a jolt on EB.\n851\t  - The \"Camera sway\" setting scales these; reduce motion turns them off.\n852\t- **Look:** the right stick or a right-drag shifts `lookX` up to ±25% of W and `lookY` ±10% of H, springing back. It's a glance, not a free camera.\n853\t- **Director** (attract, ending): a list of shots `{ cam, dur, at }`, cut with 0.3 s dissolves.\n854\t\n855\t**Tests:** each camera projects our train's front to the expected screen side; `view` cycles only through the cameras that apply; the director runs\n856\tits shots in order.\n857\t\n858\t### 4.14 Cab, HUD, stop gauge (`50-cab.js`, `51-hud.js`)\n859\t\n860\t**10-foot rules:**\n861\t- Nothing smaller than the 5×7 font at scale 1. Key numbers (speed, stop distance, Halo Time, notch) at scale 2–3.\n862\t- No 3×5 `tiny` font for information.\n863\t- A 5% safe margin from the edges.\n864\t- Information is never in colour alone: aspects also show as lamp *positions* plus a letter.\n865\t- At most 6 HUD groups at once: the Halo ring, the station line, the top-right cluster, the stop gauge, the centre banner (judgments and toasts),\n866\t  and the subtitle box.\n867\t\n868\t**Cab** (screen space; the bottom 30% of r.H; scales to any width):\n869\t- Windshield frame: pillars at the sides, a top header with a sun visor, wipers (rain), faint glass reflections at night.\n870\t- **Dashboard, left to right:**\n871\t  1. **timetable card** (station / ARR / DEP, next stop highlighted, PASS rows) with the **pocket watch** clipped beside it;\n872\t  2. **speedometer** (analog 0–120, needle smoothing, with a big digital readout under it and the ATS lamps: white \"ATS\" power, red \"ATS\");\n873\t  3. **notch display**: a big readout (\"P3\", \"N\", \"B5\", \"EB\") plus a 15-cell strip with the current cell lit, power blue and brake orange;\n874\t  4. **BC/MR pressure gauge**;\n875\t  5. **pilot lamp** (orange), door side arrows, SLIP and WSP lamps, the comfort face;\n876\t  6. **buttons**: ATS confirm, horn, EB (big and round; they depress when used; clickable).\n877\t- The **master controller**: Halo 8000 has one T-handle; Halo 3000 has two handles, brake and power, the brake one moving first. It is drawn in\n878\t  perspective bottom-left; a **white-gloved hand** rests on it and follows its tween (80 ms per notch).\n879\t- Hover with the mouse highlights a widget (focus); click operates it; drag the handle to change notches.\n880\t\n881\t**HUD:**\n882\t- **Top-left: Halo Time ring.** A gold halo of 30 px, filling clockwise as a share of 99, with the seconds inside and \"HALO\" under it.\n883\t- **Top-centre: next station.** Line 1: `NEXT  HANABATAKE` (scale 2). Line 2: `1.25 km · ARR 06:42:05` (or `PASS`).\n884\t- **Top-right:**\n885\t  - the limit in force as a white plate with the number;\n886\t  - the next limit, e.g. \"▸70 in 320 m\";\n887\t  - the next sighted signal as a mini 4-lamp head, its letter and distance;\n888\t  - the clock.\n889\t- **Stop Gauge** (600 m → 0, above the dash, centred):\n890\t  - a horizontal bar with the stop marker and our train's nose, and a big \"123.4 m\" readout;\n891\t  - within 30 m it zooms to a ±2 m scale with 10 cm ticks;\n892\t  - with **Braking guide** (assist) on: an amber \"BRAKE\" zone where B4 must start, `d = v²/(2·aB4) + v·τ`, and a ghost marker at the predicted\n893\t    stop point.\n894\t- **Judgments:** centre screen, `E.font.title` scale 3, colour-coded (PERFECT gold, GREAT cyan, GOOD green, OK white, SHORT orange, OVERRUN red,\n895\t  ON TIME gold, LATE red, SMOOTH pink), held 1.2 s, then a smaller list of the Halo Time changes.\n896\t- **Toasts** (top-centre under the station line): \"HORN +1\", \"CALL GREAT\", \"LIMIT!\", \"ATS!\".\n897\t- **Subtitles:** a box above the dash with the speaker's name in their colour and a portrait for story characters (§4.18).\n898\t\n899\t**Tests:**\n900\t- The HUD state builder (pure function) for given states (approach at 500 m with the guide on, ATS ringing, rain, night).\n901\t- Widget hit boxes map clicks to actions.\n902\t- Element count ≤ 6.\n903\t- Layout at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690 (upright) keeps everything on screen and out of `game.cornerArea()`.\n904\t\n905\t### 4.15 Input, devices, glyphs, touch-only (`00-core.js`, `05-glyphs.js`)\n906\t\n907\t**Input map** (custom preset):\n908\t```js\n909\tconst KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {\n910\t  power: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'Pad7', 'Pad12', 'PadAxisUp'],  brake: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'Pad6', 'Pad13', 'PadAxisDown'],\n911\t  neutral: ['KeyN', 'Pad5'],  eb: ['Backspace', 'KeyX', 'Pad4'],  call: ['KeyE', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0'],  horn: ['Space', 'Pad2'],\n912\t  ats: ['KeyQ', 'Pad3'],  view: ['KeyC', 'Pad1'] });\n913\tfor (const k of ['attack', 'dash', 'skill', 'jump']) delete KEYS[k];\n914\t```\n915\t- The mouse wheel is handled by our own `wheel` listener (browser only), which turns it into `input.tap('power' | 'brake')`.\n916\t- Set the drive scene's `camera: { zoom: false }` so the engine doesn't take the wheel.\n917\t- `look` isn't an action. Read `input.padAim` (the right stick) and `input.pan()` (a right or middle drag).\n918\t\n919\t**Prompt badges** (`DH.Glyph.draw(g, action, x, y, scale)`):\n920\t- The device comes from `input.lastDevice`, and the badge family from the setting (§4.25), not the controller's name.\n921\t- **PlayStation:** a round badge with ✕ (blue), ○ (red), □ (pink), △ (green); pill badges for L1, R1, L2, R2; a D-pad icon with an arrow.\n922\t- **Xbox:** A (green), B (red), X (blue), Y (yellow); LB, RB, LT, RT pills.\n923\t- **Keyboard:** a keycap with the key label from `input.label(a, 'keyboard')` (\"W\", \"SPACE\", \"BKSP\").\n924\t- **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, or the wheel with an arrow.\n925\t- Prompts change on the step the device changes. The title, menus, results and attract all use them.\n926\t\n927\t**Rumble** (`input.rumble`): rail joints .05/.03 s; EB .8/.4 s; slip .3 buzz; obstruction alarm pulses; PERFECT a short .2 tick.\n928\t\n929\t**Touch-only devices:**\n930\t- Detect `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`, with no gamepad and no key seen.\n931\t- Re-check on `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`.\n932\t- When touch-only:\n933\t  - no touch controls (don't set `touch:` on scenes; `game.input.touchEnabled = false`);\n934\t  - the title replaces its menu with a panel \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a big **SETTINGS** button (`game.menu('settings')`), and the\n935\t    engine's corner menu button stays;\n936\t  - the **attract mode still runs** after 10 s, and a tap returns to the title.\n937\t\n938\t**Tests:**\n939\t- Glyph mapping: PlayStation `call` → ✕; Xbox `call` → A; keyboard `call` → E; mouse `call` → LMB.\n940\t- Lever direction flips W/S but not R2/L2.\n941\t- Wheel taps.\n942\t- Touch-only (stub matchMedia and navigator): the title shows the message; Start can't be reached by taps; attract starts after 10 s; a tap exits it.\n943\t\n944\t### 4.16 Sound: soundscape, sfx, melodies, music (`40-sound.js`)\n945\t\n946\tAll sound is made in code. Use the engine's `game.audio` (sfx, `define`, music, `sting`) for one-shots and songs, and a small **continuous\n947\tsynth** on `audio.ctx` routed through `audio.bus('sfx')` (§3.8) for engine sounds. Build its node graph once; change only parameters each frame.\n948\tIn headless mode every call does nothing.\n949\t\n950\t**Continuous layers** (parameters from speed, notch and zone):"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"4c9fe884-b330-4140-9318-3845fe7d88af","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:32.596Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","content":"  - late beyond the window: **LATE +n** (−1 s for each second past the window; the multiplier is a slider);\n  - early beyond the window: **EARLY** (no bonus, 100 pts).\n- **Smooth stop:** if the deceleration at the moment of stopping is ≤ 1.0 km/h/s and EB wasn't used in the last 100 m: **SMOOTH!** (+2 s, 300 pts).\n  Teach it in S0 (\"ease to B1 just before you stop\").\n- Passing a stopping station without stopping (the front more than 50 m past the target while moving): −10 s, \"MISSED STOP\", and the stage\n  continues to the next stop. If it was the last stop, the stage ends there and goes to results.\n- Stopping at a **pass** station within 50 m: \"WRONG STOP\" (−5 s); the next `power` continues.\n\n**Dwell and departure** (every beat has a sound; timings for Normal):\n1. t+1.0: doors open (chime \"pin-pon\" ×2; side lamp L/R on the dash). Passengers alight and board (§4.10).\n2. The departure melody (the station's own, §5.8) starts at `max(dep − 9 s, t + 6 s)` and lasts 6–8 s.\n3. Then the \"doors closing\" announcement (Mika, gibberish) and the door chime; the doors close (thunk).\n4. +1.0 s: the conductor's buzzer (two short buzzes). +1.5 s: the **pilot lamp** lights (all doors closed).\n5. The starting signal clears at `max(dep − 3 s, pilot lamp)` if the block allows. A **departure call** opens: \"Shuppatsu, shinko!\" (§4.7).\n6. Power is accepted once the pilot lamp is lit. Pressing power earlier gives a short \"DOORS\" buzz and no penalty.\n- If the train arrived late, the dwell shrinks to at least 12 s, so the train can make time back.\n- Events can add time: the **door rush** in S4 re-opens the doors for 8 s.\n\n**Stop Cam** (setting, on by default):\n- After each judgment, an inset slides in (or full screen on Easy) for 2.5 s; any button skips it.\n- It is a **2D side elevation**: the platform edge, the stop marker post with its number, the train's front car in profile easing to its final\n  position, a dimension line (\"+0.12 m\"), and the door queue marks lining up with the doors.\n- Haro-kun pops up on PERFECT.\n\n**Tests:**\n- The judgment table at err = 0.05, 0.25, 0.6, 0.95, −2, +3 and +7 (back-up then OK).\n- Re-stop counting.\n- ON TIME, LATE and EARLY at clock offsets −3, 0, +5.\n- SMOOTH when B1 is applied at < 3 km/h; no SMOOTH when B8 is held to the stop.\n- Dwell order: doors, melody, close, buzzer, pilot, signal, call.\n- Power before the pilot lamp gives no movement.\n- A missed stop and a wrong stop each apply their penalty.\n\n### 4.7 Point and call (*shisa kanko*) and the horn (`33-rules.js`)\n\n**Call points:** every signal (once sighted), limit board, station approach board, pass board, crossing (optional, no penalty) and the departure.\n\n**Window.**\n- For things on the line, the window opens 180 m ahead and closes 30 m ahead.\n- The **GREAT** sweet spot is 120–60 m ahead.\n- The departure call's window runs from \"signal clear\" until 5 s after the doors close; it counts as GREAT if made before the first power notch.\n- On screen: a thin white **bracket** around the target (via `rail.project`), with a prompt `[✕] CALL` (glyph per device, §4.15).\n\n**Pressing `call`:**\n- The **white-gloved hand** rises from the bottom of the screen and points at the target's screen position (raise 0.15 s, hold 0.4 s, back 0.25 s).\n- The driver's voice says the phrase in gibberish (§4.17), with a subtitle in romaji and English.\n- Score: GREAT +0.5 s and 100 pts; OK 50 pts. Missing a call costs nothing in Halo Time; Hoshino remarks once per stage.\n- **Auto calls** (assist setting) makes the calls for you at the sweet spot, with no score.\n- With the mouse, clicking the bracketed thing (`rail.pick`) or anywhere in the view while one call is open does the same.\n\n**Phrases** (aspect-aware; subtitles shown):\n\n| Target | Romaji | English subtitle |\n|---|---|---|\n| starting signal | Shuppatsu, shinko! | Departure. Proceed! |\n| block G | Heisoku, shinko! | Block. Proceed! |\n| home G | Jonai, shinko! | Home signal. Proceed! |\n| YG | Gensoku! | Reduce speed! |\n| Y | Chui! | Caution! |\n| YY | Keikai! | Restricted! |\n| R | Teishi! | Stop! |\n| limit board | Seigen, (number)! | Limit (number)! |\n| stop approach | Teisha! | Stopping! |\n| pass board | Tsuka! | Passing! |\n| crossing | Fumikiri, yoshi! | Crossing, clear! |\n\n**Horn.**\n- The horn board starts a 150 m **horn zone**. Holding `horn` for at least 0.5 s inside it gives \"HORN +1\" (+1 s, 100 pts).\n- Leaving the zone without sounding it costs −1 s (Normal/Expert) or nothing (Easy).\n- The horn always plays when held (§4.16). Track workers (§4.11) wave when they hear it.\n\n**Tests:** a window opens and closes at the right distances; GREAT vs OK by distance; a call works only while its target is sighted; the departure call\ncounts as GREAT before power; the auto-calls setting gives no points; a horn hold of 0.6 s in the zone scores; 0.3 s doesn't; a missed zone costs −1 s\non Normal and 0 on Easy.\n\n### 4.8 Timetable and clock (`32-stations.js`)\n\n- Each stage has `startClock` and `stops: [{ id, arr, dep }]`, plus `passes: [{ id, at }]` for display. Show times as `HH:MM:SS`.\n- The **timetable card** on the dash lists each stop's ARR and DEP, highlights the next stop, and marks passes as \"PASS\".\n- The **pocket watch** shows the clock, with a ticking second hand.\n- **Calibration** (`node tools/calibrate.mjs`): run the autopilot in **min-time mode** (§4.19, \"par\") over every stage with default tuning and\n  print the arrival clocks. Set each timetable so par arrives **6–12 s early** on Normal: a good human can then be on time, and the\n  timetable-aware autopilot has room to arrive on the second. The S4 door rush and the S2 freight wait are part of the par run.\n- The §5.3 numbers are starting values; replace them with calibrated ones and commit them as data.\n\n**Tests:** the clock advances 1:1 with game time and stops while paused; calibrated par arrives early by 6–12 s at every stop (a guard against tuning\ndrift); the timetable card highlights the right row.\n\n### 4.9 Halo Time, score, ranks (`33-rules.js`)\n\n**Halo Time** is the life: a pool of seconds.\n\n| Difficulty | Start | Stop windows | Penalties |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Easy | 40 s | ×1.5 | half |\n| Normal | 25 s | ×1.0 | as listed |\n| Expert | 15 s | ×0.7 | ×1.5 |\n\n- The cap is 99 s.\n- Changes apply at events (arrival, stop, penalty, bonus), each with a floating \"+3 s\" or \"−2 s\" by the ring.\n- At ≤ 10 s the ring pulses red and a clock tick plays.\n- At **0**, game over at once: the screen desaturates, the halo ring cracks (particles), and Hoshino says \"That's enough. Hand me the key.\"\n- S0 shows the ring but freezes it.\n\n**Bonuses and penalties not already covered:**\n- obstruction avoided (stopped before the crossing): **HAZARD AVOIDED** +5 s, 800 pts;\n- EB with no hazard: −3 s, −200 pts;\n- rollback over 1 m: −2 s.\n\n**Score:** the points in the tables above, plus remaining Halo Time × 50 and comfort × 5 at the end of the stage.\n\n**Checkpoints.**\n- At every departure (the moment the pilot lamp lights), take a snapshot: s, clock, Halo Time, score, comfort, the events fired, the other train\n  actors' state, and the dwell phase.\n- The pause menu's **Retry from last station** restores it as it was.\n- After a game over, **Continue from last station** restores it with Halo Time set back to the stage's start value and the score halved.\n- **Retry stage** starts the stage over.\n\n**Ranks:** let **Max** = the sum of every scoring chance in the stage (each stop PERFECT, each arrival on time, each SMOOTH, every call GREAT, every\nhorn, the hazard bonus, Halo Time end value = start + bonuses, comfort 100).\n- **S** ≥ 90% of Max;\n- **A** ≥ 75%;\n- **B** ≥ 55%;\n- **C** ≥ 35%;\n- otherwise **D**.\n\nThe ranks show as a red *hanko* stamp on the results screen.\n\n**Tests:** game over fires at 0; Halo Time is capped at 99; Max computes for S1 with no NaN; a perfect autopilot run ranks S; a run with three\noverruns ranks C or lower; difficulty multipliers apply.\n\n### 4.10 Passengers, crowds and comfort (`33-rules.js`, `art-people.js`)\n\n- **Door queues.** For the stage's train length, compute the door positions back from the stop target (4 doors per 20 m car, at 2.6 / 7.4 /\n  12.6 / 17.4 m from the car front).\n- Each door mark gets 0–3 queued Humanoids (the station's `crowd` level times the stage's time-of-day factor), from a seeded stream.\n- Mix of looks: commuters (suit and coat, briefcase drawn at `rig.hand()`), students (uniforms), elders, kids, festival visitors (yukata colours,\n  S3), and umbrellas in rain.\n- **Animation:**\n  - idle breathing (built in);\n  - heads turn toward the arriving train (facing);\n  - kids `pose: 'wave'` at the cab;\n  - when the doors open, alighting passengers spawn at the doors and walk to the stairs, and the queued ones walk in and vanish;\n  - at departure, the platform staff do `pose: 'point'`, then `'flag'`;\n  - at the S5 finale, everyone `'bow'`s.\n- **Levels of detail:** full rigs within 60 m; beyond that, cached 2-tone silhouette sprites.\n- **Comfort** starts at 100.\n  - Penalties: a change of ≥ 3 notches within 0.4 s while moving (−2); EB (−15); slip or slide (−3); more than 5 km/h over a curve limit (−5/s);\n    a stop jolt with final decel > 2.0 km/h/s (−8); rollback (−5).\n  - It recovers +1 every 8 s of smooth running.\n  - A passenger face icon on the dash has 4 moods. Mika gives a line at < 60.\n\n**Tests:** queue counts per crowd level; door positions match the train length and stop target; boarding empties the queues before the doors close;\ncomfort penalties and recovery; the LOD switches at 60 m.\n\n### 4.11 Events (`34-events.js`)\n\nData-driven per stage: `{ id, type, at: s | clock | trigger, params }`. Each fires once and is logged in `snapshot().events`.\n\n| type | behaviour |\n|---|---|\n| `oncoming` | a train actor spawns on the opposite track 1,400 m ahead, heading toward us at 80 km/h; passing gives a whoosh, a pressure jolt (shake) and a toot |\n| `preceding` | a train actor ahead on our track with a legs script (S2 freight); it holds blocks |\n| `rival` | a same-direction express on the parallel track (S4), scripted relative to our progress: alongside, then pulling ahead, stopping at Koen beside us |\n| `crossing` | gates and bells: warning when our ETA < 25 s or within 500 m; gates down in 4 s; up after our rear clears. Waiting kei cars, bikes and pedestrians |\n| `obstruction` | a crossing blocked (S3: the festival's takoyaki cart). The special emitter 350 m before flashes red; stop before the crossing. It clears 12 s after we stop |\n| `workers` | slow order 25 km/h; a lookout rig with a flag and whistle; the crew steps aside and waves on the horn (bows if we're under the limit) |\n| `doorRush` | at a station: Kenji runs, Mika re-opens the doors (+8 s dwell) |\n| `festival` | crowd ×2, lantern strings, taiko drum loop near the station |\n| `fog` | fog density from s to s, plus sighting distance 180 m |\n| `glare` | sun-glare bloom when the track heading points at the low sun; the visor drops |\n| `radio` | a story line (§4.18) |\n| `tip` | a first-time tutorial tip (once per save, unless \"Show tips\" is on) |\n| `flash` | the oncoming last train flashes its headlights; answer with the horn within 4 s for +2 s (S5) |\n\n**Tests:** each event fires once in its stage under the autopilot playthrough; crossing gates are down while our train is on the crossing; the obstruction\nclears only after we stop; the horn makes workers wave; door rush adds 8 s to that dwell.\n\n### 4.12 Time of day, weather and light (`40-sound.js` for audio, `rail.js` for the image)\n\n**Time of day** is a float hour from the stage (or Free Run). It picks one of these grades, blended linearly between neighbours:\n\n| grade | hours | sky (top → horizon) | ambient | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| dawn | 5–7 | #2a2850 → #f0a080 | .70 warm | low sun east, long shadows (darker ground bands) |\n| day | 8–15 | #3a78d8 → #bfe0ff | 1.0 | |\n| afternoon haze | 14–16 | #5a8ad0 → #e8e0c8 | .95 | |\n| sunset | 17–18.5 | #3a2a6a → #ff9a4a | .75 orange | big low sun on the backdrop; glare zones |\n| night | 19.5–4.5 | #060818 → #1a2440 | .30 blue | stars, moon, city glow; lit windows; headlights; platform lights stay bright |\n\n- The backdrop is an `E.Backdrop` with layers per zone (mountains, hills, city, sea, clouds). Its `turn` scrolls with the curve × distance travelled.\n- **Rain** (S2, Free Run):\n  - darker grade (−20%); streaks and windshield droplets that build up;\n  - wipers sweep every 1.2 s in the cab;\n  - wet rails; puddles on platforms (bright blots);\n  - umbrellas on the crowds;\n  - rain hiss in the audio, and lower adhesion (§4.3).\n- **Fog:** distance fog, larger signal glows, short sighting. Hoshino: \"When you can't see, the signals see for you.\"\n- **Glare:** an additive white-orange bloom over the upper half, scaled by `cos(heading − sunHeading)`. The sun visor animates down.\n\n**Tests:** the grade blend is continuous at every boundary hour; rain sets the adhesion; fog sets sighting to 180 m.\n\n### 4.13 Cameras and the director (`60-drive.js`, `61-attract.js`)\n\n| camera | placement | use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `cab` | s_front − 1.5, x = track, h 2.9, horizon .40 | gameplay (default) |\n| `cabMin` | the same, without the dashboard | \"Minimal\" cab display setting; duel viewports |\n| `chase` | behind the rear by 25 m, h 9, x +2, fov 70 | View button; attract |\n| `chaseHigh` | rear − 40 m, h 22, x +6 | quad-track shots, the hub card |\n| `platform` | on the platform 15 m past the stop target, h 1.7, `dir: -1` | View button at stations; attract; the S5 finale |\n| `trackside` | 4 m beside the line at a scripted s ahead, h 2, `dir: -1`; holds while the train passes, then cuts | attract, ending |\n| `stopCam` | 2D side view (§4.6) | after every stop |\n\n- `view` cycles through cab → chase → platform (platform only near a station) → cab.\n- **Cab feel:**\n  - lateral sway ∝ curve × v²;\n  - 1-px vertical bumps at rail joints (every 25 m, timed with the joint sound);\n  - the view pitches 2–3 px forward under braking and back under power (`pitchPx` follows the smoothed acceleration);\n  - a jolt on EB.\n  - The \"Camera sway\" setting scales these; reduce motion turns them off.\n- **Look:** the right stick or a right-drag shifts `lookX` up to ±25% of W and `lookY` ±10% of H, springing back. It's a glance, not a free camera.\n- **Director** (attract, ending): a list of shots `{ cam, dur, at }`, cut with 0.3 s dissolves.\n\n**Tests:** each camera projects our train's front to the expected screen side; `view` cycles only through the cameras that apply; the director runs\nits shots in order.\n\n### 4.14 Cab, HUD, stop gauge (`50-cab.js`, `51-hud.js`)\n\n**10-foot rules:**\n- Nothing smaller than the 5×7 font at scale 1. Key numbers (speed, stop distance, Halo Time, notch) at scale 2–3.\n- No 3×5 `tiny` font for information.\n- A 5% safe margin from the edges.\n- Information is never in colour alone: aspects also show as lamp *positions* plus a letter.\n- At most 6 HUD groups at once: the Halo ring, the station line, the top-right cluster, the stop gauge, the centre banner (judgments and toasts),\n  and the subtitle box.\n\n**Cab** (screen space; the bottom 30% of r.H; scales to any width):\n- Windshield frame: pillars at the sides, a top header with a sun visor, wipers (rain), faint glass reflections at night.\n- **Dashboard, left to right:**\n  1. **timetable card** (station / ARR / DEP, next stop highlighted, PASS rows) with the **pocket watch** clipped beside it;\n  2. **speedometer** (analog 0–120, needle smoothing, with a big digital readout under it and the ATS lamps: white \"ATS\" power, red \"ATS\");\n  3. **notch display**: a big readout (\"P3\", \"N\", \"B5\", \"EB\") plus a 15-cell strip with the current cell lit, power blue and brake orange;\n  4. **BC/MR pressure gauge**;\n  5. **pilot lamp** (orange), door side arrows, SLIP and WSP lamps, the comfort face;\n  6. **buttons**: ATS confirm, horn, EB (big and round; they depress when used; clickable).\n- The **master controller**: Halo 8000 has one T-handle; Halo 3000 has two handles, brake and power, the brake one moving first. It is drawn in\n  perspective bottom-left; a **white-gloved hand** rests on it and follows its tween (80 ms per notch).\n- Hover with the mouse highlights a widget (focus); click operates it; drag the handle to change notches.\n\n**HUD:**\n- **Top-left: Halo Time ring.** A gold halo of 30 px, filling clockwise as a share of 99, with the seconds inside and \"HALO\" under it.\n- **Top-centre: next station.** Line 1: `NEXT  HANABATAKE` (scale 2). Line 2: `1.25 km · ARR 06:42:05` (or `PASS`).\n- **Top-right:**\n  - the limit in force as a white plate with the number;\n  - the next limit, e.g. \"▸70 in 320 m\";\n  - the next sighted signal as a mini 4-lamp head, its letter and distance;\n  - the clock.\n- **Stop Gauge** (600 m → 0, above the dash, centred):\n  - a horizontal bar with the stop marker and our train's nose, and a big \"123.4 m\" readout;\n  - within 30 m it zooms to a ±2 m scale with 10 cm ticks;\n  - with **Braking guide** (assist) on: an amber \"BRAKE\" zone where B4 must start, `d = v²/(2·aB4) + v·τ`, and a ghost marker at the predicted\n    stop point.\n- **Judgments:** centre screen, `E.font.title` scale 3, colour-coded (PERFECT gold, GREAT cyan, GOOD green, OK white, SHORT orange, OVERRUN red,\n  ON TIME gold, LATE red, SMOOTH pink), held 1.2 s, then a smaller list of the Halo Time changes.\n- **Toasts** (top-centre under the station line): \"HORN +1\", \"CALL GREAT\", \"LIMIT!\", \"ATS!\".\n- **Subtitles:** a box above the dash with the speaker's name in their colour and a portrait for story characters (§4.18).\n\n**Tests:**\n- The HUD state builder (pure function) for given states (approach at 500 m with the guide on, ATS ringing, rain, night).\n- Widget hit boxes map clicks to actions.\n- Element count ≤ 6.\n- Layout at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690 (upright) keeps everything on screen and out of `game.cornerArea()`.\n\n### 4.15 Input, devices, glyphs, touch-only (`00-core.js`, `05-glyphs.js`)\n\n**Input map** (custom preset):\n```js\nconst KEYS = Object.assign({}, E.Input.DEFAULT, {\n  power: ['KeyW', 'ArrowUp', 'Pad7', 'Pad12', 'PadAxisUp'],  brake: ['KeyS', 'ArrowDown', 'Pad6', 'Pad13', 'PadAxisDown'],\n  neutral: ['KeyN', 'Pad5'],  eb: ['Backspace', 'KeyX', 'Pad4'],  call: ['KeyE', 'Mouse0', 'Pad0'],  horn: ['Space', 'Pad2'],\n  ats: ['KeyQ', 'Pad3'],  view: ['KeyC', 'Pad1'] });\nfor (const k of ['attack', 'dash', 'skill', 'jump']) delete KEYS[k];\n```\n- The mouse wheel is handled by our own `wheel` listener (browser only), which turns it into `input.tap('power' | 'brake')`.\n- Set the drive scene's `camera: { zoom: false }` so the engine doesn't take the wheel.\n- `look` isn't an action. Read `input.padAim` (the right stick) and `input.pan()` (a right or middle drag).\n\n**Prompt badges** (`DH.Glyph.draw(g, action, x, y, scale)`):\n- The device comes from `input.lastDevice`, and the badge family from the setting (§4.25), not the controller's name.\n- **PlayStation:** a round badge with ✕ (blue), ○ (red), □ (pink), △ (green); pill badges for L1, R1, L2, R2; a D-pad icon with an arrow.\n- **Xbox:** A (green), B (red), X (blue), Y (yellow); LB, RB, LT, RT pills.\n- **Keyboard:** a keycap with the key label from `input.label(a, 'keyboard')` (\"W\", \"SPACE\", \"BKSP\").\n- **Mouse:** a mouse icon with the left or right button lit, or the wheel with an arrow.\n- Prompts change on the step the device changes. The title, menus, results and attract all use them.\n\n**Rumble** (`input.rumble`): rail joints .05/.03 s; EB .8/.4 s; slip .3 buzz; obstruction alarm pulses; PERFECT a short .2 tick.\n\n**Touch-only devices:**\n- Detect `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`, with no gamepad and no key seen.\n- Re-check on `gamepadconnected` and `keydown`.\n- When touch-only:\n  - no touch controls (don't set `touch:` on scenes; `game.input.touchEnabled = false`);\n  - the title replaces its menu with a panel \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a big **SETTINGS** button (`game.menu('settings')`), and the\n    engine's corner menu button stays;\n  - the **attract mode still runs** after 10 s, and a tap returns to the title.\n\n**Tests:**\n- Glyph mapping: PlayStation `call` → ✕; Xbox `call` → A; keyboard `call` → E; mouse `call` → LMB.\n- Lever direction flips W/S but not R2/L2.\n- Wheel taps.\n- Touch-only (stub matchMedia and navigator): the title shows the message; Start can't be reached by taps; attract starts after 10 s; a tap exits it.\n\n### 4.16 Sound: soundscape, sfx, melodies, music (`40-sound.js`)\n\nAll sound is made in code. Use the engine's `game.audio` (sfx, `define`, music, `sting`) for one-shots and songs, and a small **continuous\nsynth** on `audio.ctx` routed through `audio.bus('sfx')` (§3.8) for engine sounds. Build its node graph once; change only parameters each frame.\nIn headless mode every call does nothing.\n\n**Continuous layers** (parameters from speed, notch and zone):","numLines":320,"startLine":631,"totalLines":1952}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Q2RCJcqFoRyhw4rtG27dMZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
