{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghrZkRnHAwk6HzT4GC61","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XxCB7v8KqYTbTYWjbvGm48","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","offset":1581,"limit":371},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10795,"cache_read_input_tokens":110318,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10795},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"13587f4a-6b94-4c06-ad65-877c0252ba41","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:39.337Z","request_id":"req_011CghrZk5SRi8qh7YYvgg5U","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01XxCB7v8KqYTbTYWjbvGm48":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","offset":1581,"limit":371}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XxCB7v8KqYTbTYWjbvGm48","type":"tool_result","content":"1581\t| **Kenji** | salaryman, always late | suit, tie, briefcase | kenji | the S4 door rush |\n1582\t| **Ren Kurosawa** | Sky Liner driver | purple uniform, sunglasses | ren | cocky radio, friendly at the end |\n1583\t| **Sato** | old driver on the inner loop | like Hoshino, older | sato | the S5 headlight flash |\n1584\t| crowds, staff, workers | — | seeded outfits by zone; staff in navy with white gloves; workers in orange vests and helmets, the lookout with a flag | crowd | §4.10 |\n1585\t\n1586\t### 5.5 Writing style guide and script\n1587\t\n1588\t**Style** (after the source's tone):\n1589\t- Short, earnest sentences in railway vocabulary.\n1590\t- Announcements are formal, say the station name twice, and repeat formulas (\"The doors on the left side will open.\").\n1591\t- Calls are romaji with English subtitles.\n1592\t- The instructor is terse and proverbial and hides his warmth; the conductor is cheerful.\n1593\t- Arcade banners are 1–3 words in capitals.\n1594\t- Light comedy comes from Japanese railway earnestness, never from mocking it.\n1595\t- No line wraps past 3 rows at 320 px.\n1596\t\n1597\t**Banners:** PERFECT HALO! · GREAT! · GOOD · OK · SHORT · OVERRUN! · OVER THE MARK · ON TIME! · LATE +4 · EARLY · SMOOTH! · CALL! · HORN +1 · LIMIT! ·\n1598\tATS! · EMERGENCY · HAZARD AVOIDED · MISSED STOP · WRONG STOP · SIGNAL PASSED AT DANGER.\n1599\t\n1600\t**Announcer (pa):**\n1601\t- \"Thank you for riding the Halo Line. This is a local train for Kujira-bashi.\"\n1602\t- \"The next station is Hanabatake. Hanabatake. The doors on the left side will open.\"\n1603\t- \"This is a Rapid service. We will not stop at Todai-shita.\"\n1604\t- \"Okiku-dera. Okiku-dera. Festival visitors, please mind the gap.\"\n1605\t- \"The next station is Akari Central, the last stop. Thank you for riding the Halo Line today.\"\n1606\t\n1607\t**S0 (Hoshino):**\n1608\t- \"So. You're the new one.\"\n1609\t- \"Thirty-eight years I drove this ring. Not one second late. Today is my last day.\"\n1610\t- \"Your paperwork says you can drive. The rails don't read paperwork.\"\n1611\t- Steps:\n1612\t  1. \"Lever up: power. P1. Gently.\"\n1613\t  2. \"N. Let her coast.\"\n1614\t  3. \"Lever down: brake. Watch the needle, not your hand.\"\n1615\t  4. \"The yellow board. Put your nose on it.\"\n1616\t  5. \"Before you move, point at the signal and say it. Shuppatsu, shinko.\"\n1617\t  6. \"Forty is the limit. Forty is not a suggestion.\"\n1618\t  7. \"Near the stop, ease off to B1. The passengers' coffee will thank you.\"\n1619\t  8. \"That is the emergency brake. You will hate the day you need it.\"\n1620\t  9. \"Last mark. Inside one metre and I sign your card.\"\n1621\t- The pass: \"...Hmph. Signed. The 06:40 is waiting, Rookie.\"\n1622\t\n1623\t**S1:**\n1624\t- Mika: \"Good morning, Driver! Conductor Aoba, at the back. Call me Mika!\"\n1625\t- Hoshino: \"Arrive on the second. Early is not on time. Late is not on time.\"\n1626\t- Mika: \"Melody, doors, my buzzer, then she's all yours!\"\n1627\t- Kota (on the platform): \"Haro! Haro! Mister driver!\" Mika: \"Our biggest fan is here again.\"\n1628\t- Hoshino (at the horn board): \"The fishermen under the bridge like to know we're coming.\"\n1629\t\n1630\t**S2:**\n1631\t- Hoshino: \"Today, the old 3000. She's older than you. Her brakes think slowly. Ask early.\"\n1632\t- Mika: \"Umbrellas everywhere! The rails will be slippery!\"\n1633\t- Dispatcher: \"Halo 1015, freight ahead of you through the cliff tunnel. Expect restrictive signals.\"\n1634\t- Hoshino at Y: \"Yellow. Forty-five. Don't argue with yellow.\"\n1635\t- Mika on slip: \"Wheels spinning! Ease the power!\"\n1636\t- Hoshino passing: \"Tsuka. Good. Eyes forward.\"\n1637\t\n1638\t**S3:**\n1639\t- Mika: \"It's the Chrysanthemum Festival!\"\n1640\t- Hoshino: \"Up the hill it's easy to be slow. Down it, easy to be fast.\"\n1641\t- Hoshino at the workers: \"Horn. Now.\"\n1642\t- Mika at the obstruction: \"The crossing alarm! Something's on the tracks!\"\n1643\t- Afterwards: \"The takoyaki survived. So did we.\"\n1644\t- Fuji: \"Thank you, driver-san!\"\n1645\t\n1646\t**S4:**\n1647\t- Hoshino: \"Ten cars. Your marker is the 10, not the 6.\"\n1648\t- Ren: \"Evening, Halo Line! See you at Koen... if you can keep up!\"\n1649\t- Hoshino: \"Ignore him. He has his timetable. We have ours.\"\n1650\t- Kenji: \"Wait! Wait for me!\"\n1651\t- Mika: \"One moment please! Doors re-opening!\"\n1652\t- Hoshino: \"Eight seconds. Earn them back. Without speeding.\"\n1653\t- Hoshino in the glare: \"West sun. Squint if you must. Don't guess.\"\n1654\t\n1655\t**S5:**\n1656\t- Mika: \"Last train of the night... and Hoshino-san's last run.\"\n1657\t- Hoshino: \"Don't make it a ceremony. Drive it like any other train.\"\n1658\t- Hoshino in the fog: \"When you can't see, the signals see for you.\"\n1659\t- Hoshino at Sato's flash: \"That's Sato. Thirty years. He's flashing for me... Answer him.\"\n1660\t- **Finale:**\n1661\t  - Ota: \"Hoshino-san. Thirty-eight years. The whole station is here.\"\n1662\t  - Hoshino: \"...And the last stop was yours, Rookie.\"\n1663\t  - Hoshino: \"Here. My watch. It runs two seconds fast. I never told anyone.\"\n1664\t  - Hoshino: \"A loop has no last station. Every ending is the next departure.\"\n1665\t  - Mika: \"Welcome to the Halo Line, Driver!\"\n1666\t  - Haro-kun: \"Harooo!\"\n1667\t\n1668\t**Results** (Hoshino, by rank; one line per stage per rank, varied):\n1669\t\n1670\t| rank | S1 line |\n1671\t|---|---|\n1672\t| S | \"...Exactly there. Exactly then.\" |\n1673\t| A | \"Good. Now do it four hundred more times.\" |\n1674\t| B | \"Acceptable. Your brakes are nervous.\" |\n1675\t| C | \"You drove. That is all I will say.\" |\n1676\t| D | \"We will talk about this at the depot.\" |\n1677\t\n1678\t- Game over: \"That's enough. Hand me the key.\"\n1679\t- Write the other stages' lines in the same voice.\n1680\t\n1681\t### 5.6 Art assets\n1682\t\n1683\tAll art follows the engine's native look: PS1-era pixel art drawn with `E.px` primitives and `E.font`, shaded with `E.tones` (`deep`, `sh`, `base`,\n1684\t`lt`, `hi`), lit by palette per time of day. Every world drawer has 3 levels of detail by k (§4.1).\n1685\t\n1686\t**New (made for this game; these make it feel like the source):**\n1687\t\n1688\t| Group | Items | Technique |\n1689\t|---|---|---|\n1690\t| Trains | Halo 8000 / 3000 / Sky Liner / freight loco plus containers; body pattern (windows, doors, stripe, skirt, bogies, pantograph); front faces (headlights, tail lights, LED destination, cab windows, driver silhouette) | strips plus a front-face thing |\n1691\t| Cab | 8000 modern panel, 3000 classic panel, windshield frame, visor, wipers, glass droplets, master controllers, the gloved hand (4 poses: on handle, point, press button, horn), pocket watch, timetable card | screen overlay |\n1692\t| Line-side | 4-lamp signal (lit states, glow), shunting signal, ATS beacon, limit and END boards, horn board, stop approach and PASS boards, stop markers \"6\" / \"10\", km posts, slow-order boards, special emitter, catenary masts (lattice, concrete), wires | things / per-segment lines |\n1693\t| Stations | platform slab patterns, canopy, columns, name boards, vending machines, bins, clock, LED board, stairs, fence, door marks, Haro-kun poster | strips plus things |\n1694\t| Town (parametric) | `building({ w, d, h, floors, roof: 'flat'|'gable'|'hip', facade: 'office'|'apartment'|'house'|'shop'|'konbini'|'warehouse'|'machiya', palette, lit })` drawn as boxes (front, side, roof), windows lit at night | strips |\n1695\t| Landmarks | terminal clock tower, greenhouses, whale-arch bridge, gantry cranes, container stacks, ferry, lighthouse (beam at night), pagoda, temple gate, torii, observatory dome, clock tower, Ferris wheel (turning), sakura trees | things |\n1696\t| Crossings | road band, crossbuck, flashing lamps, gate arm (tweened), kei cars, bikes | band plus things |\n1697\t| People | outfits per §4.10 and §5.4; briefcase, umbrella, flag, camera attachments at `rig.hand()` | Humanoid rigs |\n1698\t| UI | logo plus halo ring, halo map, glyph badges, Halo Time ring, notch display, stop gauge, judgment titles, hanko rank stamps, postcards, weather and time icons, DEMO badge | overlay |\n1699\t\n1700\t**From the engine's library** (generic props; bake once at boot with `E.shapeSprite(id, { view, facing, size })` into things):\n1701\t- `tree`, `pine`, `bush`, `rock`, `fence`, `bench`, `lamppost`, `crate`, `barrel`, `sign`;\n1702\t- `kenney-fantasy-town:hedge*`, `kenney-pirate:boat-row-large`, `kenney-holiday:bench` (load their sets per `src/assets/README.md`);\n1703\t- `E.prop('tree' | 'pine' | 'bush' | 'flowers' | 'grass' | 'lamp' | 'streetlamp' | 'trashcan' | 'hydrant').frames[0]`.\n1704\t\n1705\tSearch for more with `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"<words>\"`, and cut what you use into a set of your own (`tools/asset-set.mjs`) so the bundle\n1706\tstays lean.\n1707\t\n1708\t### 5.7 Animations (new, on top of the engine's rigs)\n1709\t\n1710\t- **Gloved hand:** rides the handle (an 80 ms tween per notch), point-and-call (raise, point at the target, return), press ATS, press the horn.\n1711\t- **Controls:** handle tweens, needles (a critically damped spring), lamp blinks, wiper sweeps, droplets that gather and are wiped away, the visor\n1712\t  dropping.\n1713\t- **Train:** doors sliding (exterior and platform cam), the pantograph bobbing with sparks at night, the S5 headlight flash, the back-up crawl.\n1714\t- **World:** crossing gates and lamps, the obstruction emitter flashing, the lighthouse beam turning, the Ferris wheel turning, falling sakura petals,\n1715\t  seagulls flapping, festival lanterns swaying.\n1716\t- **People:** idle and gait (built in); `wave` (kids); `cheer` (festival, the duel winner); new `bow`, `point` and `flag` poses (§3.8 #6) for staff,\n1717\t  workers and the finale; boarding and alighting walks; umbrellas.\n1718\t- **Haro-kun:** squash-and-stretch bounce, the halo spinning and glowing, wing flaps, a hop on PERFECT, waving on the title.\n1719\t- **UI:** the stamp slam, the halo ring cracking (particles), the postcard flip, the halo map's selection glow travelling the ring, the title train\n1720\t  arriving.\n1721\t- Check every rig animation with `node tools/filmstrip.mjs index.html#gallery --steps \"...\" --seed 1`.\n1722\t\n1723\t### 5.8 Audio assets\n1724\t\n1725\tEverything in §4.16 and §4.17. Departure melodies, one per station:\n1726\t\n1727\t| station | melody id | character |\n1728\t|---|---|---|\n1729\t| akari | `akari` | stately and bright: a 4-bar bell fanfare |\n1730\t| hanabatake | `hanabatake` | skipping, pastoral |\n1731\t| kujira | `kujira` | rolling, wave-like |\n1732\t| minato | `minato` | jaunty, ends with a ship-horn note |\n1733\t| todai | `todai` | airy (used when stopping in Free Run) |\n1734\t| okiku | `okiku` | pentatonic, koto-like |\n1735\t| yuhi | `yuhi` | warm, falling |\n1736\t| shotengai | `shotengai` | busy, syncopated |\n1737\t| daigaku | `daigaku` | a school-bell quote |\n1738\t| koen | `koen` | light and lilting |\n1739\t| hoshimi | `hoshimi` | twinkling and high |\n1740\t| kawasemi | `kawasemi` | quick, bird-like |\n1741\t\n1742\t---\n1743\t\n1744\t## 6. Build order and milestones\n1745\t\n1746\tEach milestone ends **playable** and **green** (`node test/run.mjs`). Don't start the next until the acceptance checks pass. Cut nothing silently.\n1747\tIf time runs short, use the cut order in §7, removing whole features (never stubbing them).\n1748\t\n1749\t| # | Milestone | Build | Acceptance |\n1750\t|---|---|---|---|\n1751\t| **M0** | Setup | the dev folder; engine copied; `index.html` (session.js first); `src/00-core.js`, a minimal title scene (the logo text and a START item; it grows into the real title in M7); `test/run.mjs` with lib.mjs loading engine plus src in a vm; `tools/bundle.mjs`; ENGINE-CHANGES.md | page opens from file:// with no errors; `node test/run.mjs` prints `N passed, 0 failed`; bundle builds and opens |\n1752\t| **M1** | **Rail renderer** | `rail.js`: segments, square-pixel projection, ground, ballast, sleepers, rails, catenary, strips (platform, canopy, wall, overpass, tunnel, bridge truss), things (signal, boards, masts, trees via shapeSprite), crest clipping, tint, fog, rear view, `project`, `pick`; a small test route; `#gallery` | the §4.1 tests; the 13 gallery screenshots reviewed (§8.5): rails converge cleanly, platforms read as raised, nothing squashed on 16:9 |\n1753\t| **M2** | Train and cab core | `30-train.js`; lever and input map; the cab overlay (speedo, notch, gauges, handle, glove); the HUD skeleton; camera sway; the continuous sound (VVVF, rolling, joints, brakes); one station stop with judgment and Stop Cam on the test route | §4.3 check values; drive and stop by keyboard and pad; judgment shown; sounds respond to v and notch |\n1754\t| **M3** | Rules: full S1 | route compiler and the S1 route; signals and blocks; ATS; limits; stations with dwell and departure sequence; timetable and clock; calls and horn; Halo Time, score, ranks; comfort; briefing and results; minimal title → S1 → results flow; story data for S1; first Babble voices | S1 playable end to end by a human; §4.4–4.10 tests |\n1755\t| **M4** | Autopilot and Dev Mode | `35-autopilot.js`; `DH.proof`; Dev Mode (tools, sliders, Reset, debug overlay, sound board); engine changes §3.8 #3; `tools/calibrate.mjs` | the autopilot ranks S on S1; slider tests; the proof hash is stable; Dev Mode works by pad and mouse |\n1756\t| **M5** | Multiplayer | PR stage and the quad-track route section; train actors as ghosts; `63-hub.js` with our card; `64-duel.js`; hub harness script | mock-hub tests; **harness passes** with 2p, with 4p plus latency, jitter and loss, and with host-leave; the duel finishes with two autopilots |\n1757\t| **M6** | Career content | the whole halo route and depot; zones and scenery sets; stations and landmarks; S0–S5 data, events, rival, freight, crossings, workers, obstruction, fog, glare, rain, night; crowds and characters; all story lines; postcards; calibrated timetables | **full playthrough test** (autopilot skill 1 and 0.6 over S0–S5 and PR) passes; every event fires; gallery shots per zone reviewed |\n1758\t| **M7** | Front end and attract | boot steps and splash; title (touch-only branch); halo map; Free Run; Duel lobby; Driver's Log; How to Drive; About & Credits; game over; ending and credits; **attract mode**; glyph badges everywhere; settings additions | §4.15, 4.20, 4.22, 4.25, 4.26 tests; attract loop screenshots; every screen at 1080p and 720p readable at 10 ft (§8.5) |\n1759\t| **M8** | Audio and polish | the full soundscape, 12 melodies, all music, all Babble voices; rumble; animation polish (filmstrips); a balance pass with the sliders (a human drives each stage on Normal: an S is hard but possible, a C is easy); accessibility review | sound board audition; node-leak test; no look notes left from `tools/check.mjs` that matter |\n1760\t| **M9** | Ship | final bundle; About/Credits text; exit-interview.json; the full suite on the **bundle** (headless plus browser plus harness) | §9 checklist all ticked |\n1761\t\n1762\t**First playable target (end of M3):** S1 driven by a human, start to finish, with real feedback. That's where the fun gets proven or fixed. Tune the\n1763\tphysics and judgment windows there before any content work.\n1764\t\n1765\t---\n1766\t\n1767\t## 7. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1768\t\n1769\t| Risk | Why it's risky | De-risk |\n1770\t|---|---|---|\n1771\t| **Rail renderer quality** | It is the whole look; pseudo-3D strips (platforms, trains, buildings) and the rear view are new code | Build it first (M1) on a test route; projection unit tests; the gallery with screenshots before anything else; keep strip faces simple (flat colour plus pattern), add detail later |\n1772\t| **Stopping feel and precision** | If precise stops feel random, the game fails | The lag model plus the stop gauge's fine scale plus the braking guide; MPC autopilot as proof it can be done; tune in M3 with telemetry (debug overlay); sliders for lag, windows, brake scale; record human sessions (`?record=1`) and replay them |\n1773\t| **Babble and VVVF synthesis** | They can sound harsh or leak WebAudio nodes | Fixed node graph for the continuous layers; a capped node pool for syllables; the sound board for auditions; master limiter (a DynamicsCompressor on our bus); the leak test |\n1774\t| **Hub compliance** | Easy to miss a rule (ready without input, one result, stopping on end) | Copy `examples/ghost.html`'s flow; mock-hub tests in M5; run the real harness early, and again on the final bundle |\n1775\t| **Content volume** | 12 stations, 13 zones, 6 stages | Data-driven scripts; a parametric building generator; seeded scenery per zone; hand-draw only the landmarks; stations from one kit |\n1776\t| **Determinism** | Tests and hub copies must agree | `DH.rng` streams only; fixed steps; proof hash twice; no wall clock |\n1777\t| **10-foot legibility** | 320×240 pixels on a TV | Font-size rules (§4.14); screenshots at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 viewed scaled down; at most 6 HUD groups |\n1778\t| **Engine edits** | Could break standard features | Minimal patches (§3.8); rerun the handoff tests; log them |\n1779\t| **Disk and /tmp** | /tmp was full on this machine | `TMPDIR` inside the dev folder; clean `_src` clones after copying |\n1780\t| **Time** | A big scope | Milestone gates; the cut order below |\n1781\t\n1782\t**Cut order if time runs out** (each cut removes the feature cleanly, including its menu entries and tests):\n1783\t1. postcards 7–8;\n1784\t2. the Halo 3000 cab art (it keeps its physics and sound and uses the 8000 cab);\n1785\t3. the `trackside` camera;\n1786\t4. the Stats page in the Log;\n1787\t5. Free Run's train choice.\n1788\t\n1789\t**Never cut:** any TASK.md requirement (dev mode with sliders, attract, the hub, the 10-foot UI and glyphs, touch-only handling, About/Credits, the exit\n1790\tinterview) or any stage S0–S5 or PR.\n1791\t\n1792\t---\n1793\t\n1794\t## 8. Testing\n1795\t\n1796\t### 8.1 One command\n1797\t\n1798\t`node test/run.mjs` (from the dev folder; also `npm test`).\n1799\t- It runs every headless suite and prints **only failures**, each as `FAIL <suite>: <what> (<detail>)`, then **one line**: `57 passed, 0 failed`.\n1800\t- Exit code 1 on any failure.\n1801\t- `node test/run.mjs physics stations` runs a subset. Rerun only the suites that cover the code you changed, and the whole run before each milestone\n1802\t  gate.\n1803\t- `--browser` adds the Playwright checks (§8.3). `--hub` adds the harness (§8.4). Both need `TMPDIR` set (§0).\n1804\t\n1805\t`test/lib.mjs`:\n1806\t- creates a vm context with stand-ins: `window`, `addEventListener`, `removeEventListener`, `location {hash, search}`,\n1807\t  `navigator {maxTouchPoints, getGamepads}`, `matchMedia`, and optionally a mock `AIHub` (§4.23);\n1808\t- loads `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `engine/modules/rail.js`, then `src/*.js` in order;\n1809\t- returns `{ E, DH, game, I }`.\n1810\t- Each test drives the game with `game.advance()`, `I.tap/hold/press/feed`, and `game.debug`.\n1811\t\n1812\t### 8.2 Headless suites (what each asserts)\n1813\t\n1814\t| suite | asserts (see each §'s Tests) |\n1815\t|---|---|\n1816\t| `rail` | projection, square pixels, rear mirror, crest clipping, strip faces, project/pick (§4.1) |\n1817\t| `route` | compiling, segments, curves, grades, spans, validation good and bad (§4.2) |\n1818\t| `physics` | check values, lever rules, slip, determinism (§4.3) |\n1819\t| `signals` | aspects, starting signals, ATS ring/confirm/EB, SPAD, S2 freight (§4.4) |\n1820\t| `limits` | tolerance, once per section, rear clears (§4.5) |\n1821\t| `stations` | judgment table, re-stops, on time/late/early, smooth, dwell order, pilot interlock, missed and wrong stops (§4.6) |\n1822\t| `calls` | windows, GREAT vs OK, sighting, departure call, auto calls, horn zone (§4.7) |\n1823\t| `timetable` | clock, pause, card, calibrated par early by 6–12 s (§4.8) |\n1824\t| `score` | Halo Time cap and 0, Max, ranks, difficulty (§4.9) |\n1825\t| `crowds` | queues, doors, boarding, comfort, LOD (§4.10) |\n1826\t| `events` | each event fires once and does its effect (§4.11) |\n1827\t| `tod` | grade continuity, rain adhesion, fog sighting (§4.12) |\n1828\t| `cameras` | placements, the view cycle, the director (§4.13) |\n1829\t| `hud` | state builder, hit boxes, element count, layouts at 3 sizes (§4.14) |\n1830\t| `input` | glyph families, lever flip, wheel, touch-only (§4.15) |\n1831\t| `sound` | parameter function, names exist (§4.16) |\n1832\t| `babble` | determinism, contour, voices, Dialog hook (§4.17) |\n1833\t| `story` | briefings and results lines exist, queueing, tips once, wrap length (§4.18) |\n1834\t| `autopilot` | skill 1 → S on every stage; skill 0.6 → no game over; proof stable (§4.19) |\n1835\t| `attract` | 10 s trigger, order, loop, every exit input, no records, touch-only (§4.20) |\n1836\t| `dev` | sliders live, Reset, hidden from Settings and from sessions, tools (§4.21) |\n1837\t| `progress` | postcards, store round-trip, unlock order (§4.22) |\n1838\t| `hub` | the mock-hub contract (§4.23) |\n1839\t| `duel` | two players, viewports, winner and tie (§4.24) |\n1840\t| `settings` | each setting applies and persists (§4.25) |\n1841\t| `screens` | every screen's open, close and routes; About has every label (§4.26) |\n1842\t| **`playthrough`** | **the scripted whole game:** boot → title → (wait 10 s → attract → input → title) → Career S0…S5 via the menus, driven by the autopilot at skill 1 → results after each → the ending and credits → title; then PR via Duel (2 autopilots) and via the mock hub; asserts no errors (`game.errors` empty), S0 passed, every service stage (S1–S5) rank S, every postcard 1–8 unlocked, every event id fired, and the same proof hash when run twice |\n1843\t\n1844\t### 8.3 Browser checks (`--browser`; Playwright 1.63.0, file:// first)\n1845\t\n1846\t1. `node engine/tools/check.mjs <bundle.html>`, plus `#gallery`, `#stage=S2`, `#attract`: no errors; act on its look notes (fill, colour count,\n1847\t   contrast).\n1848\t2. A Playwright script on the bundle:\n1849\t   - the page loads; the title is drawn; after 10.5 s of no input, attract is on (`__dh.snapshot().attract.active`); a key press returns to the\n1850\t     title;\n1851\t   - keyboard drive: Enter, then CAREER, then S1 via the menus; hold W for 3 s and the speed is above 0;\n1852\t   - mouse: hover over the title menu moves the focus, and a click picks;\n1853\t   - **touch emulation** (`hasTouch`, `isMobile`, with no keyboard events sent): the message and the Settings button show; attract still runs.\n1854\t3. Audio node count after 60 s of autopilot driving stays bounded.\n1855\t\n1856\t### 8.4 AI Hub harness (`--hub`)\n1857\t\n1858\t- Copy `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and the bundle (as `game.html`) into `test/.serve/`.\n1859\t- Start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory test/.serve`, read the port from stdout, and confirm that `GET /game.html` is our\n1860\t  page (its `<title>`).\n1861\t- In Playwright, open in turn:\n1862\t  1. `harness.html?game=game.html&players=2&auto=1`;\n1863\t  2. `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`;\n1864\t  3. the same with `&scenario=host-leave`.\n1865\t- Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and assert `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. On failure, print the failed checklist items.\n1866\t- Stop the server **by its PID**.\n1867\t- Also open the bundle outside the harness to confirm the normal game still runs (the hub's own `hub-check` does this too).\n1868\t\n1869\t### 8.5 Screenshots (only for what scripts can't judge)\n1870\t\n1871\tSave them to `shots/` at 1920×1080 and 1280×720:\n1872\t- the 13 gallery shots;\n1873\t- each zone at its stage's time of day;\n1874\t- a station dwell with the crowd boarding;\n1875\t- the crossing with gates down;\n1876\t- the obstruction;\n1877\t- rain wipers;\n1878\t- fog;\n1879\t- the rival alongside;\n1880\t- the S5 finale bow;\n1881\t- the title, the halo map, a briefing, results with the stamp, game over;\n1882\t- Driver's Log;\n1883\t- About;\n1884\t- the attract DEMO overlay;\n1885\t- the duel split screen;\n1886\t- the hub card (from the harness).\n1887\t\n1888\tJudge each: does it read from 10 feet (view at 25% size)? Does it look like a PS1-era pixel game, not an Atari one? Is any colour flat or muddy?\n1889\t\n1890\tFilmstrips: the glove's point-and-call, the staff bow and flag, the stamp slam, the crossing gate.\n1891\t\n1892\t---\n1893\t\n1894\t## 9. Ship checklist\n1895\t\n1896\t1. `node tools/bundle.mjs` writes `../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model>-<ts>.html`:\n1897\t   - `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then the **unmodified** `session.js` `<script>` as the first script;\n1898\t   - then the hub meta tags (§4.23), the viewport and the `<title>Densha de Halo!</title>`;\n1899\t   - the engine's boot splash markup at the very start of `<body>`: copy the template's `#e3-boot` div with the title changed, or generate the\n1900\t     string `E.bootSplash('DENSHA DE HALO!')` returns at build time in Node;\n1901\t   - the canvas;\n1902\t   - then inline scripts: the engine (patched), rail.js, `src/*.js`.\n1903\t   - No harness files, no external URLs, no `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`.\n1904\t2. `node test/run.mjs --browser --hub` passes **on the bundle**: 0 failed.\n1905\t3. Search **your** code (`src/`, `rail.js`, your engine patches) for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `lorem` and `stub`: nothing found. The engine's own\n1906\t   source may use some of these words legitimately.\n1907\t4. Every TASK.md item is present (the checklist below).\n1908\t5. **About & Credits** pages filled with: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan = `PLAN.md`, Genre and Perspective, Tech with versions, Art\n1909\t   Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage), Testing Feedback (4 answers), Engine Feedback (6 answers),\n1910\t   Prompt Feedback (4 answers). Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Put the same text in the chat reply.\n1911\t6. `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML, in exactly TASK.md's shape. Check that it parses (`node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`).\n1912\t   Suggested `aiHub` values, to adjust to what you built:\n1913\t   - name: \"Densha de Halo!\"\n1914\t   - tagline: \"Stop on the mark. Leave on the second.\"\n1915\t   - description: \"Drive the Halo Line, a loop railway around Akari City, from the first train at dawn to the last train at night: stop exactly\n1916\t     on the mark, keep the timetable to the second, and point and call every signal. Race up to three friends on the four-track Parallel Run.\"\n1917\t   - type: \"Game\"; genre: \"Simulation\"; source: \"Densha de Go!\"; series: \"Densha de Go!\"\n1918\t   - players: \"1–4\" (solo, local duel, sessions of 2–4)\n1919\t   - controls: \"Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△ · EB Backspace/L1\"\n1920\t   - tags: [\"first-person\", \"vehicles\", \"arcade\", \"retro\", \"versus\", \"story\"]\n1921\t7. Source Information defaults to start from (make them true):\n1922\t   - Genre: \"Train driving simulation with arcade scoring\".\n1923\t   - Perspective: \"First-person cab view (pseudo-3D), with chase, platform and trackside cameras, a side-view stop cam and a ring map\".\n1924\t   - Tech: \"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) + custom rail.js renderer; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; vanilla JS; Node + Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\".\n1925\t   - Art: \"the engine's PS1-era pixel style; new code-drawn trains, cab, signals, stations and Japanese townscape; library props via E.shapeSprite\".\n1926\t   - Animation: \"engine Humanoid/Blob rigs with new bow/point/flag poses; code-tweened cab controls, gloved hand, doors, gates\".\n1927\t   - Audio: \"all procedural WebAudio: VVVF inverter, rail joints, brakes, horn, crossing bells, 12 departure melodies, chip music, Babble gibberish voices\".\n1928\t   - Online: \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 2–4 players\".\n1929\t   - Research: what you did, e.g. \"read the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol; no web research\", or what you looked up.\n1930\t8. Delete scratch clones (`_src/`) and `test/.serve/`, and stop any servers you started.\n1931\t\n1932\t**TASK.md coverage checklist** (tick each in M9):\n1933\t- [ ] title by RNG (above)\n1934\t- [ ] genre and perspective chosen (§1)\n1935\t- [ ] single HTML plus dev folder (§3.2, §9)\n1936\t- [ ] built on the handoff engine, changes logged (§3.8)\n1937\t- [ ] the engine's style plus new source-flavoured art (§5.6)\n1938\t- [ ] the engine's animation plus new animations (§5.7)\n1939\t- [ ] all audio in code, gibberish voices (§4.16, §4.17)\n1940\t- [ ] AI Hub ghost sessions plus the game on its own plus local duel (§4.23, §4.24)\n1941\t- [ ] feature-complete demo that shows every mechanic (§5.3: each stage's \"Teaches\")\n1942\t- [ ] dev mode from the pause menu with live sliders and Reset (§4.21)\n1943\t- [ ] attract mode after 10 s; any input exits (§4.20)\n1944\t- [ ] source-like narrative tone (§5.5)\n1945\t- [ ] 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; mouse hover and click in menus and the world; prompts follow the last device;\n1946\t      PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; no dense screens (§2, §4.14, §4.15, §4.25, §4.26)\n1947\t- [ ] touch-only message, Settings button, attract still runs (§4.15)\n1948\t- [ ] no TODOs, placeholders or stubs (§9.3)\n1949\t- [ ] About/Credits plus the chat reply with Source Information and the three feedback sets (§9.5)\n1950\t- [ ] exit-interview.json valid (§9.6)\n1951\t- [ ] tests behind one command printing failures plus a one-line count, including the full scripted playthrough (§8)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"a4ab6d84-d7fe-4354-a06f-6bef5f98d4e9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T12:03:39.473Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/PLAN.md","content":"| **Kenji** | salaryman, always late | suit, tie, briefcase | kenji | the S4 door rush |\n| **Ren Kurosawa** | Sky Liner driver | purple uniform, sunglasses | ren | cocky radio, friendly at the end |\n| **Sato** | old driver on the inner loop | like Hoshino, older | sato | the S5 headlight flash |\n| crowds, staff, workers | — | seeded outfits by zone; staff in navy with white gloves; workers in orange vests and helmets, the lookout with a flag | crowd | §4.10 |\n\n### 5.5 Writing style guide and script\n\n**Style** (after the source's tone):\n- Short, earnest sentences in railway vocabulary.\n- Announcements are formal, say the station name twice, and repeat formulas (\"The doors on the left side will open.\").\n- Calls are romaji with English subtitles.\n- The instructor is terse and proverbial and hides his warmth; the conductor is cheerful.\n- Arcade banners are 1–3 words in capitals.\n- Light comedy comes from Japanese railway earnestness, never from mocking it.\n- No line wraps past 3 rows at 320 px.\n\n**Banners:** PERFECT HALO! · GREAT! · GOOD · OK · SHORT · OVERRUN! · OVER THE MARK · ON TIME! · LATE +4 · EARLY · SMOOTH! · CALL! · HORN +1 · LIMIT! ·\nATS! · EMERGENCY · HAZARD AVOIDED · MISSED STOP · WRONG STOP · SIGNAL PASSED AT DANGER.\n\n**Announcer (pa):**\n- \"Thank you for riding the Halo Line. This is a local train for Kujira-bashi.\"\n- \"The next station is Hanabatake. Hanabatake. The doors on the left side will open.\"\n- \"This is a Rapid service. We will not stop at Todai-shita.\"\n- \"Okiku-dera. Okiku-dera. Festival visitors, please mind the gap.\"\n- \"The next station is Akari Central, the last stop. Thank you for riding the Halo Line today.\"\n\n**S0 (Hoshino):**\n- \"So. You're the new one.\"\n- \"Thirty-eight years I drove this ring. Not one second late. Today is my last day.\"\n- \"Your paperwork says you can drive. The rails don't read paperwork.\"\n- Steps:\n  1. \"Lever up: power. P1. Gently.\"\n  2. \"N. Let her coast.\"\n  3. \"Lever down: brake. Watch the needle, not your hand.\"\n  4. \"The yellow board. Put your nose on it.\"\n  5. \"Before you move, point at the signal and say it. Shuppatsu, shinko.\"\n  6. \"Forty is the limit. Forty is not a suggestion.\"\n  7. \"Near the stop, ease off to B1. The passengers' coffee will thank you.\"\n  8. \"That is the emergency brake. You will hate the day you need it.\"\n  9. \"Last mark. Inside one metre and I sign your card.\"\n- The pass: \"...Hmph. Signed. The 06:40 is waiting, Rookie.\"\n\n**S1:**\n- Mika: \"Good morning, Driver! Conductor Aoba, at the back. Call me Mika!\"\n- Hoshino: \"Arrive on the second. Early is not on time. Late is not on time.\"\n- Mika: \"Melody, doors, my buzzer, then she's all yours!\"\n- Kota (on the platform): \"Haro! Haro! Mister driver!\" Mika: \"Our biggest fan is here again.\"\n- Hoshino (at the horn board): \"The fishermen under the bridge like to know we're coming.\"\n\n**S2:**\n- Hoshino: \"Today, the old 3000. She's older than you. Her brakes think slowly. Ask early.\"\n- Mika: \"Umbrellas everywhere! The rails will be slippery!\"\n- Dispatcher: \"Halo 1015, freight ahead of you through the cliff tunnel. Expect restrictive signals.\"\n- Hoshino at Y: \"Yellow. Forty-five. Don't argue with yellow.\"\n- Mika on slip: \"Wheels spinning! Ease the power!\"\n- Hoshino passing: \"Tsuka. Good. Eyes forward.\"\n\n**S3:**\n- Mika: \"It's the Chrysanthemum Festival!\"\n- Hoshino: \"Up the hill it's easy to be slow. Down it, easy to be fast.\"\n- Hoshino at the workers: \"Horn. Now.\"\n- Mika at the obstruction: \"The crossing alarm! Something's on the tracks!\"\n- Afterwards: \"The takoyaki survived. So did we.\"\n- Fuji: \"Thank you, driver-san!\"\n\n**S4:**\n- Hoshino: \"Ten cars. Your marker is the 10, not the 6.\"\n- Ren: \"Evening, Halo Line! See you at Koen... if you can keep up!\"\n- Hoshino: \"Ignore him. He has his timetable. We have ours.\"\n- Kenji: \"Wait! Wait for me!\"\n- Mika: \"One moment please! Doors re-opening!\"\n- Hoshino: \"Eight seconds. Earn them back. Without speeding.\"\n- Hoshino in the glare: \"West sun. Squint if you must. Don't guess.\"\n\n**S5:**\n- Mika: \"Last train of the night... and Hoshino-san's last run.\"\n- Hoshino: \"Don't make it a ceremony. Drive it like any other train.\"\n- Hoshino in the fog: \"When you can't see, the signals see for you.\"\n- Hoshino at Sato's flash: \"That's Sato. Thirty years. He's flashing for me... Answer him.\"\n- **Finale:**\n  - Ota: \"Hoshino-san. Thirty-eight years. The whole station is here.\"\n  - Hoshino: \"...And the last stop was yours, Rookie.\"\n  - Hoshino: \"Here. My watch. It runs two seconds fast. I never told anyone.\"\n  - Hoshino: \"A loop has no last station. Every ending is the next departure.\"\n  - Mika: \"Welcome to the Halo Line, Driver!\"\n  - Haro-kun: \"Harooo!\"\n\n**Results** (Hoshino, by rank; one line per stage per rank, varied):\n\n| rank | S1 line |\n|---|---|\n| S | \"...Exactly there. Exactly then.\" |\n| A | \"Good. Now do it four hundred more times.\" |\n| B | \"Acceptable. Your brakes are nervous.\" |\n| C | \"You drove. That is all I will say.\" |\n| D | \"We will talk about this at the depot.\" |\n\n- Game over: \"That's enough. Hand me the key.\"\n- Write the other stages' lines in the same voice.\n\n### 5.6 Art assets\n\nAll art follows the engine's native look: PS1-era pixel art drawn with `E.px` primitives and `E.font`, shaded with `E.tones` (`deep`, `sh`, `base`,\n`lt`, `hi`), lit by palette per time of day. Every world drawer has 3 levels of detail by k (§4.1).\n\n**New (made for this game; these make it feel like the source):**\n\n| Group | Items | Technique |\n|---|---|---|\n| Trains | Halo 8000 / 3000 / Sky Liner / freight loco plus containers; body pattern (windows, doors, stripe, skirt, bogies, pantograph); front faces (headlights, tail lights, LED destination, cab windows, driver silhouette) | strips plus a front-face thing |\n| Cab | 8000 modern panel, 3000 classic panel, windshield frame, visor, wipers, glass droplets, master controllers, the gloved hand (4 poses: on handle, point, press button, horn), pocket watch, timetable card | screen overlay |\n| Line-side | 4-lamp signal (lit states, glow), shunting signal, ATS beacon, limit and END boards, horn board, stop approach and PASS boards, stop markers \"6\" / \"10\", km posts, slow-order boards, special emitter, catenary masts (lattice, concrete), wires | things / per-segment lines |\n| Stations | platform slab patterns, canopy, columns, name boards, vending machines, bins, clock, LED board, stairs, fence, door marks, Haro-kun poster | strips plus things |\n| Town (parametric) | `building({ w, d, h, floors, roof: 'flat'|'gable'|'hip', facade: 'office'|'apartment'|'house'|'shop'|'konbini'|'warehouse'|'machiya', palette, lit })` drawn as boxes (front, side, roof), windows lit at night | strips |\n| Landmarks | terminal clock tower, greenhouses, whale-arch bridge, gantry cranes, container stacks, ferry, lighthouse (beam at night), pagoda, temple gate, torii, observatory dome, clock tower, Ferris wheel (turning), sakura trees | things |\n| Crossings | road band, crossbuck, flashing lamps, gate arm (tweened), kei cars, bikes | band plus things |\n| People | outfits per §4.10 and §5.4; briefcase, umbrella, flag, camera attachments at `rig.hand()` | Humanoid rigs |\n| UI | logo plus halo ring, halo map, glyph badges, Halo Time ring, notch display, stop gauge, judgment titles, hanko rank stamps, postcards, weather and time icons, DEMO badge | overlay |\n\n**From the engine's library** (generic props; bake once at boot with `E.shapeSprite(id, { view, facing, size })` into things):\n- `tree`, `pine`, `bush`, `rock`, `fence`, `bench`, `lamppost`, `crate`, `barrel`, `sign`;\n- `kenney-fantasy-town:hedge*`, `kenney-pirate:boat-row-large`, `kenney-holiday:bench` (load their sets per `src/assets/README.md`);\n- `E.prop('tree' | 'pine' | 'bush' | 'flowers' | 'grass' | 'lamp' | 'streetlamp' | 'trashcan' | 'hydrant').frames[0]`.\n\nSearch for more with `node tools/asset-find.mjs \"<words>\"`, and cut what you use into a set of your own (`tools/asset-set.mjs`) so the bundle\nstays lean.\n\n### 5.7 Animations (new, on top of the engine's rigs)\n\n- **Gloved hand:** rides the handle (an 80 ms tween per notch), point-and-call (raise, point at the target, return), press ATS, press the horn.\n- **Controls:** handle tweens, needles (a critically damped spring), lamp blinks, wiper sweeps, droplets that gather and are wiped away, the visor\n  dropping.\n- **Train:** doors sliding (exterior and platform cam), the pantograph bobbing with sparks at night, the S5 headlight flash, the back-up crawl.\n- **World:** crossing gates and lamps, the obstruction emitter flashing, the lighthouse beam turning, the Ferris wheel turning, falling sakura petals,\n  seagulls flapping, festival lanterns swaying.\n- **People:** idle and gait (built in); `wave` (kids); `cheer` (festival, the duel winner); new `bow`, `point` and `flag` poses (§3.8 #6) for staff,\n  workers and the finale; boarding and alighting walks; umbrellas.\n- **Haro-kun:** squash-and-stretch bounce, the halo spinning and glowing, wing flaps, a hop on PERFECT, waving on the title.\n- **UI:** the stamp slam, the halo ring cracking (particles), the postcard flip, the halo map's selection glow travelling the ring, the title train\n  arriving.\n- Check every rig animation with `node tools/filmstrip.mjs index.html#gallery --steps \"...\" --seed 1`.\n\n### 5.8 Audio assets\n\nEverything in §4.16 and §4.17. Departure melodies, one per station:\n\n| station | melody id | character |\n|---|---|---|\n| akari | `akari` | stately and bright: a 4-bar bell fanfare |\n| hanabatake | `hanabatake` | skipping, pastoral |\n| kujira | `kujira` | rolling, wave-like |\n| minato | `minato` | jaunty, ends with a ship-horn note |\n| todai | `todai` | airy (used when stopping in Free Run) |\n| okiku | `okiku` | pentatonic, koto-like |\n| yuhi | `yuhi` | warm, falling |\n| shotengai | `shotengai` | busy, syncopated |\n| daigaku | `daigaku` | a school-bell quote |\n| koen | `koen` | light and lilting |\n| hoshimi | `hoshimi` | twinkling and high |\n| kawasemi | `kawasemi` | quick, bird-like |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Build order and milestones\n\nEach milestone ends **playable** and **green** (`node test/run.mjs`). Don't start the next until the acceptance checks pass. Cut nothing silently.\nIf time runs short, use the cut order in §7, removing whole features (never stubbing them).\n\n| # | Milestone | Build | Acceptance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **M0** | Setup | the dev folder; engine copied; `index.html` (session.js first); `src/00-core.js`, a minimal title scene (the logo text and a START item; it grows into the real title in M7); `test/run.mjs` with lib.mjs loading engine plus src in a vm; `tools/bundle.mjs`; ENGINE-CHANGES.md | page opens from file:// with no errors; `node test/run.mjs` prints `N passed, 0 failed`; bundle builds and opens |\n| **M1** | **Rail renderer** | `rail.js`: segments, square-pixel projection, ground, ballast, sleepers, rails, catenary, strips (platform, canopy, wall, overpass, tunnel, bridge truss), things (signal, boards, masts, trees via shapeSprite), crest clipping, tint, fog, rear view, `project`, `pick`; a small test route; `#gallery` | the §4.1 tests; the 13 gallery screenshots reviewed (§8.5): rails converge cleanly, platforms read as raised, nothing squashed on 16:9 |\n| **M2** | Train and cab core | `30-train.js`; lever and input map; the cab overlay (speedo, notch, gauges, handle, glove); the HUD skeleton; camera sway; the continuous sound (VVVF, rolling, joints, brakes); one station stop with judgment and Stop Cam on the test route | §4.3 check values; drive and stop by keyboard and pad; judgment shown; sounds respond to v and notch |\n| **M3** | Rules: full S1 | route compiler and the S1 route; signals and blocks; ATS; limits; stations with dwell and departure sequence; timetable and clock; calls and horn; Halo Time, score, ranks; comfort; briefing and results; minimal title → S1 → results flow; story data for S1; first Babble voices | S1 playable end to end by a human; §4.4–4.10 tests |\n| **M4** | Autopilot and Dev Mode | `35-autopilot.js`; `DH.proof`; Dev Mode (tools, sliders, Reset, debug overlay, sound board); engine changes §3.8 #3; `tools/calibrate.mjs` | the autopilot ranks S on S1; slider tests; the proof hash is stable; Dev Mode works by pad and mouse |\n| **M5** | Multiplayer | PR stage and the quad-track route section; train actors as ghosts; `63-hub.js` with our card; `64-duel.js`; hub harness script | mock-hub tests; **harness passes** with 2p, with 4p plus latency, jitter and loss, and with host-leave; the duel finishes with two autopilots |\n| **M6** | Career content | the whole halo route and depot; zones and scenery sets; stations and landmarks; S0–S5 data, events, rival, freight, crossings, workers, obstruction, fog, glare, rain, night; crowds and characters; all story lines; postcards; calibrated timetables | **full playthrough test** (autopilot skill 1 and 0.6 over S0–S5 and PR) passes; every event fires; gallery shots per zone reviewed |\n| **M7** | Front end and attract | boot steps and splash; title (touch-only branch); halo map; Free Run; Duel lobby; Driver's Log; How to Drive; About & Credits; game over; ending and credits; **attract mode**; glyph badges everywhere; settings additions | §4.15, 4.20, 4.22, 4.25, 4.26 tests; attract loop screenshots; every screen at 1080p and 720p readable at 10 ft (§8.5) |\n| **M8** | Audio and polish | the full soundscape, 12 melodies, all music, all Babble voices; rumble; animation polish (filmstrips); a balance pass with the sliders (a human drives each stage on Normal: an S is hard but possible, a C is easy); accessibility review | sound board audition; node-leak test; no look notes left from `tools/check.mjs` that matter |\n| **M9** | Ship | final bundle; About/Credits text; exit-interview.json; the full suite on the **bundle** (headless plus browser plus harness) | §9 checklist all ticked |\n\n**First playable target (end of M3):** S1 driven by a human, start to finish, with real feedback. That's where the fun gets proven or fixed. Tune the\nphysics and judgment windows there before any content work.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Risks and how to de-risk them\n\n| Risk | Why it's risky | De-risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Rail renderer quality** | It is the whole look; pseudo-3D strips (platforms, trains, buildings) and the rear view are new code | Build it first (M1) on a test route; projection unit tests; the gallery with screenshots before anything else; keep strip faces simple (flat colour plus pattern), add detail later |\n| **Stopping feel and precision** | If precise stops feel random, the game fails | The lag model plus the stop gauge's fine scale plus the braking guide; MPC autopilot as proof it can be done; tune in M3 with telemetry (debug overlay); sliders for lag, windows, brake scale; record human sessions (`?record=1`) and replay them |\n| **Babble and VVVF synthesis** | They can sound harsh or leak WebAudio nodes | Fixed node graph for the continuous layers; a capped node pool for syllables; the sound board for auditions; master limiter (a DynamicsCompressor on our bus); the leak test |\n| **Hub compliance** | Easy to miss a rule (ready without input, one result, stopping on end) | Copy `examples/ghost.html`'s flow; mock-hub tests in M5; run the real harness early, and again on the final bundle |\n| **Content volume** | 12 stations, 13 zones, 6 stages | Data-driven scripts; a parametric building generator; seeded scenery per zone; hand-draw only the landmarks; stations from one kit |\n| **Determinism** | Tests and hub copies must agree | `DH.rng` streams only; fixed steps; proof hash twice; no wall clock |\n| **10-foot legibility** | 320×240 pixels on a TV | Font-size rules (§4.14); screenshots at 1920×1080 and 1280×720 viewed scaled down; at most 6 HUD groups |\n| **Engine edits** | Could break standard features | Minimal patches (§3.8); rerun the handoff tests; log them |\n| **Disk and /tmp** | /tmp was full on this machine | `TMPDIR` inside the dev folder; clean `_src` clones after copying |\n| **Time** | A big scope | Milestone gates; the cut order below |\n\n**Cut order if time runs out** (each cut removes the feature cleanly, including its menu entries and tests):\n1. postcards 7–8;\n2. the Halo 3000 cab art (it keeps its physics and sound and uses the 8000 cab);\n3. the `trackside` camera;\n4. the Stats page in the Log;\n5. Free Run's train choice.\n\n**Never cut:** any TASK.md requirement (dev mode with sliders, attract, the hub, the 10-foot UI and glyphs, touch-only handling, About/Credits, the exit\ninterview) or any stage S0–S5 or PR.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Testing\n\n### 8.1 One command\n\n`node test/run.mjs` (from the dev folder; also `npm test`).\n- It runs every headless suite and prints **only failures**, each as `FAIL <suite>: <what> (<detail>)`, then **one line**: `57 passed, 0 failed`.\n- Exit code 1 on any failure.\n- `node test/run.mjs physics stations` runs a subset. Rerun only the suites that cover the code you changed, and the whole run before each milestone\n  gate.\n- `--browser` adds the Playwright checks (§8.3). `--hub` adds the harness (§8.4). Both need `TMPDIR` set (§0).\n\n`test/lib.mjs`:\n- creates a vm context with stand-ins: `window`, `addEventListener`, `removeEventListener`, `location {hash, search}`,\n  `navigator {maxTouchPoints, getGamepads}`, `matchMedia`, and optionally a mock `AIHub` (§4.23);\n- loads `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `engine/modules/rail.js`, then `src/*.js` in order;\n- returns `{ E, DH, game, I }`.\n- Each test drives the game with `game.advance()`, `I.tap/hold/press/feed`, and `game.debug`.\n\n### 8.2 Headless suites (what each asserts)\n\n| suite | asserts (see each §'s Tests) |\n|---|---|\n| `rail` | projection, square pixels, rear mirror, crest clipping, strip faces, project/pick (§4.1) |\n| `route` | compiling, segments, curves, grades, spans, validation good and bad (§4.2) |\n| `physics` | check values, lever rules, slip, determinism (§4.3) |\n| `signals` | aspects, starting signals, ATS ring/confirm/EB, SPAD, S2 freight (§4.4) |\n| `limits` | tolerance, once per section, rear clears (§4.5) |\n| `stations` | judgment table, re-stops, on time/late/early, smooth, dwell order, pilot interlock, missed and wrong stops (§4.6) |\n| `calls` | windows, GREAT vs OK, sighting, departure call, auto calls, horn zone (§4.7) |\n| `timetable` | clock, pause, card, calibrated par early by 6–12 s (§4.8) |\n| `score` | Halo Time cap and 0, Max, ranks, difficulty (§4.9) |\n| `crowds` | queues, doors, boarding, comfort, LOD (§4.10) |\n| `events` | each event fires once and does its effect (§4.11) |\n| `tod` | grade continuity, rain adhesion, fog sighting (§4.12) |\n| `cameras` | placements, the view cycle, the director (§4.13) |\n| `hud` | state builder, hit boxes, element count, layouts at 3 sizes (§4.14) |\n| `input` | glyph families, lever flip, wheel, touch-only (§4.15) |\n| `sound` | parameter function, names exist (§4.16) |\n| `babble` | determinism, contour, voices, Dialog hook (§4.17) |\n| `story` | briefings and results lines exist, queueing, tips once, wrap length (§4.18) |\n| `autopilot` | skill 1 → S on every stage; skill 0.6 → no game over; proof stable (§4.19) |\n| `attract` | 10 s trigger, order, loop, every exit input, no records, touch-only (§4.20) |\n| `dev` | sliders live, Reset, hidden from Settings and from sessions, tools (§4.21) |\n| `progress` | postcards, store round-trip, unlock order (§4.22) |\n| `hub` | the mock-hub contract (§4.23) |\n| `duel` | two players, viewports, winner and tie (§4.24) |\n| `settings` | each setting applies and persists (§4.25) |\n| `screens` | every screen's open, close and routes; About has every label (§4.26) |\n| **`playthrough`** | **the scripted whole game:** boot → title → (wait 10 s → attract → input → title) → Career S0…S5 via the menus, driven by the autopilot at skill 1 → results after each → the ending and credits → title; then PR via Duel (2 autopilots) and via the mock hub; asserts no errors (`game.errors` empty), S0 passed, every service stage (S1–S5) rank S, every postcard 1–8 unlocked, every event id fired, and the same proof hash when run twice |\n\n### 8.3 Browser checks (`--browser`; Playwright 1.63.0, file:// first)\n\n1. `node engine/tools/check.mjs <bundle.html>`, plus `#gallery`, `#stage=S2`, `#attract`: no errors; act on its look notes (fill, colour count,\n   contrast).\n2. A Playwright script on the bundle:\n   - the page loads; the title is drawn; after 10.5 s of no input, attract is on (`__dh.snapshot().attract.active`); a key press returns to the\n     title;\n   - keyboard drive: Enter, then CAREER, then S1 via the menus; hold W for 3 s and the speed is above 0;\n   - mouse: hover over the title menu moves the focus, and a click picks;\n   - **touch emulation** (`hasTouch`, `isMobile`, with no keyboard events sent): the message and the Settings button show; attract still runs.\n3. Audio node count after 60 s of autopilot driving stays bounded.\n\n### 8.4 AI Hub harness (`--hub`)\n\n- Copy `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` and the bundle (as `game.html`) into `test/.serve/`.\n- Start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1 --directory test/.serve`, read the port from stdout, and confirm that `GET /game.html` is our\n  page (its `<title>`).\n- In Playwright, open in turn:\n  1. `harness.html?game=game.html&players=2&auto=1`;\n  2. `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`;\n  3. the same with `&scenario=host-leave`.\n- Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete === 'true'` and assert `window.__hubCheck.pass === true`. On failure, print the failed checklist items.\n- Stop the server **by its PID**.\n- Also open the bundle outside the harness to confirm the normal game still runs (the hub's own `hub-check` does this too).\n\n### 8.5 Screenshots (only for what scripts can't judge)\n\nSave them to `shots/` at 1920×1080 and 1280×720:\n- the 13 gallery shots;\n- each zone at its stage's time of day;\n- a station dwell with the crowd boarding;\n- the crossing with gates down;\n- the obstruction;\n- rain wipers;\n- fog;\n- the rival alongside;\n- the S5 finale bow;\n- the title, the halo map, a briefing, results with the stamp, game over;\n- Driver's Log;\n- About;\n- the attract DEMO overlay;\n- the duel split screen;\n- the hub card (from the harness).\n\nJudge each: does it read from 10 feet (view at 25% size)? Does it look like a PS1-era pixel game, not an Atari one? Is any colour flat or muddy?\n\nFilmstrips: the glove's point-and-call, the staff bow and flag, the stamp slam, the crossing gate.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Ship checklist\n\n1. `node tools/bundle.mjs` writes `../DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-<model>-<ts>.html`:\n   - `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then the **unmodified** `session.js` `<script>` as the first script;\n   - then the hub meta tags (§4.23), the viewport and the `<title>Densha de Halo!</title>`;\n   - the engine's boot splash markup at the very start of `<body>`: copy the template's `#e3-boot` div with the title changed, or generate the\n     string `E.bootSplash('DENSHA DE HALO!')` returns at build time in Node;\n   - the canvas;\n   - then inline scripts: the engine (patched), rail.js, `src/*.js`.\n   - No harness files, no external URLs, no `alert`/`confirm`/`prompt`.\n2. `node test/run.mjs --browser --hub` passes **on the bundle**: 0 failed.\n3. Search **your** code (`src/`, `rail.js`, your engine patches) for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `placeholder`, `lorem` and `stub`: nothing found. The engine's own\n   source may use some of these words legitimately.\n4. Every TASK.md item is present (the checklist below).\n5. **About & Credits** pages filled with: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan = `PLAN.md`, Genre and Perspective, Tech with versions, Art\n   Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage), Testing Feedback (4 answers), Engine Feedback (6 answers),\n   Prompt Feedback (4 answers). Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm. Put the same text in the chat reply.\n6. `exit-interview.json` beside the HTML, in exactly TASK.md's shape. Check that it parses (`node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`).\n   Suggested `aiHub` values, to adjust to what you built:\n   - name: \"Densha de Halo!\"\n   - tagline: \"Stop on the mark. Leave on the second.\"\n   - description: \"Drive the Halo Line, a loop railway around Akari City, from the first train at dawn to the last train at night: stop exactly\n     on the mark, keep the timetable to the second, and point and call every signal. Race up to three friends on the four-track Parallel Run.\"\n   - type: \"Game\"; genre: \"Simulation\"; source: \"Densha de Go!\"; series: \"Densha de Go!\"\n   - players: \"1–4\" (solo, local duel, sessions of 2–4)\n   - controls: \"Lever W/S or L2/R2 · Call E/✕ · Horn Space/□ · ATS Q/△ · EB Backspace/L1\"\n   - tags: [\"first-person\", \"vehicles\", \"arcade\", \"retro\", \"versus\", \"story\"]\n7. Source Information defaults to start from (make them true):\n   - Genre: \"Train driving simulation with arcade scoring\".\n   - Perspective: \"First-person cab view (pseudo-3D), with chase, platform and trackside cameras, a side-view stop cam and a ring map\".\n   - Tech: \"my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) + custom rail.js renderer; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; vanilla JS; Node + Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\".\n   - Art: \"the engine's PS1-era pixel style; new code-drawn trains, cab, signals, stations and Japanese townscape; library props via E.shapeSprite\".\n   - Animation: \"engine Humanoid/Blob rigs with new bow/point/flag poses; code-tweened cab controls, gloved hand, doors, gates\".\n   - Audio: \"all procedural WebAudio: VVVF inverter, rail joints, brakes, horn, crossing bells, 12 departure melodies, chip music, Babble gibberish voices\".\n   - Online: \"AI Hub Session Protocol v1, ghost mode, 2–4 players\".\n   - Research: what you did, e.g. \"read the engine handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol; no web research\", or what you looked up.\n8. Delete scratch clones (`_src/`) and `test/.serve/`, and stop any servers you started.\n\n**TASK.md coverage checklist** (tick each in M9):\n- [ ] title by RNG (above)\n- [ ] genre and perspective chosen (§1)\n- [ ] single HTML plus dev folder (§3.2, §9)\n- [ ] built on the handoff engine, changes logged (§3.8)\n- [ ] the engine's style plus new source-flavoured art (§5.6)\n- [ ] the engine's animation plus new animations (§5.7)\n- [ ] all audio in code, gibberish voices (§4.16, §4.17)\n- [ ] AI Hub ghost sessions plus the game on its own plus local duel (§4.23, §4.24)\n- [ ] feature-complete demo that shows every mechanic (§5.3: each stage's \"Teaches\")\n- [ ] dev mode from the pause menu with live sliders and Reset (§4.21)\n- [ ] attract mode after 10 s; any input exits (§4.20)\n- [ ] source-like narrative tone (§5.5)\n- [ ] 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; mouse hover and click in menus and the world; prompts follow the last device;\n      PlayStation glyphs with an Xbox option; no dense screens (§2, §4.14, §4.15, §4.25, §4.26)\n- [ ] touch-only message, Settings button, attract still runs (§4.15)\n- [ ] no TODOs, placeholders or stubs (§9.3)\n- [ ] About/Credits plus the chat reply with Source Information and the three feedback sets (§9.5)\n- [ ] exit-interview.json valid (§9.6)\n- [ ] tests behind one command printing failures plus a one-line count, including the full scripted playthrough (§8)","numLines":371,"startLine":1581,"totalLines":1952}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01XxCB7v8KqYTbTYWjbvGm48","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
