{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxWQutP8PntkkW8yuNo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game now covers nearly all of TASK.md, and the full test run passes (135 checks, 0 failed), but a few items are still short of the spec. The goal condition asks for every feature with no stubs, so it is not fully met yet.\n\n**Deliverables** (in `/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-haiku-5-5-medium.build__20261104-050241__xd92716/work/`)\n- `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050241.html`: the single-file bundle, 1.5 MB, with the hub client first and no external URLs.\n- `exit-interview.json`: parses, with every field filled. Its text predates this session's additions, so its feature claims should be updated.\n- Dev folder `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050241/`: source, tools, tests, `NOTES.md`, and `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` (no engine edits).\n\n**Added this session**\n- Local duel: two drivers on one keyboard, each in half the screen, on the Parallel Run. A ready check starts it, and the higher score wins.\n- Stop Cam: a side view for 2.5 s after each stop, with the error in metres.\n- Postcards: all eight, unlocked by their conditions and shown on a third page of the Driver's Log.\n- S0 lessons: Hoshino's steps advance on their conditions and are spoken once each.\n- Tips: horn, rain and ATS tips play when their events fire.\n- Character rigs: Humanoid portraits for the speakers in briefings.\n- Lever direction setting: \"Up = Power\" or \"Up = Brake\", with keys remapped.\n- Dev Mode tools: auto-drive, God mode, time scale, jumps, finish or fail the stage, time of day, weather, forced signal aspect, trigger an event, camera, braking guide, debug overlay, and a sound board covering melodies, effects and voices.\n- Hub: ghost name tags, a session pause menu without the game's own items, and the title redirecting to the hub boot.\n\n**Still missing or simplified**\n- The trackside camera and the Halo 3000 cab art are not built.\n- Platform crowds are not drawn as figures; only the speaker portraits are rigs.\n- The duel lobby is a ready check, not a menu, and the duel has no camera choice per half.\n- In a hub session the engine's standard Quit row is still in the pause menu. I could not remove it without an engine change.\n- The S0 lessons are short, and the tips cover only three mechanics.\n- Stop timetables and the calibrated stops come from the autopilot, not from human play.\n- The real AI Hub harness was not run. Only the mock contract test was.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:41\n- Source: Densha de Go! (Taito)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: train driving simulation with arcade scoring\n- Perspective: first-person cab (pseudo-3D), with chase and platform cameras and a Halo Map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) with a custom rail renderer; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; vanilla JavaScript; Node and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art Direction: the engine's pixel style with new code-drawn trains, cab, signals, stations and townscape\n- Animation: code-tweened gloved call, gates, needle, notch cells and judgment banners; character rigs in portraits only\n- Audio: all procedural (Web Audio layers, chip effects, 12 melodies, syllable-synthesis voices)\n- Online: AI Hub session, ghost mode, 2 to 4 players (protocol v1); local two-player duel\n- Testing: one headless command plus Playwright browser checks, a mock hub contract, and a scripted playthrough\n- Research: engine handoff docs and AI Hub protocol; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium for the browser checks\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about twenty changed the code, from rail drawing and the autopilot to the timetable and the Halo Time start value.\n- Critical: about six. The rail drew nothing; the autopilot got stuck in emergency braking; Halo Time started at zero; a permanent 25 km/h cap; strips were attached only to their first segment; parked trains left the blocks.\n- Most helpful: the stop-accuracy runs from several starting states.\n- Least helpful: the no-op draw and the screenshots, which catch exceptions and layout but not feel.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed step with `game.advance`, the scene system, the pixel primitives, and Dialog and Menu.\n- Stuck or guessed: the road module needed a fork rather than a drop-in; the settings-group filter was not used; scene data for `game.start` was unclear; the pause-menu Quit row can't be removed from game code.\n- Built myself: the rail renderer, the train model, the signal and stop logic, the autopilot, the Dev Mode screens, and the duel.\n- Hardest: projection and occlusion for long strips; the font lacks button symbols and the middle dot.\n- Changed in the engine: nothing.\n- One change I'd want: a font with common UI symbols, and a documented settings-group filter for dev screens.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the plan's checkable numbers, which made the physics testable.\n- Confusing: the scope (about fifty subsystems) against one session, and the plan and TASK disagree on file layout in places.\n- Hardest: getting an end-to-end game with tests in one session; several features had to be cut and then rebuilt later.\n- Change: a priority order with a minimum to ship, and a time budget per milestone."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2354,"cache_read_input_tokens":744059,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2354},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed