{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CghxyPdFCZKxvDoTZNXNN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The goal is not met, and I'm stopping here rather than claiming it is.\n\n**What's done**\n- The bundle is at `DenshaDeHalo-DenshaDeGo-claude-haiku-5-5-medium-20261104-050241.html` in the work folder. It is rebuilt from the current source.\n- `exit-interview.json` is updated to match the latest work, and it parses.\n- The full test run passed 135 checks with 0 failed, and the real AI Hub harness passed all three scenarios. The last round of changes was checked with the quick suites (76 passed).\n\n**What stops the goal**\nThe TASK.md condition is \"every feature implemented with no stubs.\" These are still partial, and I can't make them complete on my own within this turn:\n- Halo 3000 cab: a simplified panel, not period art.\n- Platform crowds: silhouettes, not rigged figures.\n- Duel lobby: an engine menu, not a full lobby.\n- Tips and S0 lessons: partial coverage.\n- Art and story: partial.\n- Stop Cam: simplified.\n- Timetables: calibrated by the autopilot, not by human play.\n\nFinishing these means real design and art work, roughly a week of work for a person. I need you to decide the scope before I continue. Either accept the current build as the deliverable, or tell me which items to prioritise and I'll work through them.\n\n**Source Information**\n- Date: 2026-11-04 05:02:41\n- Source: Densha de Go! (Taito)\n- Plan: PLAN.md\n- Genre: train driving simulation with arcade scoring\n- Perspective: first-person cab (pseudo-3D), with chase, platform and trackside cameras, and a Halo Map\n- Tech: my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (full engine, handoff package) with a custom rail renderer; AI Hub session client 1.4.0; vanilla JavaScript; Node and Playwright 1.63.0 for tests\n- Art Direction: the engine's pixel style with new code-drawn trains, cabs, signals, stations and townscape\n- Animation: code-tweened gloved call, crossing gates, speed needle, notch cells, judgment banners and the Stop Cam; Humanoid rigs in briefing portraits only\n- Audio: all procedural (Web Audio layers, chip effects, 12 departure melodies, syllable-synthesis voices)\n- Online: AI Hub session, ghost mode, 2 to 4 players (Session Protocol 1); local two-player duel on one keyboard\n- Testing: one headless command (node test/run.mjs, 135 checks), Playwright browser checks, a mock hub contract, a scripted playthrough of every stage, and the real AI Hub harness\n- Research: engine handoff docs and AI Hub protocol; no web research\n- Environment: Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux (WSL2); Chromium for the browser checks\n- Model: Anthropic, claude-haiku-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- Usage: unknown\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n- Issues caught: about twenty changed the code, from rail drawing and the autopilot to the timetable and the Halo Time start value.\n- Critical: about six. The rail drew nothing; the autopilot got stuck in emergency braking; Halo Time started at zero; a permanent 25 km/h cap; strips were attached only to their first segment; parked trains left the blocks.\n- Most helpful: the stop-accuracy runs from several starting states, and the real AI Hub harness, which confirmed the hub contract.\n- Least helpful: the no-op draw and the screenshots, which catch exceptions and layout but not feel.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n- Liked most: the fixed step with `game.advance`, the scene system, the pixel primitives, and Dialog and Menu.\n- Stuck or guessed: the road module needed a fork rather than a drop-in; the settings-group filter was not used; scene data for `game.start` was unclear; the standard Quit row is removed by setting `menu.quit` to false at runtime, which the docs don't state.\n- Built myself: the rail renderer, the train model, the signal and stop logic, the autopilot, the Dev Mode screens, the duel and the hub ghosts.\n- Hardest: projection and occlusion for long strips; the font lacks button symbols and the middle dot.\n- Changed in the engine: nothing.\n- One change I'd want: a font with common UI symbols, and a documented settings-group filter for dev screens.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n- Liked most: the plan's checkable numbers, which made the physics testable.\n- Confusing: the scope (about fifty subsystems) against one session, and the plan and TASK disagree on file layout in places. The strict every-feature condition cannot be met by partial builds.\n- Hardest: getting an end-to-end game with tests in one session. Several features were cut in the first pass and built later, and some are still partial.\n- Change: a priority order with a minimum to ship, a time budget per milestone, and a per-feature acceptance list that counts partial builds as open."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1466,"cache_read_input_tokens":785135,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1466},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ed18f8de-7c3c-4ed5-a440-ff3ec45bd88d","uuid":"f3f3c57f-0621-485e-a680-661a7f0439fe","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:27:46.075Z","request_id":"req_011CghxyPAUJXpfSQwt3FYmn"}
